      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>2 G POI&amp;Yill McDOUGALL'S Pategt SELF-RAISING FLOUR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WUMLitil'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WUMLitil'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The Queen-Sisters at Sandringham.—The Queen and the Empreas-Dowagfor of Russia, after a busy Lima sight- seeing in Loculon,marle only a snort stay at Sindriegham owing to tha return or the King, and the doparture of their Majesties neat wom.c ou thole long promised visit to Denmark end Norway. The Queen-Sisters planted some trees, and were much interested in the proposed alterations to the Sandringham grounds. The Royal Mansion has become somewhat exposed to the public gaze, by the destruction wrought by the gale to the famous avenue of elms and linos, and this has neaessi- tated the extens:ou of the wall, the removal of the Norwich gates, and the making of a new approach to the house. The Royal residence affords a great attrac- tiod for " trippers " from Hunstanton, and the growing popularity of that seaside resort enables the brakes to: do a wariug trade in drives from there to Sandringham and back in the summer time. lioacrutm and f.2.nettisharn which are nearer to Sandringham, are still old-world villages, standing some distance back from the shore, and it is on the beach at." tt4nettsera "as it is locally called, that the Queen's new bungalow stands, over- looking the Wash, and stfulding close to the domain of Sir Edward Green, their Majesties near neighbour at Sandringhani. Mrs. Asquith as Elostess.—Mr. Asqnith, the new Prime Minister is not nearly so wealthy its the late Premier, who has an income of something like £50,000a year. It is nevertheless expected that Mr. and Mrs. Asquith will do a goo I deal of entertaining and Mrs. Asquith is fortunate in possessing considerable wealth which came • o her from her father, the late Sir Charles Tennant who was immensely rich. Toe line house in Cavendish-square, where Mr. and Mrs. Asquith reside, belongs to her, but in due course Mr. and Mrs. Asquith will no doubt more into the Prime Ministers' official residence in Downing-street. It is many years since a leading Society hostess prosidedthete, and the Liberal Party is fortunate in having in Mrs. Asquith one of the most intellectual women in London to take up the duties of her position, imd a lady who has in a singular degree the gift of entertaining. It is commonly sup- posed that nowadays little importance is to be attached to the social side of politics, but according to Mrs. Cornwallis West (Lady Randolph Churchill) and others who write with authority on such matters, this is still tarpons being the case. . . .</p>
        <p>The Date ;:f Easter.—The earliest possible date of Easter is March 2nd, and the latest in April 25th, so that, falling this year on April 19th, it is almost among the latest of the thirty-five possible dates in which the festival may occur. The resaurrection according to early Christian traditions, took place soon after the vernal equinox, March 21st, and after a fall moon, and upon that believe, Easter came to be fixed for the first Sunday after the full erminoxtial moon. It occurred last on the earliest possi We date, March 2nd, in the year 1818, and will not do that again un. ii the year 2285, but it will next fall on the latest possible date, April 25th, in 1913. From a holiday point of view, a late Easter is to hi. preferred seeing that it offers a better prospect of more genial weather than we usually expect in the early spring. A New Walk of Lit,.—The Association of Lady Couriers is nicking it very evident that the lady courier is now an assured success. Daughters of professional men, clergymen, military and naval officers, are pre- ferred by the Association—who undertake to he respon- sible for every lady supplied by therm—moreover they must be well educated, with a wide knowledge o f foreign language, and possess tententive memory for interest- ing historical and other facts connected with that particular portion of the world thich they elect to pilot people. Then they must be thoroughly capable of seeing that all arrangements " run upon oiled wheels," and understand the art of imparting information in a bright chatty way. The fees paid to these lady couriers, are the same as those paid to men, and alto- gether the life would appear an ideal one, for a well edncatod, travellfd, and cheery gentlewoman. Extreme and Cseless Fashions.—For some time past the fashion papers have been of comparatively little use to women of average or small means, the majority of the illustrations and descriptions being quite impossible. For example, there are the curious wind- ing skirts which trail along the ground, and curl about the feet, in such a manner that walking in them would prove disastrous. Then there are the apparently inevitable orerdraperies, which look like shawls, snipped up here and caught rip there, often weighted with heavy cords, and tassels and embroideries. The bodices seem to be built up of multitudinous fabrics and trimmings, and coats and wraps generally are weird-looking creations and quite past the ordinary understanding. No doubt these "new old fashions" are often delightful when carried out by the great modistes, who first of all make a careful study of their customer's figure and bearing—and incidentally her stir- roundings and position t hen work ont for her, styles likely to prove becoming, but those women who have to rely upon less skilled labour, or their own hands are bound to make a failure of the generality of the modes as set forth at the present time in nearly all our fashion</p>
        <p>papers. Hats for Nursery People.—To turn from our all-too- elaborate hats and twines, with trimming,' heaped and massed upon them, to the charmingly simple headgear provided for little girls, is a rest and a relief. For quite small people, the" coolie "shape is leading, in both fine and coarse straws, and when flowers are utilised, only the most simple and natural are chosen. Preference however is decidedly shown for ribbon,rnade up as a rule in the form of high rosettes. The Pierrot Ruffle.— rhe new Pie rot ruffles, carried out in coarse net, and edged with ribbon, or spotted with chenille, are built in the same fashion as were the latest fur necklets, namely, to fasten at the hack of the neck with a huge rPihrin how. Needless to say, for economy's sake, the bow should ha a made-tip fixture, and the fastening accomplished by means of hooks and eyes. The Annual Turn-Out.—Ths spring earthquate " which is. or has been, occupying the attention of every housewife, makes 119 long for the simple mode of life, and at no other period of the year do we so much realise that superfluous possession moans superfluous work. It might be said in ninety-nine cases out of a hundred, ' that an enlarged dustbin is the first essential requisite for the spring campaign. It is the habit of storing away old clothing, old pictures, and hangings, discarded furniture, and orninymts, out of data books, and magazines, and so on, that adds so muoh to the work of the annual turnout, and it is usually the careful house- wife who has miserly instinct the most strongly deve- loped. She it is who is most loth to part with things</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>INHOESTION</head>
        <p>INHOESTION</p>
        <p>SLOW STARETION.</p>
        <p>Food is to the human body what fu d is to a furnace. Without the aid of food the body starves and dies, just as furnace fire die., grows cold, when not supplied with fuel. Undigested food is simply decaying food; so long as it lies in the stomach it is fermentingand.giving off noxious gases and acid fluids that poison the blood and flow with it all through the system.</p>
        <p>Yon cannot be healthy in such a condition. You must surely lack the snap, the energy of mind and muscle, of brain and body, which are necessary to secure success in this age of competition, of strenuous effort in every walk of life. You must " keep up with the procession " or else fill by the wayside and be lost sight of in the burly burly for success. When your food fails to supply nourishment through rich red blood, you are being starved in muscle and nerve—starved as truly as the man who has nothing to eat—only yours is slow starvation. Mother Seigel's Syrup, the great remedy for indi- gestion, has had 40 years of unvarying success all over the world. By aiding the organs of digestion to per- form their work naturally, it has given health and. comfort to millions. We have thousands of letters attesting such cures. Here is one from—</p>
        <p>Mr. W. A. Nicholls, of 22, Walpole-road, Clifton Hill, New Cross, London, S.F.. Writing January 11th, 190 S, Mr. Nicholls says : " For a long time I was a martyr to indigestion. sod fear,' that it would be a permanent condition with me; but I was quite wrong in that opinion. A course of Mother Seigel's Syrup entirely freed me of my trouble, and now I am brighter and better in every way."</p>
        <p>Mother Seipers Syrup is now also prepared in Tablet form, and sold under the name of Mother sewers Syrup Tablets Price 2'9 per bottle—</p>
        <p>one size only.</p>
        <p>that "might some day be useful," but which with rare exceptionsouerely serve to lumber up the place and form repositories for diet and microb,.s. Et may require a most heroic resolution, but it is far better to clear the house of all stmh acuumulationi, and thus lighten the labours when the next domgstic upheaval is due. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>£10.030 to ba Spent in Extensions. The 180th quarterly meeting of the Warrington Co-operative Society was held at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Monday evening, the President (Mr. 'l'. Wadsworth) in the chair. Amongst those on the platform were :—Messrs. W, Bibby, J. W. Bouchier, N. Cook, D. Donnelion, IL Farrar, G. Rushton, W. Atkin- son (general manager), and J. Jarman (secretary). Mr. VEACOCK moved that the whole of the Com- mittee's minutes be read. The SECRETARY said the Committee's business was private, and it would not he fair for the public to know what money they spent. It was most inadvisable. The CHAIRMAN said that if anyone wanted to see the minutes they could go to the °dices to see them. The SECRETARY read rule 3(i, which stated that the management of the Society should be left in the hands of the president and twelve members. COMMITTEE'S REPORT. .</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read the Committee's report which stated that the sales bad again constituted a record, having reached the total of £47,316 12s. 2ld.,an increase of £4,171 14s. 101 d. over the corresponding quarter of last year. The nett profits, after meeting all expenses, were £7,650 ss. 7L.d.,and they recommended a dit idend of 3s. 2d. in the £, the balance forward. During the quarter 314 new members bad joined, whilst 28 have lapsed or withdrawn, leaving the present membership 8,056. They were pleased to report a sabstantial increase in their boot sales, also at the central drapery; Winwick- street, especially the furnishing departruent, was not doing as well as could be wished. Doubtless when the department was removed to the Central, with greater facilities, they might look for an increased trade. The coal department and pork departments were doing well, and the new branch grocery in Marsh-street had been a success since its opening. Steps were being taken to convert property in Bewsey-road into another branch, which they hoped would shortly be opened. Arrange- ments were made for paving the yard at Wi nwick-street and several cottages had been purchte,ed in Brewed- street for further extensiona. They had gone very carefully into the question of the extension of the Central premises. They asked the members to approve of the main plant (which was submitted) leaving the Com- mittee to settle the details as to the various shops and departments as will be best for the Society's business. The Committee, after careful consideration, decided to alter the early closing day from Tuesday to Thursday, tbe latter being the general half-holiday in the town. This will take effect on and after May 7th. The following recommendations of the Committee were agreed to. (a) -That they approve the action of the committee in investing £lO each in flebden Bridge Fustian and Kettering Boot Societies. (b) That £1 ls. be subscribed annually to the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission (Liverpool). (c) That they approve the action of the committee in seibscribing is. to the Shipwrecked Fisher- men's and Nlariners' Aid Society. (d) That they subscribe £1 annually to the St. John Ambulance Association (Warrington). Mr.PEAcocK said that the central drapery department was working later on Saturday night and yet the wages of the staff had been reduced.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tnow said thetas a workingman he thought that the wages of the people in the town ought to be raised. It was impossible for a married man to live in Warring- ton and pay all the charges on a small wage. Their employees had to have a good scholastic education and they had to serve an apprenticeship us well as other things. lie thought it. was the feeling of the members that their employees should be paid living wages. They should be paid at least 30a. a week. He meant married and not single men. (Laughter.) The CIIAIRMAN raid it was the committees's degire to pay all a reasonable wage. They meant to pay their shopmen as much, if not more, than was paid to other shop men in the town. The amount of wages paid to shopinen in the town was astounding. Et was surprising how they lived. Mr. LEEcn : How maoy cottages have been purchased in Erwood-street ? The SECRETARY : Fourteen.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet was then adopted. THE EXTENSION 0? ThE PREMISES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that the committee considered the extension or the central premises absolutely neces- sary, They had appointed several committees including a finance committee, a shop, and building committee. In connection with the shops committee Mr. Bibby was Chairman and he had a report to present l'to the mertibers. The cost of the alterations had been estimated at .£lO,OOO. He thought they would all agree I, that when they did make their alterations they should make them so that they would be of credit to the Society. (Applause.) Mr. W. BIBBY said for the first time in the history of the society the members had been consulted with regard to proposals of an extensive character for the benefit of the society. The committee had confidence in the members that they would do their best in the interests of the society. The committee, who were most of them Warrington men, took very great interest in beauty in the town. The committee, therefore, wished to do their utmost as far as beautifying the town was concerned. He took great interest in the scheme as he was a Warrington man, and chairman of the committee which had the s'cheme in hand. The present premises were altogether out of date, and for many years the committee had considered that the accommodation was not nnythin, like what they believed a society of the importance of , theirs should have. The present building was anti-' coated in ventilation, heating, and other various matters. It was proposed to remove the stone pillars and the brick-work around the Sankey-street building and replace them by a modern building with ornamental steel pillars and plate glass. The whole of the bakery establishment had been removed from gankey-street and taken to Winwick-street, so that now they had greater facilities for enlargement of the back of the premises, and they could load and unload without interfering at all with the thoroughfare. The committee proposed for education purposes to increme the present facilities. The interior of the buildings Nr ould be entirely altered,and customers would be able to go from one department to another without the slightest incon- venience, by the removal of the staircase, &amp;O. The business to he carried on would be grocery, provi- sion, pork, meat (probably), confectionery, bread, floor, boots (new and repairs), drapery, millinery, clothing, tailoring, outfitting, dresses, mantles, ladies' under- clothing, furniture, &amp;c., and the business would be carried on during the alterations. The heating through- out would be by the low pressure system and radiators, and the interior would he furnished in oak, mahogany, walnut ard teak. The exterior would be of pressed brick and sandstone. The work would probably commence in May. Mr. Bibby also mentioned that the society would extend their present bakery and stables in Winwick-street, and some propertyln *Erwood-street had been acquired for that purpose. THE NEW OFFICES.</p>
        <p>The new offices of the society, the architect of which has been Mr. Albert Warburton, were opened this week. They are very spacious and very convenient, and will greatly facilitste the work of the office staff. In answer to a question, NTr. Blum' said the architect for the new scheme was Ar. Albert Warhurton.The £lO,OOO did not include the fittings.</p>
        <p>Mr. LATHAM thought the t irue was now opportune to erect a restaurant on the new premises. Mr. Tnow : How are yon going to get the money ? Mr. BIBBY : The money is alright. A resolution was passed that the purposed extension of the premises he adopted. . _ . . .</p>
        <p>The ;hole of thp Conrimittee's roport wns adopted. THE REDUCTION OF THE STAFF..</p>
        <p>Mr. LEECH asked why a member of the staff had been dismissed ?</p>
        <p>.... The CfIAIIIMAN said the Committee of 'Afanagement had heard the whole of the case in reference to the dismissal.</p>
        <p>Mr. LEECH : Is it a fact that the dismissed man has hen given no refvrenee ?.</p>
        <p>The'SECRM CA RY : No it isnot a nict. Mr. DUNBOBRIN : What have I been dismissed for, I have here an excellent referenne. The CHA ELM Will:__ von sit down?</p>
        <p>THR PRIN LING. • Mr. IV. tirt.f. asked who had printed the balance sheet. Had a firm who psid Trade Onion, rates (lows the work ? They were reported to he progressing, and they meant to go on doing so. lie moved that tbe work be given to a trido union firm. Mr. OWEN seconded.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY Naid when he took the bllance s4heet to a tootle linden Urn hn as told thAt he could not have it at the time he wanted it.</p>
        <p>A-M-P:3II3ER : Wait for it.</p>
        <p>;I;he Siicrtnrsttr said they could not wait for their work.</p>
        <p>Mr. RUSHTON said that a resolution was passed at a committee meetin; to the effect that no printing should go to a firm who did not pay trade union rates. A MEMBER : Give it to the C.W.S. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Examiner,Saturday, April 18, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner,Saturday, April 18, 1908.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS—FASHION—HOME. • LATEST IN HAT TRIMMING. • Easter is the time for the best display of hats to be seen throughbut the year. Every sem- blance of the cold season is thrown off and sum- mer becomes visibly near. Feathers and wings, and velvet and dulness of all sorts disappear from all except the travelling and general utility hat. Flowers reign 'supreme, and the rose re- mains. as ever, the queen of queens. And on this question of the übiquitous rose-trimmed hat, writes Clare North in 31. A. P.,- jet me give readers a word of warning: let the flowers be as near Nature as possible. Freaks or all kinds abound at the moment—blue, purple, green. and brown roses shake ugalufzt one auother on enor- mous erections; but the best mithners are- al- ready beginning to discard them. And the very latest Parisian note is to ‘have your hat look as if its trimming, had just oeen plucked from the garden—niagniticent full-blown roses in every shade of pink, 'red, and creamy Yellows, with the petals large and well defined: the half-operted freak-coloured rose belongs to last summer's hat. Mixed with there roses are all sorts of light. spray-like flowers, white heather being a particular favourite. But roses cre,not alone in the bid for popularity primroses. cowslips, tulips, and iris all have their. place, and that an important. one, on the Easter hat. A SMART SHIRT BLOT:SE. •</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a .remarkably natty and workmanlike blouse ,of the smartest and most up-to-date shirt type. The -original of this neat little garment was carricd out in p:ain ivory clalaine, and really looked quite nice and dainty enough for wear. with a pretty afterroon. costume. Over ,the shoulders came a plain. stitched yoke, which was cut to slope slightly from the neck to the shoulder. From the middle of this yoke came a wide box-pleat adorned on either sida • with small buttons, Which was</p>
        <p>'N7N</p>
        <p>stitched down for some littro distance and then released, though carefully pressed to keep its folds. On either side of this came four short and narrow tucks, then a wide tuck, a space, three more narrow tucks, and, finally, another wide tuck. who sleeves were of the simplest bishop type, and were finished at the wrist by small stitched and tucked cuffs. With this shirt is worn a soft lawn collar and small satin stock. A FRENCHWOMAN ON ENGLISHWOMEN.</p>
        <p>The Comtesse de Boigne was no unkintly ob- server of English manners, and, as daughter of the French Ambassador in London after the Restoration, she had ample means of studying our social customs. Yet nothing astonished this clever and sprightly lady, writes Ella Hepworth Dixon in the Skctch, more than the forlorn case of the dowager in English society. Our absence of family ties and our lack of affection struck her as it strikes all foreigners who visit this island. The system which consigns a Peeress to a small dower-house and a meagre income the instant her husband dies, while her son, nephew, or dis- tant cousin reigns in glory in her stead, seemed to Madame de Boigne heartless. Then the lack of any close sympathy, any community of inte- rests between married children and their mothers seemed equally curious. "Once the daughter is married," she writes, "she and her mother are strangers, and the mother is only formally 'invited to dinner a week in advance." The dowager, according to Madame de Boigne, now joins the army of elderly ladles who form the majority of guests at our social festivities, for they, poor souls, have literally no other oc• cupation or resource. "The manners of the country," declares Madame de Boigne, "leave them no alternative than. society or solitude, ex- treme dissipation or neglect." It is curious how this French criticism holds good to-day, and ac- counts for the elderly birds of strange plumage, the often pathetic wildfowl, to be seen at our routs and dinners, our operas and fetes.</p>
