      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WONDERFT:L FRENCH DRESS.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFT:L FRENCH DRESS.</p>
        <p>_ All Paris is talking of a wonderful gown worn by Mlle. Lanthelme at the Comedic Francaise, says a Globe fashion wrier. It is in Empire style, of thick white satin, brocaded with velvet flowers in delicate Pompadour tints. The skitt, is quite plain; and falls in thick, rich folds to the ground, on which it lies for a few inches. The .high, but not too high. waist, is outlined by a straight band, about four inches wide, of heavy silver embroidery, consisting of silver roses on silver tissue; and from the back two bretelles of this cross the shoulders, but do` not meet it in front, being caught up by silver roses. The em- broidery is laced with silver at either side, and above it appears a circular yoke, cut away a couple of inches below the base of the neck, of beautiful old !see. The sleeves are of the satin, it loose folds held up on the inner side. 10-DAY'S nEtipE. MVP'S A LA PRINCEBSE.—Sprrad some fried squaret Of ,bread„witlt smoked haddock pounded with fresh butter, and seasoned with pepper and salt, and on each of these lay a ileatly trimmed poached egg; garnish these eggs alternately with minced parsley., yolk of hard-boiled egg rubbed throl/1711 aIPvA. nail raralline pepper. and r,erve with the following sauce round. stir I together, till they thicken, three tablespoonfuls of crew, one yolk of egg, one ounce of butter, and a little cayenne; then tarninT it, add a fay drope of lemon juice and a pinch of finely- minced parsley, and servo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>CROCODILE</p>
        <p>WOMAN.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>By WINIFRED GRAHAM,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Star-Child," "The Beautiful Mrs. Leach," "When the Birds begin to • Sing," "A Strange Solution," "On the Down Grade," Ste.</p>
        <p>Men and women were hurryintt to and fro, Intent upon their own affairs. No one noticed a little old lady who strayed timidly from the Temple into Fleet-street.</p>
        <p>She stood hesitatingly upon the pavement, shooting furtive glances from right to left, and catching her breath nervously. A quaint figure she made, in her dress of 41111 green, with an elaborate pattern of crocodiles embroidered round the skirt hem, and decorating the bodice; half-covered by a lace fichu. Her grey hair, parted on the forehead, fell in ringlets to her ears. She wore a thick green- veil, over a bonnet of a past century. "Cab, ma'am ? "</p>
        <p>_ . The old lady looked up:and caught the eye of t. bans oin-cab driver.</p>
        <p>She nodded, the horse drew *up, and, with alacrity surprising for her years, the old-world dame sprang in. "Where to ?" The question seemed to puzzle her. She kept the driver waiting for hiq answer, till he shouted the words vigorously, making some jest to a passer-by on the subject of ear-trumpets. The lady's voice reached him faintly through the roof:</p>
        <p>d'll " leCa y-ctrcus Away sped the sprightly grey horse, while this strange figure of a woman leant back, pale, gasping, against the friendly cushions. The crowded thoroughfare- became a jumbled mass of moving vehicles and hurrying pedestrians; she could see nothing clearly; for the world was in a haze. Like one in a dream, she stared throngh the spotted net of her heavy veil with bloodzdiot eyes. Her face grew whiter, her lips more colourless.</p>
        <p>"My heart," she kept murmuring faintly. "Oh, my poor heart !" She drew up her loose sleeve and examined her vhite arm, scarred by the marks of angry fingers. Then a deep, quivering sigh escaped her—the sigh of a fluttering soul near the gate of eternity, the sigh of one sad and broken. "Piccadilly-circus, ma'am !" shouted a hoaree voice. "Piccadilly-circus! Lor', the old woman's asleep! Hi, ma'am, wake up! It's Piccadilly--- "The lady is ill," said a voice on the pavement. The cabman looked down, and perceived that the speaker, a gentleman, was on the step of the hansom-cab.</p>
        <p>"Who are you?" came front the box. "Leave the party alone, can't yer ? Look 'ere, I'm going to get down. The old 'un's 'ard of 'earing ; she's takin' a nap,.l reckon." With these words the man on the box alighted ; the gentleman was already holding the lady's limp hand, his fingers were on her pulse. "I am a doctor," he said, gravely. "She is dead."</p>
        <p>Instinctively the face of the rough driver changed; he drew back with a muttered: "Well, I never!"</p>
        <p>A crowd had gathered round, police were on the spot; the doctor consented to accompany bn inspector to Vine-street Police-station, whete the body was to be conveyed. "Dead!" muttered a voice in the crowd. "Only to think of it—and her dressed up such: a guy, too Poor soul, it must 'ave been sudden 1"</p>
        <p>So the crowd ssed on. and the little Olti lady was borne away, in her gaudy robe, unconscious for evermore of slighting tongues and thoughtless jeers. . .</p>
        <p>But death, when it overtakes frail humanity in unexpected moments, may prove an unfolder of secrets, and a giver of surprises. A very great surprise lay in wait for the strangers into whom: hands the unknown body fell. The grey curls were but a covering for auburn hair of the silkiest texture, the wrinkles had been painted upon the marble skin, and, beneath her grotesque divguise, death's victim proved a young lovely woman, delicate as a flower, beautiful as a white moss rove. Under the mask lay as fair a picture as eye could behold ) In wonder the searchers gazed, and shivered slightly, their nerves touched by mystery's cold fingers, which drew a cloudy veil over the unclaimed dead.</p>
        <p>No address could be found, but in her Forlot typed letter, without beginning or ending. It ran, in short, jerky sentences: "He returned from America last night. Fact ! I know it. Old rooms. Don't waste a moment. Go 'at, once. Fear the worst."</p>
        <p>The flimsy sheet, of paper was crumpled as if by nervous fingers, which had crushed it in a trembling palm. No other writiti,g gave a clue to her identity the police shook their heads, and the doctor said she had died of heart disease. • Thus he left her—a fair though mournful figure— in the gloom of the mortuary. Her dead face followed him for the rest of the day. He found himself thinking, thinking, and every now and again the words of the letter rang out in his brain: 'Old rooms. Don't waste a moment. Go at once. Fear the worst!" And the worst had come, the worst or the best, for the silent sleeper. A weak heart, succumbing after some terrible shock, the Marks of fingers on the limp arm, which had been gripped in a frenzy of passion. Had she loved ? Had she hated ? Only the angels knew!</p>
        <p>So the nameless wanderer lay, surrounded by whitewashed walls, without a prayer, , without flower, without a tear</p>
        <p>Ile was distinctly good-looking, and a man of character: about thirty, clean-shaven, with clear-cut features and a decidedly clever mouth. His eyes were a mixture of blue and slate, a few premature grey hairs protruded themselves on the well-brushed head. He wore the expression of one conscious of some haunting care, or deep anxiety. His pallor might have been caused by illness, worry, or the light. He strolled, with a preoccupied air, into the Carlton Hotel, Pall Mall, and asked for Miss Mina, Vandyck. He was sliewn to her sitting -.Too m. An extremely handsome woman, matching him in age and height, rose with a smile. She wore a dazzling gown of silk muslin wrought with glittering crystal, in a design of Stuart roses, with long, icicle-like fringes in crystal and silver. The room was full of roses, and she carried a delicious perfume in the folds of her lace fan.</p>
        <p>_ Cyril Warrender gazed at her a moment without speaking. "What a woman !" he thought. "Fit to be a duchess!"</p>
        <p>She returned his look fully, her eyes glowed. , "I know what you are thinking, Cyril," she cried. He shook his head. No, you don't." "Yott are thinking,"" she continued, taking no notice and speaking with a decided American accent, "that I'm a deal too good for you, and wondering I don't want a title. Guess I could take one, if I cared to look round. But, there What is the good of having Money if you can't marry for love ? I love you because I believe you to be a first-class fellow, because I admire your intellect. I like your writings, I like your conver- , nation, I like your cheek, insisting upon my engaging myself to you. Say, now, do you remember that look I gave , you when you first ' proposed ? It would have withered up the average man, and just frozen the life out of him! I meant you to turn awfully blue ; instead you never even turned a hair. You just kissed me. That's the kind of man I like. But, see here. I'm a bit s:ck about your not coining this afternoon. I waited till past four o'clock. I called you lots of pretty names! Our first day in England, too! Ma could hardly believe her eyes when she saw me starting off to drive alone. Ma hasn't got over the journey yet, and is in bed." "If4nie," he said, "you know what business is. On your side of the water it's a pretty big factor in the race of life. I had to grant an interview t.e. an important person who forced an entry into my chambers. To-morrow I must shew you the dear old Temple. I want you to admirn my snuggery. It is full of ghosts mind histories." "All right. But about this evening—where are we going " "To the first night of `The Crocodile Woman.' A friend of mine has written the piece. It's a roaring farce, tie tells me., The play takes its name from one of the minor parts. The Howardens have stalls next to ours, and they want us to sup With them afterwards. I've a letter for you .from Lady Howarden ; formal invitation, I suutiose. end noniarnhniontions. She is already babbling about a wedding present, and hinting that she wants to lend us Castle Wentworth for our honeymoon."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>"Don't poke fun at the poor old soul," said "Rush!" said Menie, waving the woman aside ; Monk, decidedly; "she's a real nice woman, and I and, entering on tiptoe, she softly closed the door like her. True, the first time we met, she called behind her. ' you a regular scamp, who shut his brains in the There on the hearthrug. with only .the firelight ink-pot, and occasionally upset it—just to keep a playing on his silky curls, lay a sleeping child, name before the public." with a face so seraphic that Menlo thought of Cyril smiled. He was thinking witit pride of Raphael, and clouds with cupids peeping over Menie's wealth and beauty—his own attractions them. The red glow of the embers hid his pallor, seemed to him so small in comparison with hers— yet the whole scene expressed loneliness. and as he gazed at his fair fiancée, the vision of a She crept close to him and knelt down; the grotesque face, livid with passion, lips that formed perfume of her presence gave to the room a flower- bitter threats, and eyes flashing fire, floated before laden atmosphere. his eyes. The child stirred, and only half-opened his eyes. No wonder, with such a picture in his brain, he A smile broke ujom the small red lips. felt like a skeleton at the feast, as he dined with "Oh, moth. r, I thought you were never Mtinie, and reached the theatre, the flaring lights coming!" he murmured. seeming to scorch his eyeballs. Menie stroked the ' soft gold hair. "I am not As they crossed the pavement, a wild-looking your mother, darling. Wake up, and look at me. man, with long hair and low collar, darted out of You will see lam quite a stranger." the darkness, laying a detaining hand on Cyril's He obeyed, the sudden flush of pleasure fading, arm, "Warrender," he said, it is going to be a but no trace of shyness manifesting itself in the frost! For goodness' sake, come into the manager's large, wistful blue eyes. room this side of the house. I must speak to "I see now; you are prettier, and your dress is you!" • so grand. Mother is very pretty, too, but she "But I have a lady with me." hasn't roses and things. Mrs. Potter says mother "The lady will come, too," said the American will never come back; she has gone to Heaven— beauty, casting a sympathetic eye upon the perhaps you live there yourself ? " distracted author. That a writer of farce should No wonder he thought her an angel, this dazzling wear long hair, upset her preconceived theories, vision in the glittering robe. Together they followed him through the Menie was a creature of good impulses, and the vestibule, and up a side staircase to a private forlorn condition of the child she now fully apartment. believed to be Cyril's touched her deeply. Anger ' 'Nice little room—cosy t said Miss V andyck. with him kindled in the heart that yet warmed The author wiped his brow with an enormous towards his offspring. She remembered "coals of handkerchief. fire." Were they not the sharpest, most torturing . .</p>
        <p>"Tell us the trouble," said Cyril. revenge? At the thought, she Jgathered the tired "Trouble I Why, Warrencler, it's a disaster! child into her arms. You've read the piece. You know that quaint "No, dear, neither you nor I can go to Heaven little part of the Crocodile Woman? She opens yet. But I will take you away from this dreary the play! This afternoon we had a dress rehearsal, house, to a beautiful, bright place. You don't during which Miss Rowland was brought a letter by want to stay here with Mrs. Potter ?" special messenger. Directly she had run through He shook his curls and clung to Milk,. her part, she read it. A man who was watching "Oh, no, not if mother can't come back. I'd her said she seemed quite queer, as if she were rather go with you." going daft, then, without waiting to change her So it hap,pened that that night, while the mirth- things, she bolted into a hansom. Now the provoking 'farce was in full swing, while Cyril curtain is waiting to go up—but no Miss Rowland! Warrender paced his chambers in gloomy unrest, We have sent to her rooms, but she has not been and the silent sleeper lay in the whitewashed room home. The understudy is here, but Miss Rowland of death, a little orphan boy fared sumptuously in has got the clothes! She must have gone mad; to a private apartment at the Carlton Hotel, and was rush off like that! We are communicating with soothed to sleep by a beautiful woman, whose eyes the police." were full of unshed tears. Cyril bit his thin lips and stroked his chin. IV. "Awkward!" he said. "What sort of a woman was Miss Rowland " "I can hardly incle it out at all," declared Mrs. "Pretty, little, sad-looking thing, first-rate at *Vandyck the following morning to her confidential character parts." maid. "Miss Mane gave ine such a confused The door burst open, and someone—hot, excited, account of what happened last night. The sudden and panting gasped out a piece of hurried death of a woman, a destitute child—Menie, the Inf