        <p>The thee wo'rds came shortly. She watched acquaintance of Miss Rowland, an actress. She had him feeling his way down the long line of smartly- been living abroad, and only lately come :to dresed men and women. England. I found her real name was Warrender ; "There is something queer happening," said and when she shewed me her wedding lines, and I Menie" Vandyck. ."I guess there's more in this saw her faithess hustand's Christian name as well, than meets the eye!" I just said: 'This will suit Tom I' " i . I " 111. And what did Tom do?" asked Miss , 1 Vandyck, coldly, Her tone was enough to freeze Meuie felt uneasy. She was quite certain anyone:but the self-assured Miss Hendick. her present state of mind would prevent 4ter 1 ."Wea, my Tom ups and declares she ought to enjoying the play, and determined upon a speedy claim her husband, but Miss Rowland was pig- plan of action. For several moments she sat headed on the point. They had quarrelled, she contemplating the.situation, half-surprised at the said, and parted willingly at Texas, where the one wild ideas flitting through her brain, short year of their married life was spent. It had She felt convinced that in some way Cyril was not been a happy year, and he knew nothing of the connected with the mysterious Miss Rowland. , son-that was to bear his name. Like a brave soul,</p>
        <p>"Lady Howarden," she whispered, while the she determined to suffer alone, sooner. than slay first act was till in progress, "I am so worried with a man who had so quickly ceased to love her. about Cyril, I am going to slip out and see if by It was not until she heard of his engagement to any chance he is in the lobbi. He lool(ed you that the blood boiled within her. Tom lee awfully ill ; and Fie got just a splitting headache her know at the theatre when Mr. Warrender way myself out of sympathy. If I don't feel a deal ; back ; and I waited about on the stairs to hear what better, I'll drive home right away, and beg you to ' passed. Such a scene! I couldn't help being excuse me. No, no, don't move, please. I would attracted to the keyhole. he'd gOt her by the rather be alone; and it won't disturb these folks wrist, and *tot her in a frenzy, and she • was around so much if I only take myself off." • weaing her 'acting clothes! `So this is your So Lady Howarden and her party were deserted revenge!' he cried, and even Tom can't think by the ice-maiden, and a second figure beat a hasty what was meant by revenge. I am afraid—as 1 retreat from the same row of stalls. have heard nothing from her this morning—he No sign of Cyril ;he had' evidently gone home. I extracted some promice. So I took Tom's advice, A burning desire seized Millie to know more of the and ventured to call and tell you the facts. But dead woman, whose fate had moved him so for Torn and -me, you might have married a strangely, and drawn him away, pale, agitated, bigamist; and I thought, Miss Vandyck, you trembling. , being so rich, you might possibly be pleased to do Shq wrote quickly on the back of her progfamme : something for Tom, out of gratitude." Miss "Would you kindly give me Miss Rowland's Hendick wriggled and leered as she spoke in a address ?"; and sent the message by hand to the most objectionable manner. manager. I "You do not appear to bare read the morning</p>
        <p>The programme was returned, with the address papers, or you would have seen that Miss scribbled beneath the question: "7, Tredegar-road, Rowland, the actress, died yesterday. I am Bloomsbury." . • expecting Mr. Warrender here every minute; Withont• a moment's hesitation, Menie, with the perhaps you would like me to introduce him to true American spirit of independence, sent for a your amateur detective, that We may consider the hired Vehicle, gave the address to the driver, and financial claim together." found herself whirling away in the direction of Miss Hendick gathered up her umbrella, a small Tredegar-read. plush bag, and the silk gloves she had withdrawn . "Perhaps it. was a mad ac' ion," she told herself, from her fingers. "possibly an unjust one to Cyril." But curiosity: She glanced round nervously. "Oh, lot', no; cried with a loud voice to be satisfied, and Menie for mercy's sake, let me • get but before he comes I responded with the courage of a resolute nature. l'd be afeared for Tom's life!" As she alighted at a cheap lodging-house in her I As she spoke, she caught the sound of Cyril magnificent opera cloak, she did not realise the Warrender's voice in the passage, talking to Mrs. effect her presence would have on the grimy land- Vandyek; it seemed to paralyse her or a moment, lady who opened the door, then, quick as a flash of lightning, she darted Truly, Menlo Vandyck might have been a spirit through the door and ,made for the staircase like from another world, by the expression of wonder a rabffit seeking its hole. and blank amazement on Mrs. Potter's sallow ' But for the dead weight of misery on Menie's countenance. soul, she must have labeled. , • "Is this' where Miss Rowland lives ?" ca,i from "Just the woman to spy!" she said. "Not an the doorstep. The cab wait,d by the pavement, atom of pluck at the back of her. Heavens ! How "Well, lady—yes, lady!" gasped the confused I wish I did not love him !" owner of the lodgin(.7s ; "that is to say, she did live ' Never till now did Mettle realise the hold this here—till this afternoon. But there! It's been love had taken on her life. With all his sins such an upset to me, I 'ardly know 'ow to tell you. crowning him with ignominy, he was still her My man says I shall never see a penny of the rent Cyril, her own, and the hands that could :have now, for she's gone and died sudden. Oh, yes ' snatched him from her were limp and helpless. she's dead right enough, for Mr. Potter went with m She thought of the dead woman, the little child in the police to the mortuary. Died in them acting 'the next room. Surely there must be some explana- clothes, too—the—the—let me lee, what did she tion ; he could not have meant to deceive her. call herself ?" She sank on a sofa, breathing hard. The</p>
        <p>"Crocodile Woman ?" sugqested Miss Vandyck, furniture danced before her eyes. 'Presently she in tremulous accents. , _ heard him come in. She pretended to be readily, _ . . .</p>
        <p>• "Yes, your ladyship. Her real name was Mrs. a book. The touch of his hand on her shoulder Warrender. but she limited as Miss Rowland, for it roused her. The touch could still thrill ! How seems her husband deserted her while she was still handsome he was! in her teens. Poor dear! And it seemed a hard "Yon—you—don't look well, Mbnie," he said, struggle, for- she was never strong. But, there! bending over her. she took wonderful care of the boy, and brought "You were ill last night," she retorted. him up a thorough little' acioleman. Can't think "Yes, I saw a ghost •it was the ghost of my what is to become of the poor little brat now ! He dead past. Some day I will tell you—before we won't, go to bed, because she never coins in to are 'married, '3le'nie. I always meant to toll you so per. I've told 'im 'e won't see 'er any more, something before that! The'story will be a little awl he jm:t sits and cries somethink hawful !" different now. Bat don't let .us talk of "The child!" yesterday ; it was is terrible day." M6itie started 'back, and put her hand to her "Cyril," said Male, slowly, "you thought she heart. She was as pale now as the imystals was dead before. Ah ! you must have thought it; glistening on her roses. The veil of romance was you could not have been so wicked as to desert her, slipping away like the haze of early morning, to deceive me! That woman you married, who hiding an ugly structure. She could almost have died last night. I am willing to listen, to be wished she had not come, convinced; but, remember, I shall know if you are "You are worried about your rent," she said, telling me the truth or not. Yes, yes, I heard; with an etTort drawing herself together. "Don't can't you see I'm snffering ? I never expected to let that trouble you ; send the account to me, I find a good man, but I wilted n straight one. will See it is paid. And, if you don't mind, Don't ask me nuestions • merely accent, thet'fact that should like to speak to the boy. He won't be I know everything, and tell me why you did it: friffhtened, will he ?" engaged yourself to me, with a wife living? Mrs. rorter tea the way to us dingy room at as Think what it meant—the crime-- far end of the long, narrow passage. Her blazing eyes met his; she looked magnificent She pushed the door ajar and peered in; in her beauty, her despair! "I kept a bit of fire going for him," eh. He returned her kindling glance with equal fire, whispered, "but he wouldn't eat no supper." And held his head 1.5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>"She did ft for revenge—a Woman's trick, and cruel one. Poor little thing, she had suffered so bitterly, and I forgot! I handled her harshly, I spoke brutal words, I was a wretch! Think whatsit meant to me, 'Menie, loving you as I do, to su-denly see the woman I had married, the woman I believed dead, before me in the flesh? Years ago she had fascinated me with her delicate child's beauty. When we met she had been jilted by a man who, at the last moment, preferred her sister. It happened in Texas. Out of pique she en- courand my attentions. It was not for some months after our marriage that she confessed she never loved me. The old lover had soured and embittered her life; she suggested we should part —for a year at least—when we were to meet again and discuss the future. The date and the meeting axed for the following year, we separated gladly. I left her in her native city, -where she had friends and relatives. Two months later I read of an awful storm; the city in which I supposed her to be still living was swept away—ontirely wiped out of existence by a furious West Indian hurricane. Four thousand buildings were destroyed, and as. many lives lost. I travelled at once to the scene of the disaster, lint could find no trace of her. The. toy n was completely destroyed. Do you wonder that when the day for our meeting came and she did not appear, 1 told myself that 1 was free? Yesterday a voice from the grave said: 'Cyril, I am here t' • Eyes from the grave locked in raine, a hollow laugh of mockery smote my ear. It was- then she told me that pride had kept her out of my path. She never had meant to return to Vie man who failed to win her love; but she left Text 4 for England only a week before the awful storm. Life was a worthless, sunless thing to her at least, so she grudged me my happiness. She came to separate us. She provoked me to madness. I believe we both were mad; yet as she left, I realised how short a time she would keep me from you. Death was written on her face. I took fresh hope; I met you as if nothing had happened. I was quite sure she would die. I offered her money, but she refused. I meant to send it anonymously. But when I learnt the Enemy had been so quick with her, I forgot my wrongs, I turned sick and faint, with a sense of horro.:. I felt like a murderer !"</p>
        <p>"No, Cyril ; not that, not that! It was' the judgment. She deceived you, poor little thing, fpor little woman I" Menie's head fell upon the cushions, tie tears coursed down her cheeks. "Don't cry," said a soft, small voice. . . . . .</p>
        <p>The curtains from the adjoining room had been pushed aside, and a slight, childish figure, with curling gold hair, strayed to Menie's side. He had Cyril's eyes and clear-cut features; for the first time seeing them together, Male noticed the striking likeness. Cyril stared in uonder at the new-comer, whose. gentle little band stroked Meni.e's arm. "Who is this?" he asked, attracted by the singular grace and charm of her tiny comforter. "A little boy I have adopted," she said, forcing back her tears and playing with the child's fascinating hair. "I found him last ',night, Cyril, wasn't it strange? I brought him home with me. Hs loves me already, tile dear! Did you know there were sneh treasures unclaimed in, London? Such gold!" The morning sun streaming through the Window made a halo round the boy's head. _</p>
        <p>Cyril stared at him with the feeling •f one who gropes in the dark, lie was conscious of a. mystery, he sought the solution, and Male read his perplexity. "Give this gentleman your hand," she said to the boy. "Now hold mine in the other. Stand between us—so. 'You must call him `Pspa,' and some day later on, perhaps, you will call me 'mother.' " "Menie, what does this mean? I don't understand--"</p>
        <p>"If I marry a widower," she whispered, "surely I have a right to his son ?" Then Cyril gazed speechlessly at the child, and gradually the truth dawned. "I fetched him away last night from—from her lodgings," murmured 'He was asleep on the door alone by the tire. Tie had been crying pitifully. When I woke him he thought I was an angel. Ile asked me if 1 lived in Ileaven. ' I felt inclined to say 'No, America, but it's the genie thing! "Angel," whispered Cyril, "Angel, indeed, indeed!"</p>
        <p>"I guess I am pretty well pleasing myself," retorted Menie, with a frank smile. "I've just a knack of going my own way, and riding over obstacles. I thought last night Fate meant playing me a nasty trick, but I've weathered the storm; snd this little bit of flotsam won't make inueli odds. Now, if there's any more wreckage on the beach, you had better tell me straight out, %chile I'm on the clear up." Cyril caught her to his heart and kissed her passionately. "Strange," be said, "that you should have dis- Nwered the child I had never even seen ! You must ne a witch, darling." "Nearer that than an angel," replied Menie. 'You see, we American women don't pause." And, with this assurance, she returned his millrace.</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY PERILS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S HOLIDAY PERILS.</p>
        <p>Daily mishaps make Zam-Buk a daily need, especially during the holidays. Freed from school the youngsters often indulge their games and pleasures beyond the ken of parental control, and not infre- quently return home maimed or injured. The anxiety of mothers for the safety of their offspring can undoubtedly be reduced to a minimum if they keep a box of Zaru-Buk in the house. This unique healing balm is the most valuable "first-aid " remedy it is possible to possess. It is prepared from certain herbal essences of the rarest medicinal value and contains none of the rancid animal fats or mineral poison that make cheap ointments so risky and useless.</p>
        <p>Soothing in application, Zam-Buk not only casts out all impurities frova the scat of injury thus pre- venting dangerous complications, hut speedily heals the wound and restores the skin to a clean, sound, soft and perfectly healthy condition. In cases of cuts, abrasions, burns, scalds, and other flesh wounds, Zarn-Buk should be at once applied and then all fei.r of festering and blood-poisoning will be removed. In the case of bruises, resulting from falls or blows, instant relief is imparted by applying Zam-Buk over and round the injured spot. Zam-Buk is also the infallible reedy for ring- worm, eczema, running sores, and other scalp troubles. infectious diseases to which children are particularly liable at holidsy times when they play together. Every home this Easter should have its stock of Zam-Buk replenished.</p>
      </div>
