      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>RATES.</head>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>DC EASE EASE A R OF Bd. SANCTIONED. ~ , „,„‘-'n.Ttlesday evening the adjourned meeting of the "'trington Town Council for the consideration of the tatimates for the ensuing year was held at the T 0913 114 a il• It will be remembered that last week the estimates involving an increwe in the rates of 101. in the £ were referred baok to the various committees With a view of reduction. On Tuesday, following a General Purposes Committee meeting, it was reported that none of the committees had been able to make any anbstantial reductions, but that a grant of £2,000 would ,a made by the Electricity Department, which enabled loe Council to reduce the proposed increase in the rates rum 10d. to Bd., making the rates for the ensuing year 'ls. ed. , The Mayor presided and there were also present : Aldermen A. Bennett, T. Burton. John Evans, W. J. torshaw, D. Tinnion ; Councillors T. H. Ashcroft, J. 'all, T. Barton, J.. Broadhurst, T. Cheater, E. Cunnington, J. Dickenson, James Evans, J. Hawthorn, .'?..... Lord, J. J. Nield. J.Pernberton,W. Rogers, J. Sankey, ..tt• Singleton,J. Skelland.T. S. Steel,F. Stott, 0. Toole, ''• Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson ; Messrs. J. L. Whittle (Town Clerk), A. T. Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk), J. Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), J. Deas (water Engineer), C. Madeley (Librarian), F. V. L. Mathias (Electrical Engineer),J. M. Murray (Director of Educe- d°n), F. G. Ruddoek (Borough Analyst), mad Dr. J. C. liibbert (Medical Officer). Alderman BENNETT in moan* the resolutions fixing 111, 0 Borough Rate said the only difference from the resolu- :Pn he moved at the last meeting was an alteration in rne figures front the sum of £39,035 16s. 10d. to £38,535 'us. 10d. The difference was due to a clerical error in 4....11 arrangement between the Council and the Education 1-tommittee. He was glad to find that as the result of the careful examinations which had been made since the ;est meeting of the Council the original estimates had been justified, and that it had been decided that none of them could he 'educed with any real wisdom or satis- faction to the Council. The Council had consequently decided not to make any reduction. The total amount that it would he possible to reduce the borough rate by ,l'as £772 which was considerably less than Id. in the £. 9e need not go through the detailr, but if he did he . thought he would he able to show the Council that none of the reductions were really justified on any soundbasis. , Councillor PEMBERTON : Question. ' Alderman BENNETT : That is my opinion and it is the opinion of the Council and it would be scarrping , ,ui'ork and neglecting to perform duties it is our bounden duty to perfom, to that the borough rate will atand if the resolution I move is carried, at the old tlgure subject to the alteration I have named of £2OO. Councillor I Wthwissos seconded.M Councillor PEBERTON said the Town Clerk had ttiled that they could not now call upon the Education •,,oruruittee to alter their estimate. That was FIOWT, to aim, but it had struck him since they had formed them- selves into a Council that they could request the committee to allow.them £5OO from the £l,OOO. It was l'ltlY a reserve fund which was being built up. They had £5,000 in hand, and the committee only wanted ' another £l,OOO. It only meant taking off £5OO this ,year and in that way they could knock a penny off the obrough rate. I.,COuneillor BARTON said that Councillor Pemberton, tlke himself, thought the/ would have the opportunity nat night of saying whether they were in favour or „ '-:„..°t• He underbtood the Coencil could veto the spsnd- 10f4 of the education department. The TowN CLERK' said Mr. Jolley explained that the atimates had ta.2n approved, hut the Education Corn- ilittee re-considered them out of courtesy to the Town s-ollticil. e eoUncillOr WILKINSON Snid the £l,OOO referred to by ouncillor Pemberton was the Education Committee's Zeetribution to a working balance of £6,000, which, he ,blieved, they were obliged to build tip in six years. . his was the last year the contribution had to be paid, 'llO that next year they would not have to find what was tVal to a penny rate. Some of the reductions which a c.ommittee: proposed to make were silly. ii Alderman BENNETT, replying to the discussion, said 11' fallY appreciated Councillor Pemberton's desire for Pv rates, which they all shared. But Councillor IPTherton had surprised him. The one predominant izelleitile he bed stood for was sound business manage- Li but hi a action that night was not in accordance h what he so often told them. He (Alderman 1 eenett)was doubly surprised at what he said about the if on estimates. It would have been extreme folly eel h 5 Education ComMittee ceased to put the money neeuee before they bed arrived at the uorking balance £l,osat.Y. Next year they would not ba"e to pay the the L ",,, and they would ft:el the bent-fit of it at a time I.Nvouid probably want the money. The ed-ication had crept up year by year, and they would be glad 3,,,thelr sould say "thus far and no further." V.,PROVEMENT RATE REDUCED BY 2u. GRANT OF £2,000 FROM ELECTRICITY _Alde DEPARTMENT. f‘,l* rtean BENNETT then moved "That the estimate an . Pita Improvement rate, as recommended by the tte amid Estate Committee be confirmed, and that</p>
        <p>p ,T.</p>
        <p>; I</p>
        <p>il</p>
        <p>P</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>the Finance and Estate Committee do make an improve- ment rate accordingly at 2s. -10 d. in the pound, to be collected by two half-yearly instabirents, and be authorized to affix the Common Seal of the Corporation thereto." Alderman Bennett said the original proposi- tion was for an improvement rate of 3s. in the £. The committees which came under this rate had carefully gone through their estimates, and had decided not to make any reductions. The way the estimates I had been prepared reflected great credit upon Mr. Fairhurst and Ai r. Woodcock, for they found the only possible reductions that could be made were,£772 on the borough rate and £762 on the improved rate. To make 2d. in the £ they needed £2,000, . and moreover the reductions were not con- sidered desirable or necessary. Happily, or unhappily, there came unexpectedly, as though out of the clouds, a benefaction of £2,000 from the Electricity Committee. It was thought that it was not possible for them to touch the profits of the electricity department until they had created a reserve which amounted to one- tenth of the total capital expenditure, hut the Town Clerk advised them that was not so. They had decided to accept the £2,000, and it was welcome to all of them. The real remedy said the Alderman, lay in the committees keeping within their estimates. (Hear, bear.) They did not want, for the sake of immediate economy and imme- diate concession to the indignant ratepayer, to find themselves 12 months hence in a worse position than they were in now. That might be avoided if they took a manly and straightforward course that night. They were not consenting under public clamour to cut their estimates down, and to reduce their rates at the sacrifice of a sound financial position. (Hear, hear.) If they kept within the estimates, and if their re-productive undertakings continued to flourish, as he hoped they might, he trusted that next year they would be able to make a substantial reduction and have no fear of an increase which alarmed the Council on the present occasion. (Hoar, hear.) He hoped that those who had criticized their action that night would give them credit for having a sound financial administration, and admit ths.t they were brave enough to face the facts. (Hear, hear). Councillor PEMBERTON seconded and said that with regard to the electricity department be considered the reserve fund was mtignificent, considering the short time they had been receiving profits. They ought', he contended, to give further concessions to the public, and begin to use the profits-which were made in the way it was intended they should be used. What was municipal enterprise meant for but better public service and reduction of rates ? Be did not believe it was sound finance to accumulate reserve ; they ought to have a progressive profit. Councillor STOTT observed that instead of tapping the Electricity and Gas Departments as they were doing the Council ought to begin to tap landlords like Lord Newton and Lord Lilford who were reaping the benefits of better streets, better lighting, and the intro- duction of tramways into the town. These improve- ' ments were putting wealth into the pockets of the men who owued the land in their suburbs, and the Council ought, therefore, to take advantage of every opportunity which presented itself of trying to influence Parliament to give something back which was due to those who were thus spending money year after year. Alderman BENNETT replied to tire discussion, and thought it would be a good thing if they could establish a reserve for the equarization of rates so as to avoid those violent fluctuations. That, however, was not allowed, and it was one of the anomalies municipalities bad to put up with. With regard to future sources of revenue his views were much the same as Councillor Stott's. Be had the utmost faith in the grant mrni- ' cipalities of this count' y. Those people who were try- ing to draw ftrbitary lines were simply beating the ' lad. There would be new sources of revenue and new , opportunities of making great improvements as time went on, and as a profound believer in muniCipal pro- gress and numicipal trading in its best sense, he hoped to lice to a: 3 them. The resolution was then adopted. • GENERAL SbWELIS RATE. Alderman BENNETT formally moved "That the estimates of the Se,wers Bate its recommended by the Finance end Estate Committee he confirmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make a General : Sewers Rate accordingly at twopence in the pound, to be collected by two half-yearly instalments, and be ' 'authorised to affix the common seal of the Corporation thereto." Councillor PEMBERTON seconded and the resolution was adopted, THE LATE ALDERMAN JOHN ROBERTS. The MAYOR said he bad been invited to unveil a memorial tablet to Alderman John Roberts in St. ' Peter's Chord, on Sunday, May nrd, and he invited the ' members of the Council to accompnny him. , ALDERMAN FORSHAW AND ALDERMAN 130LTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CORRECTION.</head>
        <p>A CORRECTION.</p>
        <p>Aldermen FORSHAW said that at lest Tuesday's meet- ing he made a statement that Alderman Bolton's expenses on the deputations amounted to £3OO. He found that that was a misstatement, and if he made a misstatement he was always ready to do the amende honorable. (Hear, hear.) He overstated the amount by more than £5O, and he regretted it. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April lB, 1908. WARRINGTON EDUCATION PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April lB, 1908. WARRINGTON EDUCATION PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Franklin had always spoken her mind, and she intended to do it so long as the gift ol speech was spared her. Her children and grand- children knew her habit, and found it not alwart cheering. "pa like to have you tell me what in- duced Edith to fall in love with that. young man I saw last night for the first time," said the old lady to one of her daughters. "1 think she wn attracted to him at first because be's such athletic fellow and such a splendid swimmer, the mother of Edith ventured, feebly, after moment's casting about in.her- mind for a satis- factory answer. "Humph !" snorted Mrs. Franklin. "Which does she prcpose to keep him in after she marries him—a gymnasium oz an aquarium?"</p>
        <p>A thief broke into it large mansioh early in the horning and found himself in the music room. Hearing footsteps -approaching, he hid behind screen. From seven .to eight o'clock the: eldest daughter had a lesson on the piano. If: um eight to nine o'clock the second daughter took a sing. ins lesson. From nine to ten o'clock the eldest ion had a violin lesson. From ten to eleven o'clock the other son took a lesson on the flute. At eleven o'clock all the brothers and sisters- as- sembled and studied an ear splitting piece MI the piano, violin, flute, and voice. The thinl staggered out from behind the screen at hull. past eleven and, falling at their feet, cried, "Vol mercy's sake have me arreked, but stop :" -( "The brute," erxelairned the bride of a year. "Have a cup of tea, dear," said her fondest friend, "and tell me all about it. What has he been doing new?"" You know I told you he has been encouraging me in learning to cool: ; has praised my nice little entrees, takes we Le the theatre as'a.n occasional reward, and air .that." "so good ground for a separation in ti:at, imagine," said the dearest friend, with sl.ght areßsm. "Your sympathy is worse than youl tea," retorted the bride, who was obviously out of sorts. "But I am going to tell you, anyway. Not long ago he promised me a surprise if I would -turn out a nice dinner, cooked all by my. self, from soup to coffee. Last night I did so. Everything pleased hint. My pet,' he re. marked, I believe I promised-you a surprise on aa occasion like this." Yes, darling, I an. swered. 'Oh, do tell me what it is? ' I shall discharge the cook at the end of the month,' he . added.'</p>
        <p>Tramp: "Please, mum, I'm a financier, and —" Housekeeper: "Huh! You look like it." Tramp: "You surprise me, mum. I'd hardly have suspected that a lady in this ot4of-the-way place would be so familiar with Wall-street as to know a man on the wrong side of the market when she sees him."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Suburb: "I don't see what's the matter with our hens. They don't lay at all." Visiting American: "You don't feed 'em right,, mum. Just you give 'on about two shillings' worth of corn every week, and they'll lay you a,shialing's worth of eggs every seven days.'"</p>
        <p>Wife: "If it is so necessary that we should economise, why don't you shave yourself, instead of paying a barber to do it?" husband: "That shows all you women know about profit and loss accounts. Why, a little piece of court plaster, no bigger than the end of my chin, costs tea cents:"</p>
        <p>Returned Traveller: "What has become of Catchem and Cheathem, the rich lawlers't Retired, I presume?" Resident: "Yes, retired. They are both jri the poorhouse." "Phew! What happened?" "They had a quarrel, and sued each other."</p>
        <p>Friend: "I don't understand why you and vont husbaud should have separated so -span." Mrs. Aftermath: "It was all owing to the. baby's temper." "Mercy on us! How could that be?" "We couldn't agree as to which one of us the baby took after."</p>
        <p>First Crook : "Titres are mighty hard just now." Second Crook: "Deed they are. I sandbagged six men las' .night, an' only got fifteen cents out o' the hull lot." .„</p>
        <p>Solid Citizen : "Enough, sir ; I will hear no more. I have no time to waste talking with an avowed Socialist." Reformer: "But yon have i not heard all our plans. One of them sto have the Government erect sound-proof asylums, tc which all babies shall be committed until they are old enough to go to school." Old Citizen : ,!'Whoop! I'm with you."</p>
        <p>Citimnn : "Doesn't- the noise and bustle of the elty confuse you?" Suburb "Not a bit. All my- neighbours have lawn mowers, babies and chickens."• ,</p>
        <p>Upton: "There goes Miss Deßielt on her bicycle. The man in livery following on another bicycle is, of course, a servant." 7Downtown: very well, so far as it goes. But a servant can't prevent tires from puncturing or machines from breaking." Upton: "Oh, the wagizon with the blacksmith shop is coming close‘bohi-nd.." Housekeeper: "Are,you sure this bakirg pow. der has the most raising nOwer?" Dealer: "Raising power? . Well, madam; about ten years stgo balter in this town was so near ruin that lir had only one barrel of flour left. Ire began using this baking powder, and to-day he's a rich man, and that barrel of flour isn't quite gont vet."</p>
        <p>"4h," said the fair widow. "you have been in some pretty tight squeezes, haven't you, colonel?" " Yes," answered the old warrior, putting his arm around her waist, "and rni not the only one." And he immediately proved the truth of his assertion.</p>
        <p>He: "I've forgotten what was•the hmgest day in the year." She: "T. am not sure of the (late, either,. but I remember it was that day in Junc when you called both in the morning and after. noon!"</p>
        <p>"He Married moneyr didn't, he?" "No, he thought he was marrying money, but he merely married something that talks." Teacher: "Metre means measure. Thus, s gas meter measures gas. Can anyone tell me what the metre in poetry measures?" Johnny: "Hot air."</p>
        <p>"Josh Billings" wrote:— Mi natral idea iz that no man nor woman lz ev much importanse to the world out ov their sphere; altho a taylor may make an exquisit suit ov clothes, he isn't the rite man to lead the fashuns. A broken harte iz the saddest ov all things, but. thanks to kind Heaven, it iz one ov the rarest. I never have seen a person who had one that I thought could not be mended. Yung man, if ye look to the world for yore re- ward, ye will be insolvent before ye git half way thru life. First get the approval of yure own eon- Kinetic°, and the world will certainly, in due time, giv ye theirs. Center shots are what tells—one bullet in the bull's-eye iz worth 10 that just miss it. The best ov us owe more to chance than we are willing to admit. I have butlittle konildence in mi own judgment. and I haven't mach more in other people's. Ekunemy will beat all the good Ink and kuuning in this world, and will give even kapasity a hard lite for the belt. I There iz nothing so cheap az opinyune; every- :body haz sum to dispose or. They ain't worth muteli ennyhow ; if they whz, folks wouldn't be se anxious to give them away. Most ov the lies that are told are told from habit, rather than a wallah:is desighn. Yu are not certain or enny man's reptitashun or , estate until he iz in the hands or the undertaker. I Silence iz allwuss safe, and iz frequently the smartest thing we ken say. I It iz the fear or the law, more than the 'uv ev it, that keeps the best ov us out or mischief. It iz no disgrace to be bit bi a dog once, but the seekond time it iz. . I think we reach more konklushuns bi guessin than We do bi reazoning. The most penitent and humble people in the world are thoze who hay simply outlived their sins and transgeshuns. iattery often may be innocent, but it iz never but one remove from decepshum •</p>
        <p>• - ; Mother: "What's that smacking noise in the parlour ?" Studious Boy (who goes to Hchool ) : " sister and her young man exchanging miN•obes." •-•</p>
        <p>"..ii,;;4: "The doctor says an ocean voyage will be a good tonic for me. Is an ocean voyage btlfflu • latiog ?" Beenoff : " Stimulating! Before you : • are out ot sight of land you will feel as if von had drunk a whole demijohn of liquor the day before." Daughter: "The paper says thaLedible snad are advertised in some of the restaurants." Old Lady: "Land sakes ! Who'd ever a' thought that ediblegnails were fit to eat." " '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>QUESTION OF THE RATES.</head>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF THE RATES.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday even- ing under the presidency of Councillor Fulton-Smith. There were also present :—The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Alderman Bennett, Counoillors Wilkinson. Sankey. Barton, Toole. Pemberton, Dickenson, Nield, Revs. James Davies and John Yonge, Miss Williams, Messrs. H. Roberts, A. J. Jolley, H. Eingdon, C. S. Burgess, R. T. Fairclough, E. Plinston, J. Upson, T. P. Bradshaw, C. H. Unsvrorth, G. Walker, J. Moore Murray (secretary and director), R. B. Roberts (assistant director), and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RATES.</head>
        <p>THE RATES.</p>
        <p>11 In view of the desire expressed at the last meeting of the Town Council for a revision of- the estimate of the expenditure for the year 1908-9 by the various corn- Imittres, with the object of any possible reductions 'therein being effected, the Finance Committee recon- sidered their estimates, but, with the exception cf the reduction of the sum or £3OO in respect of the medical inspection of school children to £3OO, they were unable to recommend any alteration being made. It was accordingly resolved, that the estimates of the expendi- ture of the committee for the ensuing year, which were approved by the Education Committee on the 17th February, 1908 be amended by the substitution of the sum of £3OO for the £5OO in respect of the medical inspection of school children. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said he understood there had been some talk amongst some members of the Council and others about the possibility or desirability of removing from the estimates the sum of £l,OOO which was put down for the building up of a working balance. The Finance Commit tee after careful consideration—several members of the Council being present—were absolutely unanimous in their recommendation that no change in that direction should be made. They were under an obliga- tion to raise a working balance and the Local Govern- ment Board sanctioned the raising of that sum being spread over a term of six years. This was the last year in which the payment of the equivalent value of a penny rate would be made. It was anticipated that after this year the working balance which was absolutely necessary for the proper carrying on of the work of the committee would be adequate for the needs of the authority in respect to education. Aldermen BENNETT seconded, and the minutes were, approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SCBOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCBOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the minutes of this Com- mittee, which were mainly of a routine character, Mr. UPSON pointed out that the grant received from the Board of Education for the Pupil Teachers' Classes (£406) showed a considerable decrease, but that for the Secondary School (£698 155.) was less, owing for one thing to a re-organisation of the methods of 'Dement, and not to any loss of efficiency in the schoefl. The payment of £22 10s. in respect of the Beamont cookery centre was a new grant, and £136 ss. was received for the Technical Institute cookery centre. The Committee had had a conference with the governors of the Grammar School, but any action with regard to the new regulations of the Board of Education was post- poned uhtil the next meeting of the Committee, when the matter would be thoroughly discussed, and an agreement would no doubt be arrived at. At the tueeting of this Committee the CHAIRMAN mentioned the question of the provision of a country school during the summer months for children of poorer parents, and, some discussion having taken place, the principle was approved; and it was understood that further details of the proposal would be brought for- ward later. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded and the minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HALF-TIME SYSTEM.</head>
        <p>THE HALF-TIME SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee considered the Education (School Attendance) Bill (House of Lords) and the Elementary Education (School Attendance) Bill now before Parliament. A communication on the question bad been received from the Association of Education Committees. The Director ' submitted the following statement on the Bills The former has been introduced into the House of Lords by Lord Stanley and the latter into the House of Commons by Mr. Walter Rea. Both Bills are identical, and their object is to make 13 the minimum agent which a child can obtain any exemption from the obligation to attend school. Under the existing Education Acts it is necessary that every Bye-law regulating the attend- ance of children at, school shall contain a provision granting partial exemption to every child over the age of 12 to whom the Bye-la es are applicable. Such a 1 Bye-law exists in the Bye-laws for the County ' Borough of Warrington,- but as a result of the employers refusing to employ half-time labour, it has become a dead letter. The 'object of the Bills is to make compulsory in other parts what -has been voluntary in Warrington, end, so far as the Factory Acts are concerned, the result will be practically to abolish half-time attendance at school. Both BUIS have received a considerable amount of support from all parties, and the Association of Edncation Com- mittees desires each Education Committee to lend all the support they can in this matter by making repre- sentation in favour of the Bills and requesting the Members of Parliament for their respective districts to use their influence in the same direction. It was decided to ask Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., to support the Bills during their passage through Parliament. MEDICAL INSPECTION OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the following committee was appointed to deal with the medical inspection of school children :—Rev. James Davies Miss Williams, Councillors Evans, Dickenson and Wilkinson, Messrs. C. H. Unsworth, A. Jolley, E. Plinston, C. S. Burgess, C. E. Parker, J. Upson. The committee will carry out so much of section 13 of the Education (Administrative Provisions) Act, 11)07 with reference to the medical inspection of, school children as falls under the control of the Education Committee. The -minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"A DRINKING DEN."</head>
        <p>"A DRINKING DEN."</p>
        <p>Woman Who WouNI Not Be Reformed</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Saturday, before Mr. J. W. Hayward and other justices, Mary Harris, whose husband was sent to gaol on Thursday for six months for neglect of children,was charged on, warrant, with having neglected her three children. Mr, Browne prosecuted on behalf of the N. s.p.n.c., and said that inasmuch as prisoner had been convicted' many times before, his instructions care to ask that she be sent to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions with a view then of further indicting her under the Habitual Drunkards Act, for the purpose of sending her to a home.</p>
        <p>Inspector Cox gave evidence as to prisoner's drunken habits which resulted in the children being sadly neglected. On April 3rd he found her drunk on the sofa with the baby in her arms, and she kept lifting up her right band and striking the baby's face, making it cry out. The baby was in a dangerous position, end he took itaway. There was a strange man and woman there, and the table and floor were saturated with beer. Tt was nothing but a drinking den. At 8 p.m. the same day he went to the house with P.C. Chrimes, and'found two men and a woman there drunk. On the next day, be visited the house and found prisoner and three more people drunk. Thethree children were very neglected. The beds and bedrooms were in a filthy condition, and there was no doubt the children were being ruined. Be called in Dr.Peacocke to examine both the prisoner and the children. There was no hope for the woman unless something of a drastic nature was done for her. He was quite convinced prisoner was an habitual drunkard and unable, to take care of herself. When soberfshe was a very hard working and industrious woman.</p>
        <p>Dr. Peecocke saiihe was called into the house on April 4th by Inspector Cox, and saw the three children, and examined them. They were very poorly clad and in a neglected condition. He then examined the bedrooms, and they were very filthy. The bedding— what there was of it—was also in a filthy condition. The only food in the house was a loaf of bread. He again visited the hou3e the Fame night and found prisoner there drunk, and from her appearance, he was of the opinion that he was an habitual drunkard. The children had been SU neglected as to cause unnecessary suffering and injury to health. Sergeant Barnett said a warrant against prisoner dated April Bth was handed to him to execute. He executed it that morning at 4.45. She was in the honse,l, Littler- land's-yard, off Knutsford-road. She was under the influence of drink, and he brought her to the Police Station. he had a baby with her and they were both very dirty. He read over the warrant to her and she said " I know all about it."</p>
        <p>Prisoner. when committed for trial to the Llverpool Quarter Seasons which will ho held on April 21st, excitedly exclaimed : I will be worsd when Ido come back. You must not think you are going to reform me with all Tour" slavver." You will never reform me.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WHO WILL BE WHO?</head>
        <p>WHO WILL BE WHO?</p>
        <p>The children who will one day make headway in the world and get the best out of life are those 'hose bodies and brains are now being well</p>
        <p>nourished with energising and flesh-forming foods. Currants contain over two-thirds their weight of nutriment of the exact kind required to sustain and build up the body. To eat Currants daily is to become healthy and vigorous, ft to take active part in the battle of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Liptond</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Ligitons</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
        <p>Liptons</p>
