      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>GAS INDUSTRY.</head>
        <p>THE GAS INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT JUNIOR GAS ASSOCIATION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Visit to Messrs. Richmond's Works.</p>
        <p>On Satnrday afternoon the works of the Richmond Gas Stove an Meter Company Ltd. at Grappenhall and Academy-street, Warrington,. were visited by the Man- chester and District Junior Gas Association—an 'organization which bar 140 members all intimately con- nected with the production of gas in a large number of towns in South Lancashire, Yorkshire and Derbyshire. The great interest which was taken in Satimday's visit was evident front the faht that about 100 members attended, and at the close it was generally agreed that they had spent a most instructive and pleasant after- noon. Oa arriving at Central Station at 1.48 the visitors were met by representatives of the firm and at once drove to the Grappenhall works. Here they were received by the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) and directors of the company and cordially welcomed. There were present, in addition to the Mayor. Alderman Pierpoint (chairman of the Gas Committee), Mr. T. Dwerryhouse (chairman of the Richmond Gas Stove ana Meter Company), Mr. H. M. Thornton (vice-chairman and managing director), Mr. J. A. Ransome (managing director), Mr. J. W. Glover and Mr. J. Coxon (directors), and Mr. J. W. Slack (geonitarY). The M AYOR, having extended a hearty welcome to the members of the Association, referred to the wonder- ful progress which had been made in the gas industry of late years. As far as the relative merits of gas and electricity were concerned, he believed gas had never suffered where electricity had been introduced. (Hear, hear). They seemed to have worked hand in hand together. He hoped that when the members of that association became managers of works they would devise something which would even exceed the good results which were at present being achieved with gas. His Worship also directed the attention of the visitors to the improvements which had taken place in War- rington and particularly to the completion of the widening of Bridges-street,</p>
        <p>Alderman Pierpoint said he could assure the visitori that the Gas and Electricity Committees in Warrington were not in deadly opposition to each other. While the Chairman of the Electriwity Committee had great faith in electricity, be (Alderman Pierpoint) had greater faith in the use of gas. (Hear, bear). Warrington had reason to be proud of the firms which carried on the gas industry in the town, and it was certain that the men with the most original Ideas and the inventive genius would lead. The competition could only result in the public having better gas and appliances for pro- ducing beat by gas. The domestic uses of electricity would no doubt in time be extended but he had faith in gas and in gas cookers, end he believed that in M.'ssrs. Richmond and Mersss.Fletcher,Rnssell and Co.,the,y had two of the highest. representatives of their respective industries in the country. (Applause). With regard to electricity and gas, he said " Let the two systems fight it out and may gas win." (Applause). Mr. DWERRYHOUSE also added a word of welcome and expressed tbe hone that the members would go away with a good impression of the works. He believed the firm had got the best of everything and they had availed themselves of the latest developments in!gas apparatus. It was their intention to connect that works with a larger building to be erected in the front, and it would then be much more convenient to take the goods from the foundry into the departments where they would be finished. _ -</p>
        <p>?i JOHN di-x—oN-Sta—tTn7firm would be very glad of any hints which the members of such an important association might give them. Mr. E. WELLENS. liebden Bridge (President of the Association) cordially acknowledged the welcome and pointed out that although the members of the Associa- tion were juniors they were not absolutely at the bottom of the profession—in fact they were nicely rising to the top. (Applause). Mr. F. THonPE (Mabchester) in moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor, said the Association had already been acknowledged by the journals as a Junior Associa- tion, then by gas engineers and by firms such as Richmond's, and now they had been recognized by the civic head of the municipality. They looked upon that as a red letter day in the history of the Association. Not only did that Association appreciate the great kind- ness of his Worship, but every Junior Association in England and, Scotland would note with a great deal of gratification the fact that the Mayor had received the Manchrlter Association. IVarrington would always stand high in the estimation of gas engineers, and two names which would always be identified with it were those of the late Mr. Richmond, who was recomized as a genius of business, and the late Mr. Fletcher' a genius of experimentalists. They trusted that both firms would prosper and continue to bring out new inventions for the benefit of the gas profession. (Applause.) Mr. H. 111EncER (Widnes) seconded and the vote was heartily accorded. _</p>
        <p>1-fayi.ng partaken of light refreshments, the party adjourned to the front of the building where a photo- graph was taken by Mr. J. R. Birtles.</p>
        <p>. A: complete inspection of the foundry was then made, and proved highly interesting. Itie building, which is replete with the most up-to-date means of dealing with the raw material, covers an area of nearly 10,000 square yards, and provides accommodation for several hundred men. The entire site which the company have-acquired, however, embraces 94 acres, and it is intended in the future to concentrate under one roof the whole of the manufacturing departments of the business. Power is derived from a double plant of large gas engines and the building is lofty, well ventilated and well light?d. The most interesting feature of the visit was the cast- ing of souvenirs, each member of the party having the opportunity of trying his hand at the work. The cam- pany intimated their intention of presenting a couple of souvenirs to each visitor.</p>
        <p>The next stage of the proceedings was to drive to the Academy-street works and here the visitors were able to follow the various processes which go to the com- pletion of the gas stoves, cookers and other articles .for which MeSsrs. Richmond are so widely famed. the most admirable arrangements had been made in order that the maximum of instruction could be gained from the inspection, the party being conducted through every department and having the advantage of a brief explana- tion of each stage of manufacture. The tour of the works being ended. Mr. H. M. TtrortNroN, who had to leave for London, addressed the party and said he felt that the more they could see of the profession from the manufacturing point of view the more they would understand and lmow each other. Not only that, but the more I they understood the goods that the firm supplied the better would they he prepared to faze the consumer.' The firm had for many years advocated that there should be much greater co-operation between the profession and the manufacturer. There had not been anything like the amount of co-operation that there ought to have been .in the past. They all knew • that the commercial side of the gas profession was now as important as the manufacturing side—in fact one would almost go further and say that it was becoming more important. Be that as it, might, they as a firm bad in past, years shor‘o that they were able to sell their goods and the visitors that day would have sren that they "ere also able to manu- facture. He did not think any gas stove company had a better and more no-to-date works on the whole than they had, and he believed the outside selling staff was equal to if noebetter than that of any other gas stove firm in the conntry. In any case, they claimed to be up-to-date and while they would never run down their opponents, they said they were as good and they would try to he better. They were grateful ta the work- people who bad given up their Saturday half holiday in order that the visitors might see the works in full swing. (Applause ) The visitors then drove to the Patten Arms Hotel where the firm entertained them at tea. Mr: J. A. Ransome presided. After tea there was a little speech-making.</p>
        <p>Mr. RANSOM E said he understood from the President that there was an iiniisnally large gathe-ingiof mem- bers that day, and the firm felt it a high honour that so many should have availed themselves of theopportunity of visiting the works. Ile was sure the visitors would agrca that he should express to the workpeople their appreciation of the willingness with which they gave up their afternoon on what was called in the works "holy Saturday," for if they had not been at work the place would have Teen exceedingly dead-alive. It was a very good thing for the buyer to get into touch with the seller and for that reason the visit of the Associa- tion wng heartily welcomed by the firm. It was eight years since the Association last visited the works and : he could assure them that. the firm would be very pleased to see them again. (Applause.) Mr.WELLENS said the members readily aFsent2d to the suggestion that their thanks should he conveyed to the workpeople of the firm. it could not be denied that they had an excellent meeting that day. --,.T0 those who were present on the occasion of the visit of eight years ego, there was one cause of regret—that they had no longer the cheering presence of Mr. Richmond. whose death they so much regretted. The intarest which he took in the , visit and the lecture he delivered were a proof that he had keenly nt heart the interests of the young men of the profession. (Hem., hear.) Tt was only sixteen :ra since fhe late Mr. Richmond commenced the firm and the position it had now attained testified to the energy and erthusiasm which he brought to bear. Warrington had indeed something to thank him for. The works were now finding emplovarient for something : approaching I ,OC) work r lople. Mr. NVellens concluded by expressing the indebtedness of the visitors to the firm and to Mr. Ittimome, Arr. Slack and the staff for the splendid way in which they had been received (Applause). Mr. GrvmAN (Birkenhead) moved a vote of thanks to the company:, at said none of the visitors could have failed to gain a very hanctimme impression of the busi- ness capabilities and enterprise and the great spirit shown by the firm in developing such a splendid site as</p>
      </div>
