        <p>HBUMATICURA.—Revrest,—Beat Remedy for Rheuma- lt Mem. Safe, certain. Grand results. Small box, post free, 11. 3d. ' Send to-lay. —lin ELTMATICUR L CO., 138,700 port.</p>
        <p>Parliament having held its POLITICS formal meeting after the AND THE reconstruction of the COMING MEN. Ministry, the Easter holi-</p>
        <p>days now commence in earnest, except, of course, for those Ministers who have to take the opinion of their constituents on their change of office and on the policy and action of the Government. Mr. WlNsTort CHURCHILL has thrown himself into his contest at Manchester with enormous verve and vigour. He is, without doubt, a "first-class fighting man." His election address is one of the most inspiriting political documents that has been seen for many years past. Mr. CHURCHILL goes down to Manchester not to apologise for or to excuse the Government, as some of his political opponents seem to have expected, but to attack its enemies. He stands by its policy as a whole ; he rejoices in its achieve- ments; he is for holding every inch of the ground it has occupied, and more as occasion offers. In the Cheetham Hill district of Manchester he will be opposed by a curious and more than usually complete combination of interests. We hoar this portion of Manchester spoken of as a working- class constituency,and to some ex tent,undoub tedly it corresponds to the description ; but it is also villadom. It is to Cheetham Hill that the Manchester tradesman retires when he longer finds it necessary to live over the shop. Also, it is the Jewish quarter. It includes both the Ghetto and the region dwelt in by the more prosperous Jews. Then, too, it extends into the typically business portion of Manchester. Alto- gether, it is a constituency not less remarkable than is the young and promising stiitesman who is for a second time soliciting its suffrages.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robrermerf will probably have a less arduous fight at Dewsbury than Mr. CrtuncaiLL at Man- chester, though he, also, will have more than one opponent. It might ba worth while for those who have been striving to identify the Liberal Party with Socialism to take note of the fast that these two members of the Government get no support from the Socialists in the constituencies they have repre- sented. The Socialist support goes by an indirect route to the Conservative party. Anything more grotesque than opposition to Mr. RuNcrmAN and Mr. CHURCHILL in the name of Labour it would not be easy to imagine. These two energetic politicians represent in a special manner the spirit of youth in the Labour party. They belong less to to-day than to the future; and they illustrate in a most interesting manner the astound- ing youth of Mr. ASQUITH'S Administration. As the end of the last century approached we 'were wont to speak not only as if the old order of things, but as if the entire age, would pass away with it, and as if the world and man kind would present some marvellous aspect of</p>
        <p>rejuvenation. Nothing happened when the first of January came round. The earth kept on the same diurnal course. With a few exceptions—not more than the average exceptions of any day of the year—the same men carried on the same work in the same way. In fact, the one century seems to be indissolubly joined on to the other. There was mere continuance ; there Was no cataclysm. It does happen sometimes, however,that sudden and curiously simultaneous changes take place in human affairs. One generation of men seems to move off the stage and another tikes its place. To those whose years have made them expecienced in politics it must seam odd that Mr. ASQUITH Should now be a grey-haired veteran. It does not seem long since he was first entering the Ministry, and we were congratulating him on his early success. But since then a great generation has passed away. Mr. GLADSTJNE, Sir WILLIAM HAncouar, and almost all the most conspicu- ous of their colleagues have vanished for ever. What a changed Front Bench from that, say, of 1892 ! With the removal of Mr. MORLEY and Sir HENRY FOWLER to the House of Lords, Mr. ASQUITH and Mr. HALDANE, Mr. BIRRELL and Mr. WHITELEY, roan at their very best, intellectually and otherwise, become by very much the seniors of the Government Front Bench in the Commons. The bulk of their colleagues represent what we suppose may be called the new forces. Certainly they represent a new time. It is not possible to recall the recasting of a Ministry which has resulted in so considerable a lowering of the average age of Ministers. Those who hold, as is not unreasonable, that the present age belongs to the young men should fiud themselves greatly cheered and inspirited.</p>
        <p>We suppose neither the THE FORGED Tory Party nor the Liquor TICKET TRICK. Party will be anxious to</p>
        <p>adroft any knowledge of the forged ticket trick which failed so dismally in regard to last Friday's demonstration in favour of the Government Licensing Bill. But as both were equally responsible they can both share the dis- credit between them. Had the plot succeeded, and the meeting been broken up in disorder, it would have been hailed as smart tactics by many who are now the first to condemn it. Certainly, to attempt a dirty trick and to fail and be found out, is very humiliating. Some of the organisers of the plan to pack the meeting with half- intoxicated shouters, looked very crestfallen when they reached Parr Hall and found that it was as easy to pass through the eye of a needle as to enter therein without a bona fide ticket. We want it to be noted, however, Viet the alliance between the Tory party and the Liquor Trade has already resulted in a deacent to trickery of the most despicable kind in order to stifle free speech—that precious possession which every party professes to be proud to maintain. The politics of the " pub " are what we may now expect from the other side. Meanwhile, the Licensing Bill grows in the favour of all independent and right-minded men the more unscrupulous and virulent the attack upon it.</p>
        <p>There is not much comfort THE RATES. for the ratepayer as a result of a week's cogitation by the various Corporation Committees in an endeavour to reduce the call upon the - rates. Instead of the demand being 7s. 10± in the t it is to be 7s. 81.—a saving of 2d. on the first estimate but a jump of Bd. since last year. The Committees scraped the last farthing off their estimates, bat the sum total of the saving did not equal a penny in the pound on either the Borough rate or the Improvement rate, so that it was no use attempt- ing a reduction at the cost of so much inconveni- ence. The painting of the baths was to be postponed, fire brigade practices were to be lessened,legal expenses were to be cut down and various other economies effected which were not economies in the true sense of the word and the Council was wise in refusing to sanction them. The only real relief came from the Electricity Committee, which played the part of the fairy godmother by foregoing £:2,000 profits ".which should have been put to the reserve fund. This item is entirely responsible for the saving of 2d. in the call upon the ratepayers, some of whom will perhaps look more kindly upon that expensive luxury, electricity. As it stands, the position is sufficiently serious and not a little alarming. A year hence we cannot expect £3,000 from the Ship Canal Company in costs nor a further £2,000 from the Electricity Committee, so that unless the tightest possible hand is kept upon the ensuing twelve months' expenditure there will be another increase in the rates to face next April.</p>
        <p>The appointment of a THE UNION successor to Mr. J. C. CLERKSJIIP. Satton as Clerk to the</p>
        <p>Warrington Union has been such a fruitful source of rumour and innuendo as to suggest at once that the Board of Guardians has not managed the matter either skilfully or discreetly. On reading the report of the Board meeting on Wednesday at which the vacancy was filled it is impossible to avoid the conclusion that the Board is not best adapted for dealing with a delicate situation. The appointment of a new Clerk was complicated at the outset by the fact that one of the ablest members of the Board—Mr. A. J. Jolley—entered the field as a candidate for the office. Mr. Jolley's fitness on the grounds of experience and knowledge of Poor Law work has been doubted by no one,but there were other candi- dates with admirable qualifications and one of them, ' Mr. A. Bottomley, of Salford Union, has received the post. Bad management in the first place, and the indiscretion of Mr. Jolley's friends subsequently have been at the bottom of all the friction over this appointment. The advertisement was injudi- ciously drafted at the outset, but the Board in committee rectified one blunder by fixing an age limit before opening any applications. Thia deci- sion has the effect of cutting out Mr. Jolley, with the result that his friends on the B iard adopted unconstitutional methods to get him re-instated. Even the Chairman departed from the bounds of strict impartiality by issuing a circular in Mr. Jolley's favour, and on Wednesday all sorts of motions were launched fn order to make the Board stultify itself and bring Mr. Jolley I' in at the last moment. It was altogether an unpalatable exhibition of partizanship, and such as ' may lead Mr. Jolley to exclaim " Save me from my friends," when he made the report of the meet- ing. It was only by a majority of one that the Board saved the last shred of any reputation for ' consistency which it may yet possess.</p>
        <p>Brewers, with a portion of AN EXCELLEKT the community at any rate, EsAAiPLz. are a little out of favour</p>
        <p>just now, and they are apt to be much criticised and perhaps misunderstood persons. But we doubt whether anyone will be found to cavil at the action of Messrs. Bass in regard to the Territorial Army. Least of all should the Government fall out with them. The firm has given notice that it will afford all possible facilities to such of its employees as desire to join the new Force to fulfil the con- ditions necessary to efficiency. Not only will no disability be placed on those who are members of the Force, but other conditions being equal preference will be given to men who are serving. In addition to that, the company promises to make a gift of 2. 6d. to married men and to men who are supporting a household, in order to raise the pay of Bs. to the same rate as that formerly allowed to Volunteers attending camp. Here is an example which might well be followed by other prosperous and influential firms. Undoubtedly, one point which may tell against recruiting for Mr. Haldane's new Force is the fear which men may have that they will be called upon to undertake obligations which may cause employers of labour to prefer employees who are not so tied. We do not believe that the fear</p>
