      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>is in any way justified, or even that it is general. But the situation would be considerably improved if powerful firms employing large staffs were to adopt the course taken in this instance. Mr. Haldane stated several times during the debates ou his scheme that its success must to a largo extent depend upon the co-operation of employers of labour,and the recognition by them of the national service which is undertaken by those who enlist in the new Citiz3n Army. It should be unnecessary now to impress upon b03i0e55 men the nature of that service or the degree of security which the successof the scheme must give. Surely it is net too touch to ho.a3 that others will fallow in the line of Messrs. 8d99, and thus make a substantial contribution to the solution of the problem of national defence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Conservative Ward meeting at Silver-stree schools on Tuesday evening was a tame affair. The audi- ence at its best was not large and never enthusiastic,and as speaker succeeded speaker many took the opportunity of making a dash for the exits,tired of listening to the usual denunciations of "robbery and confiscation." The electors of Warrington are refusing to be gulled by hollow arguments advanced against the Licensing Bill and are daily becoming more clearly convinced of the absolute justice and necessity of the measure. * * * There were no less than fourteen publicans present at the crowded over-flow meeting in Bold-street Wesleyan School. Most of them came to scoff but they went away thinking deeply and with a strong inclination to bless. The absolute sincerity of men like the Bishop and the Rey. Paul Bull, and the persuasiveness of Mr. Charles Roberts, M.P., had a marked effect on those who had been avowing them- selves against the Bill. They could not help seeing the difference between men who were sacrificing much for conscience, and their own leaders who sacrificed nothing but claimed what did not belong to them. Truth will tell !</p>
        <p>* * * It was curious how the enemies of the Licensing Bill defeated their own ends by the issuing of forged tickets for the Parr Hall demonstration last Friday. The trick was soon discovered and checkmated so successfully that none of the forgers obtained admission. •They and they alone were responsible for the meeting being made into a ticket meeting, and if they had had the decency to play the game they would have found ready entry to Parr Hall after the reserved coats had been taken up. instead they had to stand outside in the cold, with nothing better to do than "boo " at their betters.</p>
        <p>Quite the most striking feature of Monday evening's meeting at Sankey Bridges in support of the Licensing Bill INAS the outspoken speech of Mr. C. E. Parker on the danger of the dominance of "the trade" in public life. He did not hesitate to condemn the efforts which "the trade " has been making in Warrington to capture the Town Council and clearly showed what a menace it is to good government that representatives of the traffic should gain a controlling influence on local authorities. It is impossible to forget the declaration that " our trade is our politics" and Mr. Parker is doing a public service in calling the atten- tion of the townspeople to this—the most serious aspect of municipal life at the present time. * * The following is a strange news item from the " Dispatch :—" The Manchester brewers, who have conducted a vigorous campaign against the Licensing Bill, have decided to suspend their agitation during the election, and in order that the election may not be considered as unduly influenced by the Trade have instructed their tenants to withdraw the Robbery Bill ' placards outside their houses during the contest." This can only mean that the brewers have found out that too much of " our trade our politics " is likely to injure the chances of Mr. Jovnson Hicks, and help the Government candidate. No-one would dream of charging them with chivalrous intentions ! *s* We are glad to see that the brewers have at last changed the bills onPocal public-house boards. Their monotonous reiteration of " six miles walk for a glass of beer" had got distinctly tiresome, even to the workingman who was supposed to but knew better than believe it. However, one lie to, merely taken the place of another. The boards are now decorated with cartoons, the principal one of which pictures the big glass of beer and the little glass of beer, with the statement that the latter will be in store for the workingman under the new Licensing Bill. If he does get only half a pint for a pint he must blame the brewer. The funniest feature of this brilliant piece of picture politics is that the big glass has a fine " top ' on, while the little glass evidently contains a very flat fluid. Up to date, there- fore, the brewers threaten their dear friend the work- ingman with dearer beer, half the quantity, and stale stuff at that. They had better be careful or they will make him a teetotaller.</p>
        <p>* * A very interesting incident has followed the appointment of Dr. Macnanaara as Secretary of the Admiralty. The first telegram of congratulation which the hon. member received was from the sergeants of the Loyal North Lancashire Regiment, in which his father was a sergeant, at present stationed at the Curragh, in Ireland. Dr. Macnamara was naturally much gratified with the telegram, and he has written to the sergeants of the Loyal North Lanca- shires saying that it had been a real pleasure to him to receive as his first message at the Admiralty their congratulations. If his father had been ,alive it would have greatly rejoiced him. Dr. Macnamara added that he felt a most affectionate regard for the regiment in whose ranks he had spent his early boyhood, and from the rank and file of which he learned many a lesson that has stood him in good stead in the years that had followed. * * * Mr. Joynson-Hicks seems determined to prove how little chance a tariff Reformer has of election in North-West Manchester. The Free Trade League asked him the very plain question, "Are you or are you not in favour of levying import duties for any other purpose than the raising of revenue ? " Churchill says "No." Mr. Joynson-Hicks, on the other hand, sayst ha he does not want to make the Fiscal Question the chief (or apparently any kind of) issue. Me. Joynson-Hicks tries to make the issue one merely for or against the Government, and he adds:</p>
        <p>I have tried to give full consideration to the views of Free Traders without forgetting the large body of my supporters who are not Free Traders. This is really splendid—Mr. Balfour in his most balancing mood hardly ever surpassed this effort in fence-sitting. We are not surprised (says the " Westminster Gazette ") to see that at least one Tariff Reform journal is mightily offended whilst the reply of the Manchester Free Trade League is a declaration in favour of Free Trade—i.e., of Mr. Churchill.</p>
        <p>* * * Every political orator has his own way with interrupters, and there were one or two examples of Mr. Churchill's way at his opening meeting on Tuesday. He catches an interruption, turns it over in his mind, and decides whether or not to notice it, before it has reached most of the people in the audience. Ti it is a friendly interruption he says quickly "Quite right, quite right," and goes on. A person who said "No " was quelled by Mr. Churchill stopping dead in his speech, turning towards him, and saying, with cold surprise, " Who says • No.'" * Then he repeated his statement with emphasis. He scored handily off a man in the gallery. It is eurious, by the way, that interrupters most often seek the shelter of the gallery ; they are at once harder to locate there and harder, when necessary, to eject. This gallery man called out " Rot ! " Mr. Churchill, without a pause or a smile, said : "The gentleman who says' Rot 'no doubt expresses very well what is in his head."</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Harmood Banner, M.P., said that, after reading the speech of the Bishop of Liverpool in support of the Licensing Bill, he was glad to think that he had not subscribed to his lordship's motor-car.--(Daily Paper.) How doth the little Harmood B.</p>
        <p>Delight to bark and bite, " And coarsely gird at Bishop C. 14114 m.), 1"1 For doing what is right. " Ah, woe is us," he wildly cried, "He's voting for the Bill ; He whom we thought our hope and pride Has fallen at the Phil. " But, while the Trade is looking glum, I'm not so badly hit as some ; For I can thank my lucky stars I don't subscribe to motor-cars. "It is a sweet, consoling thought That, though the Bishop's car was bought, Not a red cent from me he got; The other people paid the lot." Z.—ln the" Liverpool Post and Mercury."</p>
      </div>
