      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PACE 2.</head>
        <p>PACE 2.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>The Rates. ' flan Engineers at Richmond's Works. Football. C. Short Story by Fitzgerald Molloy. District News. 7. Science Notes. Stockton Heath Housebreaking Case. Time Table. 1. Coming Events. At Gothorn e-street Baptist Church to-morrow (Sunday) the Rev. R. E. Letheren will preach. In , the afternoon there will be a musical service at which</p>
        <p>Pages.</p>
        <p>lam.</p>
        <p>" Mrs. Letberen will speak on " Music." . The Sunday School anniversary will be celebrated at Wycliffe Church on Sunday, April 26th, when the Rev Professor Armitage will preach. In the after- noon a service of song entitled Under the palms " will he given. A meeting in sunnort of the Licensina Bill will be held at Bald-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday even- ing, the 26th inst., from 8 to 9 p.m. The Crosfield Amateur Operatic Society will give performances of the charming comic opera " The Emerald Isle " in the Parr Hall,in aid of the Infirmary and District Nursing Association, on April 30th, May lst, and 2nd. PLITLTC BEN. MT.—UNDER 77319 CLOCK FOE BEST BOOTS IN lANCASITIRE.—Advt. Conservative Club Billiard Handicap. The prizes in connection with the Christmas billiard handicap, at the Conservative Club were distributed on Saturday evening by Councillor Fulton-Smith in the presence of a large number of members. The successful competitors were as follows :-1, John Wells, case of cutlery ; 2, A. Scott, clock ; 3, E. Ashcroft, silver cigarette case • 4, C. Fairhurst, cruet; F. Cooke, biscuit dish ; 6, T. J. Lawrence, smoker's cabinet ; 7, T. Cooper, set of carvers; 8, 11. Cooke, Cigarette case; 9, A. Hough, umbrella ; 10, W. Leigh, umbrella ; 11, H. Jones, case of carvers; 12, M. Whittle, silver match box; 13, R. Ellis fruit dish. Consolation prizes : 1, R. F.Anderton, silver watch; 2, J. Sampson, cruet; 3, T. Horrabin, copper kettle ; 4, A. Spinks, silver match box ; 5, J. Pidduck, pipe ; 0, W. Taylor, silver pencil case. Asinty's, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. Six OursEas will purchase\ a fully guaranteed PREMTEN CYCLE. Inspection invited.—J. S. GAR- ASO, 51,53, &amp; 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. Meetings. 1 At the meeting of the Wycliffe Society on Sunday,Mr. R. Mills presided and Mr. G. Davey Smith gave an address on " Rubbish." He drew some lessons from every clay life,and showed what was rubbish,including drinking, swearing and gambling, and bad books. Mr. 11. Plumpton sang" 0 God have mercy" and " There is a green hill." Mr. Payton presided at the organ. lir. Bridley read the lesson. The secretary moved a vote of sympathy with the treasurer (Mr. R. Atkinson) un the death of mother and with Mr. Atkinson, son., on the death of his wife. Tt was also decided to send to Mr. and Mrs. Walley, of Manchester,congratulations on the celebration of their golden wedding. The 'weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was held in t, oct he Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. It being an 4 Open Sunday" several ladies were present. The zneeting was presided over by Mr. Healey, of the IVycliffe Society, who spoke on " The effectual power 01 prayer, and its relation to sin." The Rev. C. Harvey Cook dealt with the Licensing Bill, and its relation to temperance. instrumental quartettes were rendered IT Messrs. Wild, Watts, E. Addis and Stewart. , Master Watts also rendered a violin solo with p, qoavertina accr,mpaniment. The prizes were also distributed at the meeting. „ MOUNTAIN FLAX (Lnium CATHARTICUM)PILLS an agreeable aperient. Worth a trial. 90.—Advt. ASFILItY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt Oddfellowshl p. At a meeting of the Loyal " Constitution " Lodge on Saturday evening, the members had a visit from the district officers (P.P.G.M. Lot Brooks, D.G.M. ,George Freeman, I.G.M. Wm. Holbrook, and C.S• -:r.irk Webster.)—N.G. George EL W. Steel presided, ;au V.G. Richard K. Wilson occupied the vice-chair. There were also present P.P.G.M , Norbury, P.G. p ,°maa P. Forbes, P.G. Henry Taylor, P.G. Lester, p.,,,"'• Riddith, P.G. Watson, P.G. Spann, P.G. Ellison' -,;."'• Barker, and numerous brethren.—The Grand 41 aster thanked the Lodge for their reception, and el-Pressed the hope that they would be as loyal to him as they had been to the preceding Grand Masters of the district.—D.G.M. Freeman remarked that the f3nccess of a lodge often depended on its officers. He trusted that when a member had been through the Chairs of hi a lodge, he would not think all his work tras done, hut that he would still assist the younger ,Members with their duties. (Hear, hear.) —I.G.U. rt,.,2lbrook- said that whilst he was Grand Master of the Warrington District, he had always received the loyal BaPport of the " °institution " Lodge, and he felt mire that that same feeling would be extended to their 'resent Grand Master. Bro. Lot Brooks had been his .O-.M., and he could assure them that he was a good Oddfeliow, and would make a good Grand Master. (APplanse.)—On the'proposition of P.G. Peter Lester, seconded. by PG. Watson, and supported by P.G. ' llettry Taylor, a vote of thanks was accorded to the district officers for their visit. ' „,_ PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST 1300 TS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Local Shepherdry. ~,.The Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds (AU.) \ arringten district held its quarterly meeting at the The of Mersey "Lodge, Central Hotel, on Saturday. ' "le District Chief (Bro. P. L.Eclgar) presided and was , e. Apo r ted by the Deputy-Chief (Bro J. Gillooley), P.C.S. T. Baskerville, P.P.C.S. L. Weir P.P.C.S. • ,-t• Miller, P.P.C.S. J. Adams, P.P.C.S. W. ckersley, P.P.C.S. J. Haley, P.P.C.S. J. Gresty, '', r•R•e.S. James Parr, Dis. Treas. ; P.P.C.S. Peter , Eddgar: TheDiq. " Sec and the delegates from the various ges quarterly accounts were read by the district secretary which were found to be of a satis- , lactory character, and on the auditors (Bros. Greaty ~' and Miles) having certified their correctness the same 'were unanimously passed. The district had numeric- 'l ally made progress during the quarter. Bro. W. Miles ' 17,.n.as re-elected district auditor for the next six months. Ine election of a representative to the A.M.C. at '. Wrexham was considered when Bro. Thomas Busker- , lle, jun. was unanimously appointed to represent ' this District at such A.M.O. The question of being ; l'enresented at the next Lancashire and Cheshire and - Adjacent Counties Conference to be held at Newton- le.Willows on Saturday the 9th May next, was con- • sidered when the following brethren promised and were approved by the meeting as representativea to lattend such Conference, viz. :—Bros. T. Baskerville, J. Gillooley, Peter L. Edgar, James Parr, L. Weir, W. E. elegge, T. Miller, R. H. Halford, A. VT. i' Allen and Peter Edgar. The remaining business was ‘ of a formal character after which the meeting termin- ated in the usual manner. I. ..SITLEr's, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt . Thy GILLETTE SAFETY RAZORS for comfort in shavinv.—Acent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge- ,' street:Warrington.—Advt. Ard ;'. -.mated Pictures at Parr Hall. iii 1..,. tj.,arge audiences have assembled at the Parr Hall week, the occasion being the first visit of the ndon Animated Picture Company, who will also nec'euPy the hall next week, presenting a fine 1 p I'IPE4 of stereoscopic and coloured living pictures. , e eople in Warrington who have not yet seen this i ttleellent exhibition should not fail to take the oppor- c •5, tlitY of doing so, for the pictures are brilliant and let are perfectly steady and do not flicker in the There are all descriptions of subjects from the ' , A_tbetic to the humorous. The great Toronto fire in , Wirnerica is a vivid reproduction, and it is also very Pi,citing• Another good film shows the Rhine falls. etes of various boxing matches are shown includ- 4, Aff Jeptha v. Marchand, and Pat O'Keefe v- Charlie r tettial, and incidents in several wrestling bouts piet!ee.n noted wrestlers are depicted. There's a cem- ent:" change in the programme every evening and the tils_TainmeEt is quite up-to-date,one series of pictures it 7 ring the recent Grand National at Aintree. During , ner v.als Fred Kilmo and Preston Holmes give ereedoit....io,n,s on various musicAl instruments in a very i vraa Lne tnanner.—On Good Friday a sacred concert , repr_Rl"en• Next week the great attraction wAill be a 1 h uenction of the eat Ober Ammergiu Play, 'I 'anti( li great t.rhei.. ry Portrayed in coloured animated pictures. • (Sutd trul be another sacred concert to-morrow . ay) evening. c 'or Infine tire. ~ . nza Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermin aiit and 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>SLa</p>
        <p>WERE</p>
        <p>Fanciers' Success. At the Rochdale Fur and Feather annual show on Saturday Mrs. R. Bodel, of Latchford, won the first prize with her blue Persian male cat Sir Tibbie of Oaklands. This now makes his fourth success under four different judges. Musical Success. At the recent examinations held in Liverpool in connection with the London College of Music, Alan Brookes, pupil of Miss Green, A.L.O M., 74, Marsh- house-lane, was successful in passing first class with honours in pianoforte playing, intermediate section. Premier in name, Premier in quality. PREMIER CYCLES from 6 guineas.— J. S. GARLAND 51 to 55 Bridge-street Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Secondary School. The following pupils were successful at the Liver- pool Musical Festival on Saturday :—Trio, under 16, second prize, Archie Hodgson, Mary Hackett, and Dorothy McNeil ; duet, under 14, first prize, Arthur Greenough and May ,Turner ; duet, under 16, first prize, Violet Brown and John Greaves ; girls' solo, under 16, second prize, Violet Brown.</p>
        <p>Rowdy Warrington Thief. At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, Margaret Burke, Cropper's-brow, was charged with disorderly behaviour in Bridge-street, and further with stealing a slop jacket and a muffler, the property of Thomas Whalley.—The Chief Constable said that on Saturday night prosecutor called in. the Seven Stars Inn, for refreshment. He had occasion to leave the parcel for a few minutes, when, on his return, it was missing, and so was the prisoner who had been in the room previous Ty. Later in the evening prisoner was arrested for disorderly behaviour, and the stolen property was found in her possession. —The magistrates sent her to gaol for three months with hard labour. Mr. Wilmot Lunt. Mr. Wilmot Lunt (son of Mr. John Lunt, of Knots- ford-road) has another illustration in the current number of " Punch," and it is quite up to his best form. A lady is sewing near the fire, and her little boy is standing near her. He has evidently done something to justify some form of punishment, and he says to his mother "I could not find a switch anywhere but here's a stone you can throw at me. The expression on the child's face is a study. Arts and Crafts Society. A meeting of this society was held on Saturday afternoon at the School of Art. The headmaster of the School of Art (Mr. C. Mawson) read a paper on " Some Neglected Arts," dealing with the crafts of wood inlay, colonred carving, wood-block colour- printing ; sgraffito in plaster and pottery work and lead casting. Members displayed work in embossed leather, drawn thread design, pen and ink work embroidery and sketching from nature.</p>
        <p>Warrington Youth's Theft. At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, William Worsley, a Warrington youth, pleaded guilty to stealing a quantity of brass and lead piping, value SA, the property of Edward de Prez, of Moore. Prisoner, in company with two other youths, was drawing a cart laden with rags when they were stopped by Police-constable Whalley in Walton-road. The officer found the property concealed at the bottom of the cart hidden under the rags. The other two youths made good their escape.—The magistrates sent Woreley to prison for three months.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASEIIRE. —Aclvt. Death of MP. Peter Wells. We regret to announce the death of Mr. Peter Wells, 97, Win wick-road, who passed away somewhat suddenly on Wednesday afternoon: The deceased, who was fifty-four years of age, had been in the employ of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., for about forty years and had long held the position of clerk in charge at Dallam brewery. He was at work so recently as Monday, and the news of his death from heart failure came as A shock to his numerous friends. He was an active Conservative worker and formerly acted as yectetary of the Orford Ward Committee. Much sympathy is felt with the widow and family of seven in their bereavement. The funeral will take place at the Cemetery to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon. The first part of the service will be held at St. Paul's Church at 1.15.</p>
        <p>Trimming the Lamp. The circumstances attending the death of Martha Hardman, 17, Lord Nelson-street, were inquired into by Mr. S. Brighouse at the Police Station on Wednes- day. - Thomas Hardman, wire works labourer, said his wife would be about 58 years of age, and she suffered from dropsy in the legs. On Saturday last he left her in the house by herself, and wnen he returned a few hours later he found the kitchen full of smoke,there being a strong smell of paraffin,and his wife on the floor. The smoke and paraffin overcame him and he fainted. When he came round be found his wife had had been taken to the Infirmary. She afterwards returned home, but on the Sunday morning she was again taken there, where she died the same day. -- Martha Hardman, daughter of the witness, said she was not in the house at the time. Her mother told her she was trimming a lamp when she had a fainty bout.— The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermsrket Street.—Advt A Foolish Practical Joke. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ A very foolish practical joke was played on the Warrington Fire Brigade last night week. Shortly before eight o'clock they received information by telephone that a fire had broken out at the works of W. D. Houghton at Sankey Bridges. Deputy-Superinten- dent Turner and a full complement of men at once started for the scene of the supposed conflagation with the steam fire engine "Captain." On arrival at the works they found that no fire had occurred, but it was suggested that there might be one at "Houghton's Farm, Bold." The brigade without delay started for Bold, but in this case also it was found that there was no corrflagration, and they returned to the fire station pleased of course that their services had not been required, but indignant at having been hoaxed. It is supposed that some one went to the call office in the Market-place, rang up the Central office and tasked to be put on to the fire station and then gq.ve the false alarm. The police have the matter in hand and it is hoped the offender will be brought to justice.</p>
        <p>Amalgamated Friendly Societies' Medical Association. The quarterly meeting of the above took place on Thursday night at the Blue Lion Hotel, Market Place, Mr, Richard Blackshaw (the president) presiding ; Mr. Wm. Oxley, vice-president ; Mr. Peter Edgar, treasurer ; Mr. Wm. Bibby, secretory ; and the repre- sentatives of the various lodges forming the Associa- tion being present.—The Secretary reported a very heavy quarter's work. Sickness had been very great, although deaths had not been more ' than the average. e reported that everythiu, in connection with the Association was going on most satisfactory manner, the finances of the Associa- tion were satisfactory, and the medical and dispensary work all that could be desired. Messrs. Frank Shaw and Thomas Shaw, auditors, reported upon the balance sheet and accounts as being in good order and correct. Dr. Martin presented his report which was adopted. It was decided that the Managing Committee go care- fully through the registered rules of the Society and report to the next Quarterly Meeting before printing new rules. Thanks were acc-irded to medical and other officers.</p>
      </div>
