      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>'CO AGULINE,' KLINK,"TENASITINE:--Cements for broken and other articles.—Advt.</head>
        <p>'CO AGULINE,' KLINK,"TENASITINE:--Cements for broken and other articles.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Technical Institute : Science Students' Association. I On Saturday a lecture on sugar refining was delivered before the Association by Mr. S. Hardcastle, late of Fairries Ltd.. Liverpool. Mr. P.Fisher presided. Sugar refining, said the lecturer, was not a chemical ' process, but purely mechanical. The old-fashioned I methods of using white of egg and bullock's blood, were not used now. It was not done by bleaching neither by electrical means. The sugar came to this, country from abroad in a damp crystalline form, vary- ing in colour from light yellow to dark brown. It was I bought like a number of other products,by its analysis, best sugar polarising up to 97 per cent. In refining it was first melted with hot water and filtered through calico bags. It was then run out of a large cistern through a column of animal charcoal, which changed in colours from that of vinegar to clear water, white. It was then evaporated in copper vacuum pans, and as the water vapour ran off it was pumped away, until the sugar crystallized as required. The sugar was then separated from the mother liquor by centrifugal machines, after which it was graded according to the qualities, and packed. After /he lecture, Mr. Hard- castle threw on the screen a number of slides illustra- tive of the various processes which sugar went through and the various machinery. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to him, on the motion of Mr. A. J. ,Newton, seconded by Mr. Joynson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 18,1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 18,1908.</p>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY. How the Day was Observed at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The prospect of congenial weather conditions induced many Warringtonians to seek "fresh fields and pastilles new " fur the Easter holidays and Thursday witneseed a large exodus from the town, the railway stations presenting a very busy scene. Good Friday was a day of beautiful sunshine which made it pleasant to be out of doors. On the outskirts of the town the highways were literally alive with cyclists who, notwithstanding a troublesome wind and 'dusty roads, extracted plenty of enjoyment, from the pastime. In the afternoon the football match Warrington v. Leeds at Wilderspool was a great attraction. The religious observance of the day was on the customary lines, services being held at all the Church of England and Roman Catholic churches and at several Nonconformist churches. At Wycliffe Church a brief service was held in the morning, the Rev. John Yonge delivering an address. In accordance with a long-standing custom the foreign missionary anniversary was celebrated at Bold-street Wesleyan Church and at the miming service the congregation had the privilege of hearing a sermon by the Rev. Dr. Waddy Moss, of Didsbury College. At several of the Anglican Churches the chief feature of the day was the three hours devotional service with addresses on the Seven Sayings from the Cross. In the evening the choir of the Parish Church sang part 1 of Gounod's " Redemption " a large congregation listening to a creditable interpretation of this beautiful music. Given a continuance of the pleasant spring weather, the numerous excursions which have been arranged by the railway companies for to-day and Easter Monday will doubtless be largely patronized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>GOOD FRIDAY FOOTBALL. . Warrington Win th 6 Last Match. Warrington's League programme for this season was concluded yesterday, when Leeds paid a visit to the headquarters at Wilderspool. Beautiful weather prevailed, and the crowd numbered quite 5,000. From a playing point of view the only drawback was the hardness of the ground. Both teams had absentees, and lined out as follows :</head>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY FOOTBALL. . Warrington Win th 6 Last Match. Warrington's League programme for this season was concluded yesterday, when Leeds paid a visit to the headquarters at Wilderspool. Beautiful weather pre- vailed, and the crowd numbered quite 5,000. From a playing point of view the only drawback was the hard- ness of the ground. Both teams had absentees, and lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Jordan, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Watkin, three-quarters; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Harmer, Naylor, Jolley and Heath, forwards. Leeds.—Young, full back; Rowlands, Desborough, P. Thomas and liazlehurst, three-quarters ; Ward and Howell, half backs ; Webster, Birch, Brown, Wain- wright, Harrison and Whittaker, forwards. Warrington had the sun in their eyes to commence with, but were soon on the attack, and P. Boardman opened the score with a try from a cross-kick by Jordan. Fish kicked a fine goal. Leeds responded well, Ward making a fine run down the centre and serving Hazle- burst, who was bundled in touch in the home quarter The stay here was brief, and a penalty kick putting Warrington on the attack again. Lloyd swung the ball out to Dickenson who shammed a pass to Fish and raced over cleverly. Another visit to the home line was made by the Leeds forwards, and Ward tried a, drop- kick which failed. More fine passing by Warrington proved irresistible and Fish dashed over at the corner. The next item was an injury to Watkin after a long run, and he took no further part in the game. Fish obliged with another lovely try before the interval when Leeds were 14 points down. On resuming, Warrington were quickly on the offen- sive and Fish scampered over again, only to be recalled for a pass forward. The crack winger got hurt in a tackle and retired for some time. Meanwhile Taylor dropped a neat goal. Howell made a fine, dodgy run for Leeds and passed to Ward who scored easily, Young adding a goal. With only four forwards Warrington were hard pressed for a while. Fish came to the rescue and hacronly been on the field a minute when he got a short pass from Lloyd and scored Warrington's 100th try in League games this season, amid great cheering. The effort taxed his injured leg and he retired finally. Warrington held their own well and just on titne,Taylor rushed over after a combined effort with P. Boardman. Final, Warrington 2 goals, G tries (22 points) ; Leeds 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>DRUIDS' DISTRICT GRAND MASTER.</head>
        <p>DRUIDS' DISTRICT GRAND MASTER.</p>
        <p>Presentation.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the members of the King William IV. Lodge of Druids, together with the officers of the District,met at dinner to honour District Grand Master John Jackson. The company included Senior Trustee William Bibby (who presided), Dr. Martin, D.D.G.M. George Shaw, D.C.S. Ed. Allan, D.T. Robert Perdue, P.D.G. Masters Pointon, Packer, Lodge Officers and Brothers Rushton, Rose, Gilbert Riley, John Jackson, sen and others. After the usual loyal and other toasts given by the Chairman, D.D.G.M. GEORGE SHAW made the present- tion of a silver medallion, suitably engraved, on behalf of the Lodge. He said that in their District Master they had a man who was almost tire- less in his efforts to promote the best interests of the Order of Druids in Warrington. He never let him (Mr. Shaw) rest; he would have him up and doing. He con- sidered the Lodge was honouring itself by honouring the District Master. Speeches in terms of praise were given by -Bros. Pointon, Packer. Riley and Rushton. The DISTRICT MASTER in replying said he did not think he deserved all the praise he had received that night. He certainly never allowed an opportunity to pass by of doing what he could for his Order and he was pleased to find that his efforts were appreciated by the members. He joined the Order at 16 years of age and in twelve years he found himself District Master. He"was very proud of the office and tendered his best thanks to all present for the tokens of respect and good will the members had presented him with. He would always look upon their present with the kindliest feelings. The Chairman then gave" The Warrington Amalgam- ated Friendly Societies' Medical Association," coupling with the toast the name of Dr. Martin. He said the Medical Association was doing a noble work inside their Friendly Society movement, a work that was giving the utmost satisfaction to all members and at the same time giving little or no offence to the medical gentlemer of the town. ft was the strong desire of the Committee that everything in connection with their Association should be done in accordance with strict medical etiquette as they desired to see the best feel- ings exist between their Medical Officer and the other medical gentlemen of the town. _ . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. MARTIN, in replying, said he thanked all present for the very kind way his name had been received. He had now been in Warrington nearly two years and the more he saw of Warrington people, the better he liked them. He had received nothing but kindness since he came to the town, and he hoped the Friendly Society movement would continue to prosper as he believed a vast amount of good was being done by such societies in a very quiet and proper way. Mr. JOHN JACKSON gave "The Warrington Equalized District Order of Druids." Ho said he was very proud that night to see his son District Grand Master and hoped the Order would prosper under his Chairmanship, DISTRICT TREASURER (Bro. Perdue), responded and said they had paid away nearly £lOO during the last three months to relieve their sick brethren. Many a home had been made brighter by this money, and he hoped the Order would continue its useful work. DISTRICT SECRETARY (Bro. Allen) said it was pleasure to him to do honour to the District Grand Master. The Order in Warrington was doing good work and was in a prosperous position. He strongly recom- mended all young members to bring their companions iuto the Order with them as young men could not learn habits of thrift too soon. Friendly societies made young men better fitted for business and altogether improved thorn. Songs were given by Bros. Gilbert 'Riley and I. Rose.—..P.N.G.A. Rushton ;n proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman said that in Mr. flibby the Society had one of the most enthusiastic workers he had ever known. His connection with the Order was of many years standing during the whole of which time be bad been a most earnest worker and it gave him much pleasure to propose this vote. The Chairman responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Warrington and District League. (IST DIvISIONL OUGHTRINGTON PARK v. BURTONWOOD. (DODDS CHALLENGE SHIELD.)</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Warrington and District League. (IST DIvISIONL OUGHTRINGTON PARK v. BURTONWOOD. (DODDS CHALLENGE SHIELD.)</p>
        <p>This important match took place at Heatley last Saturday. The visitors came with a splendid record, not having lost a single poipt in the above competition, whereas the hornesters were determined to avenge their only defeat which was inflicted upon them at Burton. wood. Dowd started the game half an hour late and after a fruitless corner at each end, the play settled down to midfield, where the visitors showed much smart- ness. Ouglittington lost ground by over keenness and Settle cleared effectually with good play. The visitors' iforwards took up the running and Dowd scored with a shot from close quarters. The homesters pressed almost continuously up to the interval, but were unable to beat the smart defence. Buttonwood led at half-time by 1 goal to nil. Button- wood re-started with only 10 men, Appleton having met with an injury to his back, Glennon taking his place. F. Lord had a good attempt charged down and then the ball went to Barnett, who forced it into the net, thus equalizing amid great excitement. Graham had to retire and Appleton returned hereabouts. In the last few minutes the homesters pressed on the left and Goddard centred the ball. A. Lord received and taking deliberate aim, gave Ball no chance with a shot which registered the winning goal for his side, Oughtrington proving victor of a hard game by 2 goals to 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING. Benson—Garnett.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING. Benson—Garnett.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable local interest was solemnized at Trinity Wesleyan Church, Southport, on Wednesday when Miss Daisy Garnett, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. David Garnett, Sandhurst, Southport, was married to Mr. Walter llamlyn Benson,of London. Mr. Garnett is well-known in Warrington as a director of the firm of Messrs. Robert Garnett and Sons. A large congregation witnessed the cere- mony, the officiating ministers/ being the Rev. F. Benson (brother of the bridegroom) and the Rev. Enoch Salt. The service was choral, the school chil- dren being in attendance and singing the Bridal Chorus from " Lohengrin "as the bridal party entered. The hymn " 0 perfect love " was sung and Ur. Hudson presided at the organ and played festal selections. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in an ivory liberty satin dress in empire style, with embroidered holt. She wore a veil surmounted with orange blossom and carried a lovely sheaf of lilies. She was attended by five bridesmaids —Miss Katheline Bilekley (Bowdon), Miss Rosalie G srnett (Cressington),Miss Maisie Posnett (Runcorn),Miss Rosamund Parker (Penketh), and Miss Gicennie Holman (Sutton, Surrey)' who were prettily dressed in pale green satin, the bodices being hand- somely applipteel with Venetian lace, with charming hats of pale tulle, drawn under the chin with strap of green velvet. They carried poseys of yellow margnerites and wore specially designed pendants in wrought silver, the gifts of the bride. The Rev. H. H. Benson (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man After the ceremony a reception was held at the Palace Hotel, Birkdale, the guests including : —Mr. and Mrs. Benson,Mrs. Robert Garnett, Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, M”. and Mrs. R. 11. P isinett, Mr. and Mrs. R. E. Garnett, Ur. and Mrs. Christopher Bradshaw, Mr. A. Payne Garnett, Mr. and Mrs.. James Dodds, Misses Dodds, Dr. Simpson, -Mr. and Mrs. Robert Sadler, Mr. Ernest Garnett, Mr. and Mrs. Richard Holman (Benfleet Hall, Surrey), Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Buckley, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Moss, Misses Moss, Mr. and Mrs. John Boston, Misses Boston, Misses Pearson, Mrs. Fildes, Mr. and Mrs. Dow, Mrs. and Miss Hui rter, Mr. and Mrs. Waterhouse, Mr. and Mrs. George Walker, Mr. and Mrs. Baxendell, Miss Baxendell, Mr. and Mrs. Cathcart Smith, Miss Cathcart ' Smith, Mr. and Mrs. Jenks, Miss Jenks, Mr. Clegge Thomas, Mrs. Thomas, Ur. and Mrs. R. R. Allen,Mr. and Mrs. Hartley (Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs. F. G. Wallis, ' Miss Leighton, Mrs. Med ringtnn, Mr. Fpank I Mr. and Mrs. Vernon Milner, Rev. E. and Mrs. Salt, Miss Salt, Rev. and Mrs Kirby, Rev. and Mrs. McCrea, Mr. and !qrs. T. Harlock, Mr A. Redfern, Misses Birdie and Qmenie Law, Mrs. Claxton. Later in the day the newly-married cmple, who were the recipients of HO handsome presents, left for Wales, where the honeymoon is being spent. They will afterwards take up their residence at Norbury, near London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"THE MIDNIGHT WEDDING."</p>
        <p>The new romantic play "The Midnight Wedding," by Waltor Howard, which was produced at the Lyceum in June last, and was described by the London Press as " a bright, clean, dashing melodrama," will be staged at the Court Theatre next week. The company is that of Mr. Alfred Paurnier and Walter Howard —an excellent company, which includes Miss Ethel 13racewe11 as Princess Astrea von Strelsburg, and Mr. Frank E. Petley as Paul Valmar, sword- master of the Red Hussars. The play goes with a swing, and has been well received by crowded houses at every theatre visited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMM " The Prodigal Son."</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMM " The Prodigal Son."</p>
        <p>Lovers of the type of drama associated with the writings of Mr. Hall Caine have had a treat at the Royal Court Theatre this week, for the "Prodigal Son " is undoubtedly one of the hest works from Mr. Hall Caine's pen. For ourselves, we do not care much for the emotionalism which is rampant in the play ; it lacks delicacy and refinement, but to those with a taste for " strong meat " in the histrionic line it will doubtless give considerable pleasure. Mr. George Day's Company is an excedingly capable one and does ! full justice to the many exacting situations with which the play abounds. The story centres round the personnel of two brothers and two sisters. The wrong brother and sister marry each other in the first act. Oscar Stephenson thus does an injury to his brother Magnus, and finds out his mistake only when his -poor wife Thora has died of a broken heart through unrequited affection. There is a world of difference between the characters of both the brothers and the sisters, and the union of opposites in this case is not a success. Messrs. Albert Ward (Oscar) and George A. Brandram (Magnus), Miss Viola Holland (Helga) and Miss Winifred Ingham (Thora) practically carry the play on their shoulders and are a most adcomplis.hed quartette. Miss Ingham plays the dual roles of wife and daughter and is admirable in both. Fier youthfulness and naivete in the last act, where Oscar makes his great reparation, were charming. Practically the only touch of humour is provided by the sharp-tongued Margaret Neilson (Miss Mima Renouf) who has , some droll things to say about " the men " and says them well, Miss Florence Lovell makes an ideal Mrs. Stephenson, and Mr. H. H. Barrington (Stephen Magnusson) and Mr.-Charles Cooper (Oscar Neilson) make first-class outraged parents. The minor parts are all well done, and the excellence of the ,` business " has well merited the large audiences seen throughout the week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FOREIGN MISSIONS. Anniversary Services at ;Bold-street.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSIONS. Anniversary Services at ;Bold-street.</p>
        <p>Foreign Missionary Anniversary services were held at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday, the preacher in the morning being the Rev. E. J. Gawne and in the evening the Rev. Sidney Pitt who took for his text "Behold, I have set before thee an open door " (Rev. iii. 8). The preacher referred to the want of progress of Christianity in the first three centuries and to the great opportunity that was afforded for the promoting of Christianity at that time by the Roman Empire with its means of communication and other circumstances. In the world at large to-day there was a similar oppor- tunity. Mr. Pitt also enlarged upon the physical accessibility of the different sections of the world and the modern development of means of transit. The political accessibility was that the missionary could go wherever he would and was always under the protection at civilized Governments. Ile recommended the con- gregation to read a recent production entitled " Chris- tian Missions and Social Progress," and stated that missions had already done more for social progress than the Socialist agitators. He called upon them to adopt the principle of the student volunteer movement which had been introduced in the universities, for the evangelisation of the world in the present generation. The services were continued yesterday morning when a powerful discourse was preached by the Rev. R. Waddy Moss, D.D. (pro lessor of theology at Didsbury College, Manchester).</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING. In the evening the annual meeting was held, Mr. Edwin Brown (Lymm) presiding. The deputation con- sisted of the Revs. Henry Adams and R. Heslam. The Rev. T. MILLBR presented the report. The income from all sources during the year was £208,308 and expenditure £197,874. The annual subscriptions showed an increase of £ll,OOO. The Liverpool District had an increase of over £l,OOO. There had been an increase of over four thousand members. Locally they had raised £285, Bold•street Church contributing £34. Owing to delay in sending in accounts a slight decrease was shown but in reality there was an increase. (Applause). Rev. HENRY Ana.ms (Liverpool) said he had worked in the West Indies for fifteen years. It was the most marvellous place for missionary work on record. They worked amongst the negroes and they had churches, Sunday schools, and day schools throughout the different islands. At present they had over 50.000 con- verts, about a thousand local preachers, and 500 chapels and other preaching places. The work was done under many difficulties, and the islands had frequently been devastated by earthquakes and hurricanes. The greatest calamity was the recent earthquake in Jamaica, but the people were still faithful end hopeful, and the chapels were being re-built. (Applause.) The Rev. R. HESLAM (Manchester), said he had worked in West Africa for a considerable time and the great difficulty under which they laboured was the trying climate but still they had been very successful. It was very difficult for white men to live there and their most hopeful feature was the raising of a ministry in order that the natives might carry on the work themselves. Mr. Heslam's address was interspersed , with interesting descriptions of scenery and native I characteristics.</p>
        <p>For Children's Hacking Cough at night, Woods Great Peppermint Cure, Is. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>There was a first team gate on Saturday to witness the semi-final between Warrington "A" and Radcliffe Rangers in the Lancashire Shield Competition, and a very interesting game was seen. Warrington had the assistance of several first-teamers who were eligible for this competition, alai of course, proved much too strong for the Manchester juniors. The score at the end was 35 points to 7, but the Radcliffe men had much moze of the game that the figures suggest.</p>
        <p>--</p>
        <p>The match afforded a striking example of the improvement effected in the team by the introduction of a couple ofd" class" backs. Taylor and Fish made the back dlvisioninto one of considerable paerer, and about half the points came from their operations on the right wing The change of positions once again emphasised Fish's versatility. He was brilliant in the extreme and scored four tries against a defence which was particularly energetic and determined. It ie a question whether the regular" A" team would have defeated the visitors, but the help of the first- teamers doubled the effectiveness of the team.</p>
        <p>The final now rests between Warrington "A " and Wigan " A," and the result of the toss for choice of grounds having gone against Warrington, the match will be played at Wigan, probably next ,saturday. Warrington will be entitled to play Fish, Taylor, Brooks Ind P. Boardman, while Wigan will have the services of David and Todd (the New Zealander), so that the match will arouse considerable interest and should draw a big crowd to Central Park. The com- peting teams take a third each of the gate money and the remaining third goes to swell the coffers of the Union, which usually takes care to have a finger in every good pie.</p>
        <p>The final for the N.U. Cup will, after all, be con- tested by Yorkshire Clubs on the Huddersfield ground next Saturday. Broughton Rangers failed dismally against Hunslet, and the weakness which was apparent against Warrington until Jenkins got hurt, was shown up mercilessly by the crack Yorkshire team. Hull beat Leigh comfortably as was expected, but the Cup seem ,4 a certainty for Hunslet. The Parksiders have never yet won it, their solitary appearance in the final being some years ago at Fallowaeld when Oldham beat them in hollow fashion. I well remember what a great game Tom Fletcher played for Oldham that day. He was right at the top of his form and simply plunged through the Hunslet defence time after time. Hunslet stand a better chance of winning both the Nil, and the League Cups this season than did Oldham a year ago.</p>
        <p>The League semi-finals will he played to-day, Oldham and Wigan meeting at Oldham, and Hunslet and Broughton at Hunslet. It is long odds on the home teams coming out on top.</p>
        <p>The league table up-to-date is as follows : For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. 1). G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'ge. Oldham Huns] et Broughton Wi gn n Halifax Warrington 30 18 9 364 101 431 27 34 156 6500 Hull K. R. Sal ford Wakefield T 3l 19 11 1 62 92 400 56 69 319 6290 Batley 32 20 12 0 63 78 360 51 68 306 62.50 FCeighley 29 16 12 1 51 61 291 52 60 284 56.89 Bradford 12 17 15 0 53 61) 311 49 81 350 53'12 Runcorn 29 15 14 0 27 66 252 26 49 199 5P72 Barrow 30 15 15 0 25 62 236 29 59 235 50-00 Hull 3l 14 17 0 51 71 315 45 65 285 4516 Dewsbury 3l 13 17 1 50 58 274 49 78 333 43.54 Rochdale Huddersfield Leigh ' Leeds 32 10 21 1 51 56 230 67 87 395 3250 Swinton 3O 9 29 1 30 40 180 44 76 316 31.64 York Merthyr Widnes St. Helens 2B 6 20 2 32 40 184 52114 446 2500 Ebbw Vale 29 5 22 225 30 140 64 96 416 2068. Bramley 3l 426 1 29 38 172 95 157 661 14'51</p>
        <p>Warrington completed their League programme in dashing style yesterday, Leeds being well beaten by 22 points to 5. For the first time in League football Warrington passed the 100 in the number of tries scored during the season. The fifth try of the game brought up the three figures, and curiously enough this was scored when Warrington looked like finishing one short. Fish had been Ebsent most of the second half, but in a brief return visit he got the ball and scored his third and most precious try. Then he limped off. " Jack "played a brilliant game and blamed the hard ground for his damaged knee. Watkin also suffered in the same way, and it was surprising to see the way Warrington held their own with only four forwards. The greatness of Taylor and Dickenson at centre was mostly responsible for Leeds being rendered i mpotent, though Lloyd and Jordan were both smart. It was a lively game, and demonstrated the powerfulness of the home back division to a remarkable degree.</p>
        <p>--- To-day Warrington and Wakefield Trinity play an exhibition game at West Ham, London. The players leave this morning at 8 30, and the following team was selected last 'night : Tilley ; Fish, Dickenson Cartwright and Jordan ; Lloyd and Brooks ; A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Naylor, Heath, Jolley, and Harmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RUGBY WORKS COUPETITION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY WORKS COUPETITION.</p>
        <p>The following are the concluding results of the first round of this Competition. Monday, P. ,t K., 14, Walker's Brewery, 0. Tuesday, Cresfield's Soap Works, 20,Longford, 0. Second round : Wednesday, Fletcher Russell, 23, Greenall's Brewery, 2. Thursday, L. &amp; N.-W., 13, Firth .Mattress, 5. Reolayed match</p>
        <p>, - P. &amp;K. (DAllain), 5, Corporation of Warrington, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Ptrsisn 11TEETINGS.—A meeting of the Parish Council was held in the Wesleyan day school on Monday evening. at 7.15 p.m., Dr. Murray, J.P., presided. Other members of the Council present were: Messrs. J. G. Withinsha James Woodward, James Ditchfield, D. Houghton, R. Routlege, E. Foster (clerk).—Dr. Murray, J.P., was ; re-elected chairman for the ensuing 12 months and Mr.J. O.Withinshaw(v ice-chair man.) —Mr.Routledge proposed that the three present overseers be re-elected :—Mr. J. Ditchfleld,Dr.Murray,Mr. J. G.Withinshaw—Nfr. Wood- ward seconded and the motion was carried.—The follow- ing letter was read from Mr.G.F.Clarke of the Lancashire Education Committee, Earlestown. " I beg to inform you that the period of &amp;lice of the present members of the local Sob-Committee will , expire on the 31st May next and I shall be pleased Ito receive the nominations of new members not later than the 15th April 190 S. The new committee will-commence from the 31st June 1908. The basis of constitution of the new sub-committee is similar to previous years, viz. : One representative for each school with under 400 scholars in average attend- ance." Dr. Murray and Mr. J. G. Withinshaw were re-elected.—The Chairman proposed that the following resolution be forwarded to the County Council :—"That the County County be requested to use their best efforts to 'press forward the alteration of the canal bridge at Sankey Bridges."—Carried unanimously,—The Chairman read correspondence from the Post Of9ce authorities respecting the morning delivery of letters. The Council, it was stated, expected to hear further news in the course of a few days.—This concluded the Council meeting, after which the annual parish meeting was held to discuss parish affairs. In opening, the Chairman said that they had had a very quiet year. He was afraid the rates would be a little higher and the Union call also a little higher. —Mr. Mainlery said the footpath on the road from the Lion to the Stores was a disgrace, and it was resolved that a resolution he sent to the Rural District Council asking them to repair it forthwith at this point.—Mr. Ambler called the attention of the meeting to the high speed of the motor cars along War- rington-road. He was afraid that if this practice was not checked some of the children would be killed.—Mr. Houghton also spoke about the terrific speed of cars.— It was decided that the attention of The Superintendent of Police should be called to see if the speed could not be checked.—Mr. A. T. Clay proposed that this parish meeting should pass a vote of thanks to the Misses Tinsley for the gift of land in Station-road. The corner of the road had been made to look a great deal better, and the pit had also been fenced round.—Mr. Routledge seconded and the motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>What took away my deep remorse (Brought on by pork and apple sauce) And made me I could " eat a horse ?" "Life Tonic." From H. M. MIITON, 61, Sankey-st.. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>CHILD'S HORRIBLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Mr. Ridgway held an inquest on the body of Joseph Comes, Bridgewater Mills, Balton-road, whose death occurred on Friday evening after having fallen into a disused tan-pit at the above mill. It was stated that there were a number of these pits, most of which had been tilled in, and on the day in question the barricading around this pit had been taken down, and it was being tilled in.—Dr. Robinson stated that the deceased was given an emetic, and he vomited thick slutch. If be nad only swallowed water he might have got round. In his opinion he died from suffocation, caused by the solid and liquid matter pene- trating the lungs.—The jury returued a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence. SAD BURNING FATALITY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Station, on Friday, Deputy Coroner F. A. Jones held an inquest on the body of Joseph, the 13 months' old sun of Edward and Mary Reardon, of 3, Mersey View, Widnes, who died on Wednesday of last week following severe burns received on the pievious day. l'he mother of the deceased said that at half-past ten on Tuesday morning she went into the yard of her house for some coal leaving \ the baby lying on the edge of the hearthrug. 'lucre was a fire in the grate. While she was getting the coal she heard a scream. On running back into the house she found the child's clothing in flames. A piece of burning wood was lying on the fender There had been a piece of wood burning for, some time on the fire. Witness put the flames out and poured the contents of a till 01 treacle over the baby.—The Deputy Coroner : Didn't you think that the treacle would bring the !testi off with it when it was cleaned off ?—Witness : On! I hardly knew wnat 1 was doing.—ln reply to the Deputy Coroner the witness said there was no fireguard around the grate. There was a fender. The child was not wearing flannel- ette clothing.—The jury returned a verdict ot " Acci- dental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Worshouse, on Tuesday, Mr. G. Pilling in the chair. There were also present :—Messrs. T. Wright, H. Woodward, J. B. Chadwtck, C. E. Linaker, jun. T. Antwiss, P. Booth. J. Myuekyme, A. Haining, J. Weir, J. IL Davies, G. Egerton Warburton, T. Bazter,George Watson, G. F. Ashton (clerk), J. Diggle and T.6winton (surveyors). A letter was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning the re-appoiutment of Dr. Adams as Aledical Officer of Health at £l5O per annum. The Clerk reported that Mr. George Watson had been duly elected for IN o Hey. The Chairman extended a welcome to Mr. Wat Son, and remarked that they hoped Mr. IVatson would be a useful member. Norley as a rule was very behind hand.</p>
        <p>Mr. Watson thanked the chairman for his remarks and said he hoped he woulci be able to get on better than his predecessors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MR. _ASHTON THANKED.</head>
        <p>MR. _ASHTON THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said that in certain indirect ways Mr. Ashton had done little things in the interests of the Council which had been of great benefit to them. They had never made any public recognition of those services and he moved that the best thanks of the Council be I accorded to him. I Mr. Davies seconded and the resolution was carried. Mr. Ashton briefly responding. . . . .</p>
        <p>Jr. Antwiss moved "that the notification of Births Act, 1907, be adopted in the area of this Council and that the consent of the Local Government Board be requested thereto." I The following resolution was passed at a meeting of the Parliamentary Committee: "That there be added to Mr. Autwiss's resolution for the adoption of the Notification of Births Act 1907, a provision that the resolution be not forwarded to the Local Government Board for approval until a committee in conjunction with the Medical Officer of Health have devised a scheme to the satisfaction of the Council for the giving of advice and instruction to those who have charge of infants when the Council adoption of the Act has been approved by the Local Government Board." A Committee consisting of Nfessrs. Chadwick, Linaker Jun., Woodward and the Chairman and vice-chairman was appointed. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Board was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon, Mr. r. Baxter in the chair. There were also present : Messrs. .1. Spruce, A. P. Norman, J. H. Davies, G. Watson, J. Collins, A. Haining, J. Mynekyme, G. Pilling, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, jun., T. Wright, P. Booth, J. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, G. F. Ashton (clerk), G. Pearson, W. H. de Prez, T. Cooke (relieving officers), and J. M. Boyce (acting master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>COUNTY 'COUNCIL AND 'HIGHER EDUCATION. . .</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY 'COUNCIL AND 'HIGHER EDUCATION. . .</p>
        <p>The following letter was read from Mr. George Leigh, clerk to the Bucklow Union. "In reply to your inquiry the resolution of my Board sent to you aims at the £20,000 kept in hand by the County Education Authority or Council in respect of Higher Education as , a special reserve or suspense account of the County fund to meet any deficiencies in future years for further expenditure which may fall upon the Higher Education accounts. My Guardians consider that no such fund should exist but each year should meet its own expenses, &amp;c." , Mr. Morris moved that the resolution be supported. 1 Mr. Antwiss seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman moved that the resolution be not supported. He thought the reserve fund was sound finance. There had been a considerable amount of money spent on secondary education this year, and if there had been no money to fall back upon the rate would perhaps have to be doubled. He thought the ratepayers would prefer to have a uniform rate rather than have a low rate one year and a doubled rate the following year. As far as the County Council were concerned they were in the right line.</p>
        <p>;Mr. Linaker : It is time they were. I Nil% Norman said that those sort of remarks were unwarranted. The County Council as too severely criticised by those outside. He felt very strongly in that matter, and he thought the Council were doing sound business in nursing that balance. Mr. Morris said that the reason he moved that the resolltion be supported was that he believed that the Council should year by year stand by itself. Mr. S. Woodward said Mr. Morriss's resolution was 'an utter mistake, because their rates would have to be compulsorily collected. After further discussion the letter was ordered to lie on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NEW MASTER AND MATRON.</head>
        <p>THE NEW MASTER AND MATRON.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. D. Rollinson, the newly-appointed master, wrote stating that he and the matron wished to com- mence their duties at the Workhouse from Saturday, April 18th, if this met with the approval of the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ST HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED STABBING OHMAGE AT ST. HELENS Edward Sevin, a young collier, who bad only been married a week or two, was charged at St. Helens on Monday .with stabbing his brother-in-law, William CaNert, of Nicholson-street, Parr, with whom he had been residing since his marriage. It appeared from the ' evidence that accused on the evening of Saturday, the 4th inst., said to Calvert " Give me the key, and I will , lock the door and you can go to bed." Calvert refused, ' saying," No man will have the key of my house." A , fight ensued, and Calvert alleged that he was stabbed in , the side and back of the head and on the right hand by the prisoner with a breadknife. Mrs. Calvert corroborated as to the attack, and Dr. Davies described the wounds, adding that Calvert would have to remain in hospital for some time. The prisoner pleaded not guilty, and reserved his defence.—He was sent for trial at the assizes. On the application of Mr. Kelly, bail was allowed in £5O and two sureties of £2b each. ST. HELENS CRICKET CLUB. At the annual meeting of the St. Helens Cricket Club, hold on Monday, Mr. R. W. /1. Thomas, who presided, said he was sorry they had to present an adverse balance. The balance had gone up from £23 to £36, but that was caused by extraordinary expenses. Those expenses had amounted to over £2O. A good many of those items would not occur again, but the actual cost of running the club was about £155, and it could not be run successfully on less if they were to remain members of the South Lancashire League. Being in the league, they had to have a good professional; but, on the other hand, the gates had improved very considerably. They had bade very good season indeed. They niust con- gratulate the first eleven on winning the championship. They hoped to be able to main that proud position. (Applause.) They were still members of the league, and they had engaged their old professional, who had served them very well last season. No doubt, golf, cycling, and tennis had of late years interfered with cricket, not only in St. Helens, but in other towns. He rather thought that people were tiring of those games, and they would he paving more attention to cricket. Their total subsariptions from members was £B9, patrons and vice-presidents gave £l6, and the gates were £lO, a total of £146. They could not run the club on less than £155, and he hoped they would not rest satisfied until the income of the club fully reached that figure. The Rev. J. M. Courtenay, hon. treasurer, said the League had undoubtedly done the club good in many ways. They had lost three vice-presidents, one of whom, Colonel Richard Pilkington, was the best sup- porter they had. The club owed very much to Colonel R. Pilkington. Mr. F. Dromgoole seconded, and the balance-sheet was adopted. Mr. Dudley Aukland was elected secretary, and Mr. Walter Dromgoole bon. treasurer. Mr. S. G. Urmson was re-elected captain; Mr, Philip Newton, ground secretary ; with a committee consisting of Messrs. R. W. H. Thomas, M. J. Hammill, G. A. Marsden,F. Drom- goole, A. Crompton, T. Wood, P. Morris, Dr. Knowles, the Rev. J. M. Courtenay, and Dr. Dow. Mr. P. Newton was appointed mid-week captain, and the second eleven captaincy was left open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Before Colonel Wood (in the chair), and Messrs. Roges C. Parr, T. Ormand and W. COiiingwood. ALLEGED INDECENT LANGISTAGE.—Thomas Glynn, of Earlestown, was summoned for using indecent language at Earlestown.—Mr. E. A. White appeared for the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—Sergeant Cooper said that at about 12.30 a.m. on March 27th, prisoner was in his front garden shouting and using indecent language.—Mr. E. A. White submitted that there wr 3 not a statute under which the Bench could convict in this case. The prosecution had proved certain bylaws, and defendant was summoned under the statute.—The Bench dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY TRESPASS AT EARLESTOWN.—Two Earles- town men, named John Hill and John Davies, were summoned for trespassing on the railway at Earlestown, and thereby exposing themselves to danger on March 14th.—They pleaded guilty. Detective Coward said the company had had numerous complaints about people crossing the line at Earlestown. At 5.45 a.m. on March 14th, in company with Sergeant Cooper he saw defen- dants walk along the up platform, jump on to the line, cross two sets of metals, and then cross the down plat- form. He asked them what they had done that for, and they said they were " taking a short cut to their work." Witness told them they would be reported and probably summoned. There were notices at every station waning people not to cross,the line.—Hill, who had been previously fined for a similar offence, was fined 10s. and costs and Davies was fined ss. and costs. MISCELLANEOUS CASES.—The following were fined for drunkenness :—lVilliam Edwards, Frederick Hope, William Broxon, George Derbyshire, Joseph Webster, John Webster, Thomas Wood. 2s. 6d. and costs each; and Elizabeth Marsh, ss. and costs. John Garritty, ' Golborne, was fined 10s. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly, and doing wilful daruage.—For creating a breach of the peace at Haydock, Mary Ralston was bound over for three months.—Edward Occlesham was similarly dealt with for fighting at Golborne.—For failing to send his child regularly to I school, Oliver Beresford was fined 4s.—Peter Jones, Golborne, was summoned for deserting his wife, Harriet Jones, on March 28th, and was ordered to pay his wife Gs. a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>VULCAN INSTITUTE MOTHERS' UNION.</head>
        <p>VULCAN INSTITUTE MOTHERS' UNION.</p>
        <p>Nurse Heaslett, a Church Army nurse, who has come to work at the Vulcan end of the township of Earles- town, commenced her duties a few days ago. Last Thursday afternoon she was introduced to the mothers at the Vulcan Institute, at a reception tea, when the nurse was accorded a very hearty welcome. Among those present were Mrs. Collingwood, who is the chief mover in the matter, and the Rector and Mrs. Carson. About 150 attended. The Rector introduced Nurse Heaslett, and Captain Haslarn, of St. John's Mission, Earlestown, referred to the work in the district. Mrs. Collingwood also addressed the tubthers. Tea was afterwards served under the direction of Mrs. Collingwood and other lady helpers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>– PRESENTATION TO MRS. BURGESS.</head>
        <p>- PRESENTATION TO MRS. BURGESS.</p>
        <p>Last week a presentation was made to Mrs. George Burgess at the Earlestown Wesleyan Lecture Hall, as a mark deflection and esteem on her severing her long connection with the Women's Bible Class, due to her removal to Southport. Tea was provided.—The Rev. S. C. Hall, who made the presentation—a silver cake basket—spoke of many of Mrs. Burgess's good qualities, what a splendid teacher she had been, and how admir- ably she had built up a large women's class.—Mr. Burgess and Mr. Howarth also spoke, and Mrs. Burgess in her reply expressed her heartfelt thanks and her great sorrow at leaving. The class had been a great pleasure and delight to her, for she was only too glad to do anything she could in that direction; and it was one of the things she most missed on leaving Earlestown.</p>
        <p>EARLESPOWN WORKS FATACITT. Mr. S. Brighouse, city coroner, inquired at &amp;tries- town Town Hall, on Wednesday into the circumstances concerning the death, on Saturday morning, of James Sankey, forty-four, a labourer, employed at the Sankey Sugar Works, Earlestown. The evidence was to the effect that" fly-shunting operations had been in pro- gress near the boiler house, and also that a test of the fire apparatus was to be made, and it was assumed that deceased went between the waggons to escape being drenched or to support himself during a bout of giddi- ness. The post-mortem examination disclosed injuries in the plevic region, and the inference was drawn that Sankey had been caught between the draw-bar hooks. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and attached no blame to anyone. Ar. Draper, for the company, stated that that was the first fatal accident since the works were started, fifty-three years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Church. On Sunday evening Dr. Stainer's " The Crucifixion "—a meditation on the Sacred Passion of the Holy Redeemer—was given at this church by the choir. The interpretation throughout vras most creditable and fully in keeping with the beauty and solemnity of the work. The siiloists were Messrs. A. Griffiths and P. 11-vii. who sing with much taste and expression. Mr. IV. il. Payton ably presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Ellesmare-stract Wesleyan Mission.</head>
        <p>Ellesmare-stract Wesleyan Mission.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the above place of worship was celebrated on Sunday, special services being held morn- ing and evening. 'Die morning service was conducted by Mr. G. Davey-Smith, who preached an impressive -sermon on the words ; " Fornow we see through a glass, darkly ; but then face to face "(1 Cor. xiii. 12). The choir rendered the the anthem Cry out and shout." At the evening service the Rev. T. Miller was the preacher and delivered a helpful discourse on "Church life," taking as his test Phil. iv., ver. 1,2, 3. Mr. Levi Richardson's excellent voice was heard to advantage in the solos " Then shall the righteous shine " and "There is a green hill." The choir sing the anthem " Bow down Thine (AZ." At the close of the service the Sacrament of the Lord's Supper was administered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary was celebrated at the above Church on Sunday. The preacher for the day was Mr. W. R. Reed, a student at the Hartley Primitive Methodist College, Manchester, who preached powerful and interesting discourses. The children of the school, who had been ably trained by the choirmaster, Mr, A. Spruce, sang special hymns ins delightful wanner, aided by the choir. On Monday evening a tea party was given in the schoolroom, when a fair number attended. Afterwards a meeting and prize distribution took place in the Church. Councillor Hawthorn presided and was supported by Mr. J. Rowlands and the Rev. J. F. Matthews. . The Cif AIRM.kN said he was sure all present had some ' sympathy with thet Sunday school. The Sunday school . was the most important link in the Free Churches, and ' he thought it was necessary for the children to be well grounded in tho tenets of their faith. (Applause.) The Rev. J. F. MATTIiEws took for his subject" The child and the school." He said the increased attention given to child training had called forth a new type of school. The buildings now erected by the Free Churches were the finest of their kind in the world. Though they had neither taxation grants nor rates upon which to fall back, their Sunday schools compared inure than favourably with the Anglican and ttoman Catholic schools. Their attention to material equipment and grading for purposes of tuition was becoming a tine art. While their object was doubtless to claim the children for their respective communions, their immedi- ate purpose, beside which others we le insignificant, was to claim the whole childhood for,C rist. Mr. Matthews said that the men who sat on their Labour and Liberal benches in the Local Debating Society had mostly received their training in Nonconformist Sunday schools. lie urged the whole Church membership to directly claim an interest in the school. To meet the children on au anniversary was not enough. The closer the relation of the Church with the teacher, the fawily life with the school life, the timer and the more abiding would be the results of their united work. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. ROWLANDS distributed the prizes to the echolar's, offering a prize of a ss.book at the end of the school year to the boy who secured the largest number of new scholars from among those who attend no other school. Mr. B. T. PARKES, the superintendent of the Sunday school, then presented Mr. J. Bissell with a framed diploma, which had been signed by the President and Secretary of the P.M. Sunday School Union in recog- nition of his 40 years' service in connection with that church, It read :—" Presented to Joseph Bissell, of Legh-street Sunday School, in recognition of 40 years' devoted and useful service in the Sunday school." Mr. Parkes eulogized Mr. Bissell's work, and said no one could accuse him of being unfaithful to his church. He had never known him to be once late, and his punctuality was an example to all the teachers of the , Sabbath school. (Applause.) ' Mr. BISSELL, in responding, thanked the teachers for the framed diploma, and said that although he had been engaged in Sunday school work for (12 years he was not yet tired of it. (Applause.) During the evening the children sang anniversary hymns and the Misses Jessie Woodland and Hilda Spruce sang sweetly the duet" Kind words can never die."</p>
