oice been given hies At first she seemed but certain that the theory of the great scientist had a visionary creature with wistful eyes who was been practically proved, and that the thought shy of revealing herself; but with alluring capri- vibrations of his brain had, under favourable cir- ciousness and ever-increasing distinctness she curnstances, been able to focus and reflect theni- appeared to his fancy, fascinating him and stirring selves on the sensitised plate. However, his belief his heart as it never before bad been stirred by was too sacred and too personal to be revealed to mortal woman. For weeks he fell a willing victim another, and, therefore, when his friend asked him to her siren spell, living as much as possible apart what he thought of it, he replied that it was from relatives and friends that he might have time wonderful, begged that he mie,lit hare a copy of the to commune with her, awaiting her appearance with print., and hoped that the negative might be kept strange anxiety, feeling strangely happy in her instead of being returned to its seller. phantasmal presehce, and painfully lonely at her "Oh, I'll do that if you like, and keep it as a disappearances, which his imagination appeared curiosity," agreed Drummond. "But, at the same unable to prevent. Itime, I think the manufacturers ought to be more Suddenly lie awoke to the absurdity of being careful and not mix up an old plate with new tines en-laved by his fancy, and the practical part like this." of his nature assured him that in that direction "That is your explanation ?" said Alec. madness lay. Then, like a man who resolves to "Of course it is. How otherwse could that face break.himself from linbits of opium smoking, he appear on the plate ? " determined to abandon his day dreams snd remain A fortnight later Robert Cameron was well master of his own mind. To accomplish this he enough to attend business as usual, a fact that busied himself over his office work all day, sought brought, Alec great relief. The day on which the the companionship of old comrades in the evening, old man first returned to NV ark became for ever or took his cousin to theatres or concerts, and by memorable to his son. every means in his power sought to banish the The latter had been feeling unusually cheerful intruder who had so mysteriously captivated him, that morning, and, seated at his desk in his private The agonies of tke suddenly converted opium office, was mapping out a tour for his holidays smoker were his ; but. .with all the strength of his abroad, when one of the clerks interrupted him to mind he persistently turned from that alluring ' say that a young lady, who gave her name as Miss presence which he felt was always awaiting him, I Mac Neil, particularly wished to see him. Alec at hardening his heart to its appeal for affection, ; once concluded that his visitor must be the doctor's erasing from his mind the memory of those hours , sister, whom he had never seen, though he had of quiet happiness spent in its companionship until . frequently beard Madge speak of her. Though he felt. unnerved by the strain and absolutely surprised that she called on him, lie immediately . .. . .</p>
        <p>iniserable.</p>
        <p>ask.ed the clerk to shew her in.</p>
        <p>Then 'came a sudden renction, an irresistible longing, and the creators of his day dreams with her wistful eyes and reproachful smile was before him once more, and unwilling as it seemed to part from him, for she was now continually with hint during his buminess hours, when his absent-minded manner was commented on by the clerks, and the mistakes he repeatedly made in the affairs of which he had special charge, became common cccur.-ences. On these being pointed out to hint by an elderly and privileged book-keeper, remarkable for his acid smile and orange-coloured wig, Alec honestly expressed his surprise as to how they could have happened. In reply the privileged person tapped his snuff-box, helped hill self liberally to its con- tents, and suggested that a holiday might be Advisable. But Alec, resenting this, declared he was in no need of relaxation at present, and secretly resolved to keep his imagination more in check during business hours in future. Meanwhile Madge neither resented nor appeared to notice his neglect of herself, for now that his whim to take her to theatres and concerts had worn itself out she no longer received any attention from him. She, however, noticed what his self-absorption had failed to see, that his father had been looking ill for some time past. It was therefore a surprise to Alec when the old man failed to come down to breakfast for the first tints in the memory at Lis son. Both he and Madge were alarmed, and imme- diately sent for the family doctor. Their summons was responded to by a young medical man named Mac Neil, who stated that he was acting as locum towns for Dr. Gale, who I .td gone on his summer holidays. Mac Neil's quiet, self-assured manner, kindtv blue eves and decisive voice made a favour.</p>
        <p>He was bending above an opell drawer with his back to the door when she entered, and, on turn ng quickly round, remained perfectly still, his e 3 es fixed on her in amazement, his lips parted, his tace blanched as if he had seen a ghost, for before him stood the woman of his day dreams, the same whose photogravb was then lying close to his heart. She seemed too TlllOl confused to notice anything unusual in his appearance or manner, and lie arts too bewildered by the mystery and wonder of seeing the materialised form of his visions enter his office to understand what she said. His heart swelled from force of the love he bore her whom he seemed to have known for ages, who he felt was, in some incomprehensible way, destined for hint from the worid's grey beginning. He longed to draw near and clasp her to himself, and had advanced a step or two when the recollections of Madge rose before him as an insurmountable barrier. He was not free to act as his feelings dictated; he was irrecoverably bound to another woman, and without wronging her and bringing dishonour on himself, he could not break their engagement. With a look of despair he turned away, but the words he caught made him face his visitor ORO more. "Believe me, ihe was saying in a voice he thought was the sweetest that ever came from woman's lips. "I Am very, very sorry. I don't think she would have done it if she knew you would feel it so touch ; but she believed you really never loved her, and that you only agreed to marry her because your father wished it." "Who ?" he gasped, his astonishment increasing. "Madge. She said she could never marry any man but my brother, Dr. Mac Neil, and he worshipped her. I never saw anvthing like it." the</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Kays compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp; Colds.--Advt</p>
