      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Examiner, S</head>
        <p>The Examiner, S</p>
        <p>visitor remarked, with a smile that made her grave face irresistibly winning. "But—"</p>
        <p>"Don't be too angry with her. She knew her :mole would never consent to her marrying anyone but you; so she and my brother got married quite privately this morning and have just started-on their honeymoon." "Madge—is Madge married ?" he gasped, in amazement. ,</p>
        <p>"She is. You would laugh if you saw how frightened she was. She thought wild uncles would come and tear her from her bridegroom, and she didn't feel quite safe 'intik the tram steamed away with her out of the station. Then, by her wish, I came here to tell you about her marriage and to ask you to break the news to your father. Here is a letter she wrote asking his forgiveness. Will you please give it to him ? " In taking it from her he clasped her hand and held it in his own whilst he looked into her wistful eyes, in which he fancied he saw an expression of new-born wonder and joy. "Madge was right.," he said. "I never loved her. I have only loved one woman in my life, and that woman is yourself." "Me?" she gasped, pulling away het hand. "You," he replied, gravely. "And I never even knew that you saw me. My brother pointed you out to me one morning as you passed our house, and then I used to watch for you eve', day as you went to your office; and sometimes in the afternoor. I met you in the streets as you returned, but you never seemed to look in my direction, and I thought you were full of your business and cared for nothing else." "That explains much. I suppose I must have seen you all the time," he said. "I think we must have been playing hide-and- seek," she remarked, laughingly. "But now that we have found each other—"</p>
        <p>"I must be going," she interrupted, as she hurried towards the door, where she paused a second, glanced over her shoulder with a look of surprise and pleasure, then burst into a merry laugh and vanished before he could reach her. As for Alec ho stood looking after her for some seconds, scarcely able to realise that this visit was not one of those fantastic episodes that happen only in dreamland. Then, picking up the letter which had fallen to the ground, be heaved a sigh of relief as if the burden of the world were removed from his shoulders, and sat down to master the thoughts that whirled round his brain as if darxing in delight at the prospect of his independence and his assured happiness. Half-an-hour later he had gone into his father's private office, after having given orders that they were not to be interrupted. Seated in his well- worn leather chair, the old man was busy with his ledgers, and looked up as if resenting interrup- tion. "Well, what is it, Alec ?" he asked, abruptly. "Father, I have some surprising news for you, Alec began. "About the assurance company?" "No, nothing about business, but about—" "Then leave it until after business lufurs." "I would rather tell you now. It's about Madge and inpelf." "You want to be married sooner than the date I Exed ? "</p>
        <p>"On the contrary, we don't want to marry each other at all." _ _</p>
        <p>_ "What stuff and nonsense is this ?" his father asked, closing the book before him with a bang and glancing angrily at his son. "Madge found out long ago that I did not really love her, and when she discovered a man that did, she decided to marry him." "She—decided? I will never consent!" the old man thundered. _ .</p>
        <p>"She knew that, and lest you might hinder her she got married this morning, and has already started on her honeymoon." Robert Cameron stared in blank amazement; then in a choking voice asked who was the bride- groom. When told he poured out a volley of abuse on Dr. MacNeiVs head, accused him of treachery, and robbery, and fortune-hunting, and many bad qualities. Nor was Madge spared, for she had dared to thwart his long-laid scheme, to defy his authority, and his pride suffei ed. Finally he turned on Alec and called him a poor chicken- hearted creature to allow another man to rob him el such a girl as Madge. Knowing the wisdom of silence Alec left him, having first placed her letter on his desk. On ret tuning about an hour later, he found his father in a melancholy mood, the letter in his hands, his eyes red from unaccustomed tears, and his face more softened than his son had ever seen it before. The younger man put one arm round his father's shoulders affectionately, and neither spoke for some titre.</p>
        <p>At last the old man, looking up with a twinkle in his eye. said: "Aye, but I have been a fool , for when Mac Neil came to sit with me by the hour, I just thought 'twos because he liked my company nnd wished to hear me tell stories of Dumfries, and now I know 'twas because that sly little mini was there. I don't blame him, for she was a win- some lass. I mind how she used to step out after him when he left my room, and I wed to hear theni whisper on the stairs, hut I thought 'twos all about my ailments, and I believe the fellow under- stood me well. I Euppaao I'll have to forgive them , and if he settles down here I'll always have a doctor who is a relative to look after me. There's some satisfaction in that," he said, nodding his head.'</p>
        <p>"True, true," Alec ad, humouring him.• "But we shall miss Madge. She had a bright, bonny face like her mother. Aye, we'll miss her much. The house will be main lonely with only two solitary men folk." "But not for long, father," Alec ventured to say. "Why, lad? Do you mean that you--" .</p>
        <p>- "I have found the girl I am anxious to make my wife, and I think she will listen to me when-- "You haven't asked her yet ?" " '</p>
        <p>"I wished to tell you first." "Aye, lad, I'll not interfere any more," the old man said, sadly, "for young people know best what suits tbetreelves ; and if you love this lass and she is worthy of you, why, ask her to marry you, always provided,' he added with enfphasis, 'that. she has the fortune you might expect with TOM. wife.' "I don't know if she has a penny," said Alec. I "What, man! Do you mean to tell me you let her snare yon before you knew what you should have with her ?"</p>
        <p>"She has not snared me, father. She didn't even 'inspect I loved her until 1 told her. "Aye, that's.what they all say," replied the old men, cynically. "And, besides, money is not everything." "People that haven't got any console themselves by thinking so. See what a canny man -MacNeil was. He knew Madge had a tidy fortune, and now it's gone clean out of the family." - "You married a wife who hadn't a penny," said Alec, boldly. , "Ah, lad, ,but such a woman; there never was her like," answered Robert Cameron, as a softer light came into his eyes. "What my mother was to, you, this girl is to me."</p>
        <p>"If that is so, lad, I'll say no word to hinder your wedding her," said the old man, in whom old memories were stirring. Alec grasped his hand in silence, and then. leafing the ottice,went on love's pilgrimage through the crowded streets to return home at sunset with his heart stored with -joy; for he had won the woman he loved. _</p>
        <p>LTIIE END.</p>
      </div>
