      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>turday April 18, 1908.</head>
        <p>turday April 18, 1908.</p>
        <p>HOW IMPORTS ARE PAID FOR.</p>
        <p>THE CASE IN 1907.</p>
        <p>The Magnificent Total of Our Oversea</p>
        <p>Transactions.</p>
        <p>By L. G. Mazza Money, M.P.</p>
        <p>ARTICLE VIII.</p>
        <p>Great play is still made by Protectionist orators upon what they are pleased to term our " excess of imports." In 1907 our imports amounted to £646,000,000, While our exports of British produce came to £426,000,000, and our exports 4 imported goods came to £92,000,000. Therefore our imports exceeded our exports in 1907 by £128,000,000. The ' tariff reformer still believes this balance to be "loss." Not in the House of Commons, it is true, but in the constituencies it is still represented that we are. paying out money or securities in payment for the foreigner's dumping.. Ido not know if it is still issued, but the Tariff Reform League's leaflet No. 20 issued in 1903 distinctly asserted that our excess of imports amounted to a "balance against us." It deducted 1903 exports from 1903 imports and termed the difference an " adverse balance." The leaflet is headed " This Hill is Dangerous !" That is the sort of "argument " which is fired off in the constituencies, and unnecessary as the task ought to be, Free Traders must be vigilant in reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Case as to Gold.</head>
        <p>The Case as to Gold.</p>
        <p>On this head, the crudest statement which is still met with, is that we pay for the excess of imports in gold. Let us clear up this point first of all. The Custom House keeps record of our imports and exports of gold and for 1907 the record is as follows:</p>
        <p>IMPORTS AND EXPORTS OF GOLD AND SILVER. (WHETHER IN BAR OR IN COIN) IN 1907.</p>
        <p>Imports Exports</p>
        <p>Excess of Imports</p>
        <p>So that in 1907, as is usually the case, we imported more of the precious metals than we exported. The assertion that we ply for imp-irts in gold or money thus falls to the ground.</p>
        <p>What of Ftoreign and eolonial Investments,</p>
        <p>Other Protectionists admit that we import an excess of gold, but assert that our imports are largely paid for by securities going out of the country. On this Ihead we are easily able to show that our lien upon, foreign countries and British colonies is not decreasing but increasing. The Inland Revenue Commissioners show on page 217 of their report for 1907 that in the financial year ended March 31st, 1906 we received from abroad a definite sum of £73,890,265 as interest on Indian Government stocks and railways, foreign and colonial Government securities, and foreign and colonial railways and other investments, as against £60,331,525 in 1901. This shows very clearly that we are not selling out our oversee investments to pay for imports. Moreover the £73,899,000 is only that part of the interest from abroad which can be earmarked hy Somerset House. The annual sum due to us is much larger, probably as much as £100,000,050 altogether. But to leave out of account the indefinite part, the definite £73,899,000 of 905-6 is clear proof that oversea investments have not decreased. It is as untrue that we pay for imports in securities as that we pay for them in gold. Having cleared up these two fallacies of the Pro- tectionist, let us pass to the true explanation of our excess of imports. Part of it is given in the last aragra ph. There is due tons every year a sum of not less than £100,000,000 in respect of our foreign, Indian and Colonial investments. This sum either caches us as actual imports or is left abroad for re- nvestrnent. It is due and payable. and if remitted the remittances take the form of imparts of load, materials, or manufactures.</p>
        <p>Half the World's Ships are Ours. The other great factor in the excess of imports is the British mercantile marine, as to which Protec- tionists are so curiously silent. The fact that we possess more than one half the world's tonnage does not Appeal to them. To repeat the fact is to reduce to absurdity the tales of ruin. To possess half the world's ships is to possess a great earning power. Freights are really exports of services, and they are paid for in goods. The services, being invisible, do not swell our exports, while the payment for the services, being visible, increases our imports. The Board of Trade in 1903 calculated our net freight earnings at about £00,000,000 per annum. Our ships can hardly earn less than this. Here, then, we have a further .£90,000,030 of earnings receivable in a year, and if remitted they must take the form of imports.</p>
        <p>Already we have accounted for £100,010,000 of services rendered to persons abroad, but we have by no means completed the list. There are such items as banking services (we are the greatest international bankers in the world), commissions earned in the foreign trade, retriittances of pay, the Indian "home charges " Arc., whicti taken together cannot amount to lees than £25,000,000 a year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Our Invisible Imports.</head>
        <p>Our Invisible Imports.</p>
        <p>Against these big items for services rendered to persons abroad (invisible exports) there are, of course, similar items relating to services rendered to us by persons abroad (invi.qible imports), but the latter are very small when compared with the former. We have to pay oat exrorts for use of foreign ships, for interest on foreign investments here, and so forth, but these items do not bulk for much. Altogether, they prob- ably do not account for as much as the miscellaneous items on the other side for which I have named £25,000,000 as a conservative estimate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
