      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BILL CLIFF 6 BAPTIST SUNDAY SCHOOL—The anniversary services were held on Sunday, the preacher' being the Rev. George Evans, of Baptist Collee, Manchester. Special hymns were sung, the soloist being Miss Amy Whalley. In the afternoon a children's set vice was held, under the presidenov of Mr. Drewitt. The Chairman briefly addressed the children, after which Miss Lees, of Warrington, pave an instructive address which was listened to with great interest. Special hymns were sung and solos were well rendered by Miss IVhalley and Mr. Foster. The collections throughout the day were in aid of the Sunday school funds and satisfactory sums were realized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>DEAF MUTE'S SAD END</head>
        <p>DEAF MUTE'S SAD END</p>
        <p>Knocked Down by a Motor Cycle.</p>
        <p>An inquest was conducted by 'Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, at the Police Station on Wednesday evening, concerning the death of Marth Mnddocks, of no settled abode. Mary McEvoy, 4, .Tolley's-yard, said the deceased, who was completely deaf and dumb, used to lodge with her. Nobody knew her name. but she was called " Dummy." She used to go to her friends, who were always willing to help her. She would be aboutseventy years of age. The witness lest saw the deceased alive on Sunday morning at the Infirmary, but of course she could not give her any account of the occident. Nurse Griffiths' Infirmary, said the deceased entered the Infirmary on Sunday evening, the sth of the present month and died the following Kunday. Her thigh was fractured. George Charles Baxendale, Atherton, said he was riding a motor bicycle near linutsford-road on the sth Inst. He was going at about ten miles an hour. He saw the woman in the road and sounded his horn. She saw him and she stepped into the road again and be knocked her down, The machine did not go over her, but he fell on top of her. He at once gave his assist- ance and sent a friend for the doctor. Thrid there was no bln,-</p>
        <p>no Coroner sal. .10 blame attached to the witness as there was no evidence to prove that he was driving furiously. Florence Pemberton, 187, Knutsford-rond, said she saw the accident, and said that witness was driving his machine slowly. His statement was correct. Sergeant Stuart said he bad known the woman for twentylears. He had made full inquiries and he was not sure whether she bad been married or not. The driver was not to blame.</p>
        <p>The jury after a moment's consideration returned a verdict of "Accidental death," no blame being attached to the driver.</p>
      </div>
