      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday April 18, 1908. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday April 18, 1908. SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>CITRIC ACID AS STERILISER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Riegel finds that the acid of ,oranges, lemons, and limes is an efficient destroyer of the microbes of typhus, cliarrho3a, and cholera. In the Arc/tie fur Hygiene he gives an account of his experiments. A. soltition of three-fifths per cent. of citric acid, and 5 per cent. of cane sugar was mixed with water containing cholera; 'diarrhoea, and typhus germs. The first were killed in fifteen minutes, the second in from five to six heurs, and the third in twenty-four hours. Win posed to the sun's rays the times were I'3- duced to five minutes and one and a-half, hours for cholera and typhus germs.</p>
        <p>NEW APPARATUS FOR BAD AIR.</p>
        <p>The apparatus devised by Dr. Tissot for those who have to work in irespirable atm). spheres seems to possess important advan. tages over earlier forms, says a writer in the Globe. None of these latter allowed the wearer to remain more than an hour in the noxious atmosphere, while the new appa. ratus gives a period of four hours. The box, which is carried on the back, weighs 281 b., but the weight is so well distributed that it is not 'much of a burden. It contains com- pressed oxygen, and the ' breathing is by tubes fitted to the nostrils. The carbonic acid-gas of respiration is absorbed by a solu- tion of caustic potash. There are no valves, and the in-going and out-going airs are kept separate. To demonstrate the efficacy of his apparatus Dr. Tissot remained with it four hours in an atmosphere of coal-gas. Armed with it a man was able to work for five hours, and found no difficulty in breathing. The position of the box on the back is such that the workman can crawl, use a pick, and, in fact, do almost any kind of work.</p>
        <p>CITRIC ACID AS WATER STERILISER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Riegel, of the Austrian Army, de- scribes in Are/hr fuer Hygiene a number of experiments with • citric acid to determine its value as a steriliser of water. The experi- ments were made with typhus, diarrhoea, and cholera bacilli. A solution of 0.6 per cent, of citric acid and 5 per cent, of cane sugar in water was placed in shallow vessels. The cholera germs were killed in fifteen minutes; the diarrhoea bacilli were killed in five to six hours, and the typhus in twenty-four hours. When the vessels were placed in the sun's rays, the action was much quicker; the cholera germs were killed in five minutes, and the typhus in one and a-half hour. It is therefore probable, says Dr. Riegel, that the use of citric acid in water exposed to the rays of the sun in flat vessels would be of great benefit in countries where the usual sterilisation methods (cooking, ozonisation) cannot be well employed, as is the ease in most of the tropical and sub-tropical countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS ETHER.</head>
        <p>THE MYSTERIOUS ETHER.</p>
        <p>In the course of a lecture at the London Institution, Mr. Frederick Hovenden, vice president of the institution, said he had ex- , , perimentally shewn that there was a fluid, in- visible to the human eye, permeating every- thing terrestrial, always in differential motion, which, acting on moleculee,. caused them to assume solid, fluid, gaseous, or in- candescent form. The fluid, ether, was omni- present, regulating all life. • Every organic being, including man, was governed by tha flow of 'ether, and when man understood that assertion a new order of thought would arise which would materially add to the happiness of mankind.</p>
        <p>WHERE THE SWALLOW• IS RESIDENT.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to learn that the chimney swallow (hirundo rustiea), in 'Which the migratory instinct is so st/rongly marked, may yet be a permanent resident in certain districts. This is the ease, according to Major Magrath, in the Bunnu district of the North-West Frontier Province. This region is a plain, the lowest part of which is 000 ft. above sea-level, and it is bordered by hills. The extremes of summer and winter tempera- tures are great, ranging from a maximum of 125 deg. Fehr.. to a minimum of 30deg. Fahr., both in the shade. Here our summer bird of passage is content to remain the year through, building in the niud hovels of Lb* 'villages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>ARMY OF THE BIRDS.</head>
        <p>THE ARMY OF THE BIRDS.</p>
        <p>President Roosevelt has • said some striking things about the work of the Biological Sur- vey. It ha,s shewn, he says, that at least forty-three species of birds prey upon the cottonboll-weevil; that fifty-seven species feed upon scale insects, which attack fruit, trees ;, that cuckoos and orioles destroy leaf- eating caterpillars; that hawks and owls, ex- cept a fe* which attack poultry and game. birds, are benefactors; in destroying grass- hoppers and mice; and that "woodpeckers, as a class, by destroying the larvte of wood- boring insects, are so essential to tree life that it is doubtful if our forests could exist. without them:" The plumage of these birds should be admired and respected as the uniform of a winged army which defends man against some of his worst foes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>REPTILIAN PECULIARITIES.</head>
        <p>REPTILIAN PECULIARITIES.</p>
        <p>In the Biologisches Centralblatt, Dr. Wer- ner, of Vienna, has published the results of a fioniewhat unusual series of observations upon reptiles and amphibians, members of the animal kingdom of which comparatively little is known. Both amphibians and reptiles are strongly attracted by water, even in dis- tances too great for it to be detected through any sense known to us, and the writer ex- plains it as a bort of chemical attraction. Sight, perhaps, is the most acute of the senses in both reptiles and °amphibians. Alli- gators and crocodiles see best sideways, and can also see backward at an angle of 40deg. These are the only ones of both varieties which can hear, and then only to a slight de- gree. Boa-constrictors are entirely deaf. Taste is not lacking in any of these animals. The snake has a sense of . taste sufficiently delicate to distinguish between different spe- cies of snakes. The snake's tongue also pre- sents the only remarkable point observed with regard to the sense of touch, either in reptiles or amphibians. By means of this organ e.:) snake doubtless obtains knowledge of sin rounding objects which it has never touched. The tongue is moved rapidly up and de'vn. and apparently the air vibrations pro- duced strike against objects and are reflected back from them in such a way as to give an idea of theix position.</p>
        <p>EARTHQUAKES AND MOUNTAIN FORMATION.</p>
        <p>In the "Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society" Professor T. J. J. See, U.S.N., the astronomer, has published a paper on "The new theory of earthquakes and mountain formation, as illustrated 'by processes now at work in the depths of the sea." In it he gives further evidence in sup- port of his proposition that mountains are formed by the action of the sea, and not at all by the secular cooling and shrinkage of the earth. The old theory which compares the earth to a withered apple is pronounced unsound. In previous papers Professor See had argued that six great classes of phenomena, namely, earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain formation, the formation of islands and pl•ateaux, seismic sea waves following great earthquakes, • and the feeble attraction of mountains long noticed in geodesy, are but different effects of one common cause—the secular leakage of the ocean bottom. This forms steam beneath the earth's crust, which brings on earthquakes, and the earthquakes uplift islands in the sea and mountains along the shore by the expulsion of lava under the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>SEISMIC SEA WAVES.</head>
        <p>SEISMIC SEA WAVES.</p>
        <p>When the earth's crust is thus uplifted along the seashore mountains are produced, and some of the mountains break into erup- tion and form volcanoes. The expulsion of lava from under the sea undermines trenches in the sea bottom, and the crust goes down and forms the deeps often noticed near the land. The sinking of the sea bottom after an earth- quake produces the seismic sea wave so often following great earthquakes. Thus the uplift of the coast into moun- tains and the sinking of the adjacent sea bottom into a trench is the effect of the ex- pulsion of lava from under the bed of the sea. This expulsion is due to steam, which forms under the sea but not under the land, and Ilene.) when the steam pressure becomes very great it pushes out under the land and uplifts the coast. In 1899 the coast near Yakutat Bay. Alaska, mils uplifted by a great earthquake for 100 miles, and at the maxi- mum the rise was 47ft. This shews that a vast quantity of lava was expelled from under the bed of . the ocean and pushed under the land.</p>
        <p>TIIE EARTH'S EXPANSION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WHY MAN CANNOT FLY.</head>
        <p>WHY MAN CANNOT FLY.</p>
        <p>Flight as a personal matter can never be attempted by man, for the plain reason that he is not • provided with a flying body, writes Dr. Andrew Wilson. A near neighbour quad- ruped of his, the bat, has evolved flying powers, but it has developed a frame which, like that of the bird, is made for flight as its true means of locomotion. Its bones are filled with air, and otherwise it has points which render its aerial trips, not so extensive •as those of the bird, easily performed. In the bird, it is simply the whole arm or forelimb which. is modified in the wing. and it is the actual movement of this feathered arm which propels its possessor through the air. But the bat's flight is of a different kind. It calls to its aid a skinfold which stretches be- tween the four enormously elongated fingers, and runs between forelimbs and hindhmbs and between hindlimbs and tail. In the bat, therefore, we have something of the boat's sail order of things added. to the wing, as ' op- posed to the movements of the wing, pure and simple, in the bird.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>AGE OF NIAGAIIA.</head>
        <p>THE AGE OF NIAGAIIA.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. W. Spencer,' who has given some interesting data in reepect of the "Ape of the Niagara Falls," was engaged in investi- gations for a monograph on the Falls, to be published' by the Geological Survey of Canada. The mean recession of the crest. line of the Falls he found to be 4.2 ft. a-year under existing conditions; and this rate has approximately obtained for =7 years. But this rate, remarks Know/edge, will not give the age of the Falls, on account of fewer great variations in the volume of the river and the height of the Falls themselves. The chief change in volume of water depends on the fact that originally Lake Erie alone was discharged ovee the Falls, when the supply of water was only one-fifteenth of the pre- ' sent discharge. Above Foster's Flat the sudden widening indicates the inflow of we other lakes into Erie, greater water dis- charge, and greatly-increased rapidity of re- cession. The Whirlpool ,is on the site where the recession broke down the partition sepa- rating the head of the Whirlpool-St. David's buried gorge, and began to empty out the contents of this valley. The cutting with the full 'power of the water of the four lakes varied at times according to the height ot ,the fall, and is calculated to have occupied 3,509 years. Thus the entire age of the Falls is given as 39,000 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>POISONING PLATINUM.</head>
        <p>POISONING PLATINUM.</p>
        <p>Platinum, for example, in the colloidal state is capable of decomposing 1,000,000 times its weight of hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, and then of remaining as strong and as active as ever. Perhaps the most remarkable fact in connection with the extraordinary " vitality " of colloidal plati- num is that its energies are at once paralysed by such ordinary animal poisons as prussic acid, corrosive sublimate, or 6ulphuretted hydrogen. The platinum may thus be said to be poisoned, and such a small quantity as one-millionth of a . grain Of prussic acid is sufficient to prevent this great transforming power. To give another example of the de- cided effect of minute traces of various sub- stances, it has been found that certain water organisms are destroyed in water contained in a copper vessel, and yet the quantity .of copper present is only one tart in a thousand million parts of water. Such effects, .the Lancet adds, are impres- Isive, and they are calculated to impress us still mote when we. contemplate the number of processes going on in the human machine which are dependent upon the action of small things.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>OPTICIANS AND SEA SIGNALLING.</head>
        <p>OPTICIANS AND SEA SIGNALLING.</p>
        <p>There is probably no other department of life in which the instruments of observation and of prevision furnished by opticians are in greater .n.,mand than in the Navy and the mercantile marine. This fact itself, con- sidered frqm the optician's point of view, lends considerable interest to the sea-going profession; but there are other special pounds also upon which our readers, says the Optician, may profitably turn their attend tion to the requirements of the shipping world. Although the great outward and visi- ble cign of the change that is going on in our trade is the extension and development of sight-testing, yet seeing that this has its root in diffusion of a</p>
        <p>KNON.EDGE OF TECHNICAL OPTICS, we believe there are other modifications of the optician's practice to be traced directly to .the same cause. For instance, the line of demarcation be. tween the purely marine opticians and others in the seaport towns appears to be losing its dlstinctness. The marine optician may not undertake sight-testing, but he does find that his customers expect an understanding of the use and application of corrective lenses on his part. Converisely, the different types of nautical apparatus are not only more widely sold but much more generally understood. Marine sign:fling, which is so vital a mat- ter for the safety of shipping, requires nor telescopes, but various other instru- .iiente that are the product of the optician's art; and there is still great scope for the exercise of optical ingenuity, in the improve- ment of methods now in use. The air, ether, and water, have all been turned to account for transmission purposes, the electric wave and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>IrNDEG-WATER •SOUND SIGNAL</head>
        <p>IrNDEG-WATER •SOUND SIGNAL</p>
        <p>being del elopments of the use of lamp and foghorn. • Further, it would seem that there are still tettions in the spectrum that await being tut n.:d . to valuable account in this sense, so soon as the necessary optical de- Vices can be produced for dealing with them. Thus the immediate infra-red region of , the ,peetruni presents a field that, it is thought, :!itly be exploited for. the purpose of signal- itng through heavy mi‘t or fog; and as this form of radiation woilld be far more re- than the so-called' electric waves. it follows that the direction from which such signals were received could be determined, which would be a very great advantages</p>
      </div>
