      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 18, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 18, 1908.</p>
        <p>their tenants or managers; do they compensate such for this goodwill? I have never heard of this being done. Does Mr. Rylands suggest that the State should at the end of this time limit acknowledge what the brewing companies feel under no obligation to recognize in the case of their own employees? or if they do it is a gratuity of which no claim in right is recognized. But why labour the minor points? The trade knows, if Mr. Rylands does not, that it is the monopoly value its members are fighting for. This is the basis of the calculation for the " confiscated millions." They clearly recognize that the State's claim to what it has itself produced is the cardinal prin- ciple of this Bill and they are fighting this claim with all the material weapons, some or them of a very questionable character, which an enormously wealthy trade can forge. But there are ample signs that the country is thoroughly roused,and has determined to hold its own, and will not be intimidated by a prodigal display of posters, dark green tickets and bluster. Mr. Rylands' points seem hardly worth discussing; it is like dealing with pence when pounds are involved. The minor questions of counters, brass handles,pint measure, and tenants goodwill (apart from monopoly value) all these could be easily dealt with in Committee, and the tenant could, as is already done in many cases, cover his risk by insurance. CHARLES E. PARKER. l'enketh, April 11th, 1908. • Rev. F. A. Screeton and Brewers'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Subscriptions.</head>
        <p>Subscriptions.</p>
        <p>The "Hyde Reporter" publishes an interesting interview on the licensing question with the Rev. F. A. bcreeton, Vicar of St. Thomas's Church, Hyde, and formerly Vicar of Christ Church, Latchford. Referring to the Bishop of Chester's statement that he had been informed if the bill became law, impoverished brewers' would be compelled to cut down their sub- scriptions to church objects, Mr. Screeton said: " I think one's attitude should not be daunted in the least degree by any attempted threats of the kind. On the other hand they would stiffen one's back." Referring to the further expression of the Bishop that these intimations were, of course, not meant as threats, but merely as instructive and timely admonitions, the rev, gentleman, with a smile, remarked that he thought his Lordship meant it in a satirical sense. Proceeding, he said : " But I can imagine some churches which it would affect very seriously, and considering the difficulty of raising money -sufficiently for church purposes, in some cases it would tend rather to make a man pause. In my last parish, for instance, Sir Gilbert Greenall supported us very much, and also the head manager of Walker's Brewery. The loss of what they give would be a very serious matter, but still it would not have altered my attitude in the least. I should consider the question is of such vital importanse to the well-being of the com- munity that one must put that first at all costs. In fact, temperance legislation has been too long delayed, and we have allowed the trade to get too strong a hold before attempting to deal with it." In the courso of farther remarks Mr. SCREETON said the State should be master of the drink monopoly. "Really "he said "this Bill is more of an introduction to temperance reform than in any way an attempt at a final settlement of the question,and the acquisition by the public of the public-house monopoly will render such action in the way of temperance reform much easier in the future when private interests are cleared out of the way."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Resolution.</head>
        <p>Resolution.</p>
        <p>The following resolution was moved by the Rev. R. Postance, chairman, seconded by the Rev. T. W. H. Cowper (one of the honorary secretaries) and carried unanimously at the monthly meeting of the C.E.T.S. Diocesan Executive held at B Commerce Court on Thursday, April 9th,—" That this executive meeting of the Liverpool Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society expresses its deep gratitude and admiration for the courageous stand taken by the Lord Bishop of Liverpool as President of the Diocesan Branch on behalf of the Licensing Bill in face of much opposition and misrepresentation, and promises to aid him in every practicable way."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>UNION CLERKSHIP.</head>
        <p>UNION CLERKSHIP.</p>
        <p>SALFORD CANDIDATE APPOINTED BY WARRINGTON BOARD.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley's Position.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians at which the most important business was the appoint- ment of Clerk and Assistant Clerk was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. W. Houghton presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Wilkinson,Mrs. Simpson, Miss Broadbent, Revs. T. It. Matthews and M. Ryan, Dr. Murray, Mears. A. J. Jolley, J. Billing°. W. Gill, R. Forshaw, J. took, It. Bolton, NV. C. Brooks, W. Furness, T Ritchie, A. Brooks, John Ball, P. Longton, G. Mather, J. Evans, W. Staniar, E. L. Bent, M. NV. Seton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), E:J. Sutton (acting assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate Cottage Homes).</p>
        <p>THE CLERK'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said he communicated to the Local Government Board his resignation of his office and had received from them a form whereon to report particulars of the appointment of his successor. He was pleased to say the Board did not send their usual form of acknowledgment but wrote: "I am directed by the Local Government Board to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 30th ult. and to express their regret on learning that you have resigned the office of clerk to the Guardians of the Warrington Union held by you for so many years." THE LATE DR. PATTERSON.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRNIAN expressed the great regret of the Board at the death of Dr. Patterson who, he said, had been a most active and useful member. He did exceptionally good work within his own division and his popularity was by no means confined to that Board, as was evidenced by the striking demonstration at the funeral. _</p>
        <p>Mr. ikla.TnEn seconded and said Dr. Patterson would be greatly missed in the district. Mr. BILLINGE supported and said Dr. Patterson was a very loyal and true-hearted colleague. Mr. JOLLEY added a word of regret and said Dr. Patterson to many of them was not merely a working colleague but a personal friend. The Rev. T. R. NIATDIEWS supported and said he had had an acquaintance of thirty years with Dr. Patterson. rho vote was carried in silence, the Board standing. The vdcancy in Crow Lane Ward, Newton was duly declared and the election fired for May sth. LENDING LIBRARY FOR INMATES.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee, the -Master submitted a proposal for the establishment of a lending library for the inmates, there being a fair quantity of books in the building to commence with. The proposal having warmly commended itself to the Guardians, it was resolved that the same be at once initiated. VOTES OF THANKS. As this was the concluding meeting of the present financial year, Mr. J. BILLINGE moved a vote of thanks to Mr. W. Houghton for his able services in the chair and said the Board appreciated to the fullest extent the impartial and dignified manner in which he had controlled the important business of the past year. Mr. JOLLEY seconded and Mr. FORSHAW and Miss BROADBENT supported the vote which was enthusi- astically accorded. The CHMII.IIAN, in responding, said it had been a sad sort of year in more ways than one and they had had some disagreeable business. Unfortunately as time went on it got still more sad, for they were shortly going to part with their Clerk whom they had known so long and who possessed such ability that it was recognized all over the kingdom, as was evidenced by the Local Government Board's letter._ _ . _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>similar coni-piTru—e-nTi;a; ";.C.COL:ded to the Vice-Chairman (Rev. T. R. Matthews) on the proposition of Mr. FURNESS, seconded by Mr. BENT. THE CLERKSHIP. UNSUCCESSFUL ATTEMPT TO REMOVE AGE LIMIT. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>A similar coni-piTru—e-nTi;a; ";.C.COL:ded to the Vice- Chairman (Rev. T. R. Matthews) on the proposition of Mr. FURNESS, seconded by Mr. BENT. THE CLERKSHIP. UNSUCCESSFUL ATTEMPT TO REMOVE AGE LIMIT. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>The next business was the appointment of a clerk in place of Mr. J. C. Sutton who has resigned after many years devoted service. At a meeting last week the Board, after fixing an age limit of 45, selected the following five candidates to appear before them : Arthur Bottomley (30, chief assistant clerk Salford Union, eight years. Previously chief assistant clerk 13ramley Union, four years. Thomas Henry Bentham (25), assistant clerk Preston Union, from February Ist, 1904. Previously second assistant clerk, Orrnskirk Union, seven years. Cornelius Cole (39), clerk to the Newton Urban Dis- trict Council since August 1900. Previously nine years deputy clerk to Hereford Guardians. Afterwards was for eight years chief assistant clerk to West Bromwich Guardians. Herbert William Coupe (32), clerk to Newhaven Uniou,Assessment Committee and Rural District Coun- cil, two and a quarter years, previously for 13 years assistant clerk to Leigh Union. Leonard Wray Greenhalgh (30), (barrister-at-law), deputy clerk Charlton Union, since September 1903, with 13 years experience, five of which as deputy clerk; ha 4 six assistants acting under him. He is responsible for all accounts, conducts part of correspondence, and when clerk absent undertakes all duties. William Henry Roberts (30), assistant clerk to Holy- well Union, and Rural Council. Son of clerk to ditto.</p>
        <p>For Chronio Chest Complaints, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Eight years as his assistant and four and a half years assistant clerk to the Union.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley withdrew from the meeting while the matter was under discussion.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHFCR at onoe raised the question of the age limit and said the Board would recollect that when the resolution was passed he gave notice that he intended to try and get it rescinded. He would ask the Clerk whether it was requisite to rescind that resolution or merely to pass another that there should be no age limit.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : It is quite open to the Board to pursue whatever course it pleases. You may either pass a resolution or not, but I would suggest that as it was a committee of the whole Board their fixing of the age limit stands and the Board should either be satisfied or dissatisfied with the minute as it reads.</p>
        <p>The CHairtmAN : The only one affected by the age limit was Mr. Jolley. (" No, no.") I believe there was a Mr. Brown who exceeded it by one year. There was a good bit of discussion over Mr. Jolley's—l don't like to say rejection because I told you I should notify him that his name would be retained in reserve for further consideration, and it is open to start from that point again. Mr. BENT was in favour of both the candidates who exceeded the age limic (Mr. Jolley and Mr. Brown) being allowed to compete and he moved a resolution to that effect. The CHAIRMAN : You had better stick to one. Mr. BENT : I don't think it is necessary. The CHAIRMAN: You might have a seconder for one where you would not for two. The CLERK suggested that the resolution be to the effect that no age limit be fixed, as that would seem to be fair to everybody. Mr. BENT : That is practically Mr. Mather's resolu- tion so I will leave it to him.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER moved that the age limit be deleted. He said the matter was discussed by the sub-committee and when the advertisement was arranged it was dis- tinctly understood that no age limit was to be placed iu it.</p>
        <p>-Mr. BENT seconded and said that was the position he took up. Mr. W.C. Brtooxs said that when the sub-committee met he suggested that an age limit should be inserted in the advertisement. Mr. Jolley, who actually sat there and took the minutes, said application forms would be printed and it would be for the applicant to state his age. There were seven present at the meeting including Mr. Gill and Mr. Billinge, and some were pledvd to Mr. Jolley then because they actually spoke to him (Mr. Brooks) when they left the room about Mr. Jolley's candidature and wanted to know whether he was prepared to support him. "I was totally surprised "said Mr. Brooks to see Mr. Jolley sitting there actually framing the advertinement,actually suggesting the salary and then to come here when he is struck out by the age limit—l think it is something awful." The CHAIRMAN: We must take a division for or against. Mr. FORSHAW was proceeding to reply to Mr. Brooks when</p>
        <p>_ The CHAIRMAN said he would not listen to him on that matter.</p>
        <p>Mr.JANtas EVANS said he did not see that they should rush an important matter like that. It was very easy to settle things but they wanted to settle that on satisfactory lines. He was not in the meeting when the resolution fixing the age limit was passed and when he came in and was told of the Board's decision he looked upon it as a very important step to have been taken. What occurred, to his mind, was this : The Board meant what it did. The action not only of that day but of the previous meeting would be submitted to the public who were to be the judges on the question. He had heard no specific reasons why the resolution of the last meeting should be rescinded or altered and the question re-opened. The CHAIRMAN : The meeting was only a committee meeting. Mr. EVANS : It was a committee meeting of the full Board—of the Board constituted as we are to-day. I submit that if the meeting to-day is going to alter its mind, we are entitled to some specific reason for doing it.</p>
        <p>Mr. BALL : That is very easily given. The CHAIRMAN: I shall proceed with the resolution. _— . .</p>
        <p>Mr. EVANS : I protest against that. Mr. FURNESS said he agreed with Mr. Evans that it was not a question to be rushed, and they ought to have an opportunity of ventilating their ideas. He protested against the resolution fixing the age limit as being very unfair to those who had sent in applications for the position. They had a sub-committee delegated to draw up the rules and regulations under which the applications should be made, and they never inserted an age limit. It was very unfair to make it a condition after they had received the applications. A strong reason why the resolution was accepted by certain members was that a statement was made that if they engaged anyone over 45 years of age it would mean a serious burden on the rates—that an older man would cost more and come sooner upon the superannuation allow- ance. That was an erroneous impression—it was not correct at all—and he could give them the exact posi- tion. If they had a man who was forty-five years of age he must serve eleven years before he could come on the superannuation allowance, but if they engaged one who had already served eleven, twelve, thirteen or fourteen years as a Poor Law official, the week after they appointed him if his condition ot health was such as to render him incapable of discharging the duties they must give him superannuation allowance in respect of the time he had served in some other Union. The fixing of an age limit could not possibly be in the interests of the ratepayers The Rev. T. R. MATTHEWS asked whether this was something extraordinary or whether it was customary thht an age limit should be stated. Mr. FURNESS said he considered it was entirely the fault of the sub-committee that an age limit was not inserted and not having inserted it they must take the risk and accept the conditions they laid down in the advertisement. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The CLERK : lam glad you said that because in the notes sent to me from that Committee, at which I was not present, no reference was made either to non- canvassing or the limitation of age. I would like to suggest to the Board that as the candidates are now waiting and both sides have been fairly heard, the Chairman should put the question. Mr. EVANS said he was not taking any exception to Mr. Jolley. No member of the Board had a higher opinion of Mr. Jolley's abilities. But the names of the selected candidates were set out on the sheet now before the Board. There had been an unusual procedure in the Chairman issuing a circular and he would point out that the wording of it began "I am entirely confident that our proceedings on Wednesday afternoon last were perfectly in order as a Committee of the whole Board." He asked again what were the exceptional circumstances which brought them that morning to discuss the question of doing away with the age limit when a committee of the whole Board had passed the resolu- tion. He did not believe in beating about the bush, it was a question of whether their friend Mr. Jolley should be excluded on that age limit or whether they should re-open the question and insert his name in the list of selected candidates. If the blame was to rest on the sub-committee, the only fair way was to deal with the question from the very beginning. He did not believe in" mending up "of the kind proposed and he could not take part in it. He could not feel that the Board could be satisfied with its own work, neither could the public appreciate proceedings of that kind. He was extremely sorry the position had arisen but in all fairness be could not agree to an alteration on the lines suggested by the Chairman's circular or the resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. BROOKS: The whole argument in favour of this age limit was based on quite an erroneous assumption. Probably some of those who voted for it did so thinking that they were going to save the rat - payers expense. I will take a concrete instance. It is no secret that there are a good many here who want one man, I will not give his name— Mrs. WILKINSON: I object to Mr. Brooks coming out with such statements.</p>
        <p>The CHMRNIAN : Mr. Brooks is perfectly in order. Mrs. WiLx[NsoN : I don't care. It is not a right thing, and he is not a gentleman, lie said the majority of this Board wanted a certain man in.</p>
        <p>The CIIIIRAIAN : Mr. Brooks is perfectly in order. I , Mrs. WILKINSON : I don't think it is right to men- tion names. We are not all of the same opinion. Mr. BROOKS : There is one man who already has seventeen years' service. I have taken the amount that will be payable at the end of several years. Mr. 'W. C. BrcooKs : I rise to a point of order. We are not discussing these applications now. Mr. A. BROOKS: Mr. W. C. Brooks who moved the resolution at the committee meeting made several qtatements and I take it that those who voted with kim voted on those statements. When you come to analyse them they are absolutely incorrect. I will take one gentleman who has served seventeen years. If at the end of five years he became incapacitated, you would have to pay him £l5O Gs. Bd. If you take a man who has no service you pay him nothing. At the end of tee years the man with seventeen years' previous service would on becoming incapacitated receive £lB4 10s. and the man with no previous service would get £6B Gs. Bd. and so on. That is sufficient to show that the vote was taken on statements which were quite erroneous. Mr. SETON said that if the Board altered their decision now they would be making themselves the laughing stock of the whole country. Mr. W. C. BROOKS formally Moved the confirmation of the Board's previous decision. Mrs. WILKINSON seconded. "Let us proceed with the business " she said "and never mind the age limit. It is not fair to have to have to sit all Wednesday afternoon and then to receive a circular like this from the chairman asking us if we cannot put a certain person in. I object to it. ' Mr. A. BROOKS : I propose the nomination of Mr. A. J. Jolley in addition to the six alieady invited. The CLERK : That question could not arise on this preliminary matter. Mr. W. C. BRooKs : There was no age limit in the advertisement for assistant clerk, and yet the very people who say it is wrong to insert an age limit were the people who actually voted for an age limit for the assistant clerkship. The CLERK : The question before you does not affect any one name, but all the names. It not only applies to the clerk to the Board but to the assistant clerk. It •</p>
        <p>lis for this Board to adopt the resolution or by way of Mr. Mather's amendment to reject it. Mr. EVANS : It does not merely affect the names on this paper. It affects the whole of the applications. The CLERK : Precisely. Mr. A. BROOKs : Mr. Mather notified at the last meeting that he would nominate Mr. Jolley thi s morning. A vote was then taken on Mr. Mather's proposition that the age limit be removed, and it was defeated by 11 votes to 10. Mr. A. BROOKS : Has not the Chairman a casting vote? It was pointed out that the Chairman had already voted and that there was a majority of one. Some discussion then arose as to whether Mr. Jolley should now be admitted to the room. Mr. Mamsst said he did not think Mr. Jolley should be asked to return as he intended to move a further resolution with reference to his candidature when it was required. The CLERK said that if members thought that by any process they could admit Mr. Jolley's candidature, it should be done now. Mr. W. C. BttooKs : Why should we re-open the question? It has been fairly and openly dealt with. The CLERK said he felt bound to advise the Chair- man that the resolution passed by the committee and confirmed by the Board must now stand. The CHAIRMAN : That is my ruling. Each of the six selected candidates then appeared before the Board and was questioned as to his capabilities and experience. A preliminary vote resulted in the names of Messrs. Bottomley, Cole, Greenhalgh, and Roberts being retained. Another show of hands having been taken,the final selection lay between Mr. Bottomley and Mr. Cole. Mr. MATHER then moved that Mr. Jolley should be pitted against the candidate who received the most votes. Mr. W. C. BROOKS : It is ridiculous. Miss BROADBENT seconded Mr. Mather's proposition. She remarked that many of the Guardians did not appear to realize that Mr.Jolley's fifteen years' services qualified him to understand the work but not for any superannuation. Therefore it would be entirely to the advantage of the ratepayers that he should be allowed to compete. He deserved courtesy and consideration if he did not get support on account of his fifteen years' faithful and devoted services to the town, his ward and the Union. Mr. BOLTON : IS this in order ? The CLERK : It is contrary to the motion that you passed that the clerk to be appointed must not exceed the age of forty-five. That was passed by a majority of the Board. The CHAIRMAN : Since that incident I have been thinking it over. As we came to a division on that point, Mr. Jolley was entitled to be in his place in this room, and no doubt it would have been carried, because there was only a majority of one and I should have given him my support and no doubt he would have voted for himself. I contend he was entitled to remain in his place, having regard to the circumstances, and take part in the division. 0 Mr. EVANS : I must say exactly what I said at the commence:nent— Miss BROADBENT: Please don't. We have had this already. Mr. Evans : We have had what you said already. It is becoming painful for a man with an open mind to sit in this Board room. The CHAIRMAN : I object to that. Mr. EVANS : I say that without any prejudice, if these unfortunate circumstances had not happened I should have been one of the foremost to support Mr. Jolley for the position. There is not the slightest doubt about that. I say it without fear of contradic- tion. It is my conviction, having landed in the position that we are now in, that it is surely getting beyond what I look upon as common decency in the interests of Mr. Jolley. What in the name of fortune will Mr. Jolley say outside this Board room, the question having been laboured as it has been this morning ? If Mr. Jolley were absolutely dependent on the position—l say nothing about his merits; they are known to every- one of us—but surely we are not going to labour a ques- tion like this, in the interests of Mr. Jolley. It is getting painful. The CLERK : lam obliged to advise you that the resolution passed by the majority of the Board prevails and that the election is limited to the two candidates who remained at the highest figures, but I would put this suggestion to the Chairman : As a mere matter of information that might be useful to the Local Government Board afterwards, having regard to the fact that the division was pretty near, I wonld put this question : "Are you of opinion that the name of Mr. Jolley should now be included ? " without saying that Mr. Jolley is thereby elected, but in order that you may have the opinion of the Board on that one point. It would be a matter of satisfaction hereafter I for your information, and for instruction to the Local Government Board if it should be needed. Those who are of opinion that Mr. Jolley should be included, please stand up. Mr. FORSIIAw : It is becoming painful for a man with a business mind to sit on this Board. Here is an advertisement put in the papers. Are we going to adhere to it or are we not ? The CHAIRMAN : I think we have acted wrongly by Mr. Jolley in not calling him into the room when we took the division on the age limit. The CLERK ! You had no power to do that. Mr. Jolley being one of the candidates had retired from the Board and the Guardians now present have been voting on these appointments. I recommend you to merely test the feeling. Miss BROADBENT : I move that Mr. Jolley be now asked to attend. Mr. W. C. BROOKS : Mr. Mather's proposition was that he should nominate Mr. Jolley against the last man. Mr. BOLTON: Are you going to allow this to be done? Would it not be a good idea to appeal to the Local Government Board and ask their opinion? It is a scandalous piece of work. The Rev. M. RYAN : I take it the one who receives the highest vote will be the clerk. The CLERK : T say that is the legal position. Mr. EVANS : Then what is the use of torturing Mr. Jolley? The Clerk : I must remind you of your position. You have been dividing on this till you have come to two names. Now you are wanting to introduce a third name that was negatived by the vote of the Board on the other question. Mrs. WILKINsoar : it is disgraceful. The CLERK : You cannot blow hot and cold. Mr. MATHER : We have done this morning, many a time. (Laughter). Mims BROADBENT again moved that Mr. Jolley be asked to attend. The CHAIRMAN said ha did not think they could do that.</p>
        <p>A vote was then taken to test the opinion of the Board as to whether Mr. Jolley's name should be included but the proposition was defeated, the fol- lowing nine voting for it :—Miss Broadbent, Dr. Murray, Messrs. Mather, Ball, A. Brooks, Billinge, Furness, Bent and the Chairman. Some discussion then took place as to the merits of the two candidates left in the final selec don. Mr. MATHER said they had had so many good candi- dates that he was sorry they could not elect the lot. (Laughter.) They were good looking men, young men, and men of ability. Miss BROADBENT : We are sorry that so many nice men have to be disappointed. (Laughter). On a vote bsing taken, Mr. Bottomley received 12 votes and Mr. Cola 10. Mr. Bottornley was then unanimously elected to the clerkship. Afterwards Mr. Bottomlev was called into the room and congratulated by the Chairman upon his appointment. Mr. MATHER : The one great thing with this Board is economy. Mr. BoTroatxxv said he thanked them most heartily for the honour they had conferred upon him. He would certainly do his utmost to give them every satisfaction. He knew that he was following a gentleman in Mr. Sutton who was respected in Poor Law circles as highly as any officer in the sery ice. He (Mr. Bottomlev) only hoped he would be able to create the same good name amongst them and amongst the Poor Law officers in general as Mr. Sutton had done. (Hear, hear). THE CHAIRMAN: You have something to do. (Laughter.) Mr. BorromLEy : I know I have a hard task before me. lam following a veteran and a Clerk respected in the whole service. I have never seen Mr. Sutton before but I have heard his name many times. THE ASSISTANT CLERKSHIP. The Board then had before them the following selected candidates for the assistant clerkship : George Harker (23), clerk in office of Driffield Union rand Rural District Council, since May, 1905 ; was clerk from November 1902 to May 1905 in Union work, North Allerton. Robert William Peacock (35), in the service of the Clerk of this Union, nearly four years, was for six years senior clerk in the Melton Union offices. Has bad 16 years experience of Poor Law work in Unions. Henry Parton (25), private clerk to Clerk in Burnl ey Union nine years. On the proposition of Mr. MATHER seconded by Mr. EVANS, Mr. Peacock was unanimously appointed to the position.</p>
        <p>PARISH Couricu..—The anaual meeting of the Stockton Heath Parish Council was held at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening when there were present : Messrs. J. W. Bouchier, F. Pearson, G. J. Brown J. H. Clarke, W. Mclntyre, H. J. Westbrook, J. Mcdermid, J. Whitley, T. R. Westbrook and E. Bradshaw (clerk).— Mr. H. J. Westbrook was elected Chairman of the Council in place of Mr. J. W. Bouchier who retired after holding the office for three years.—Nlessrs. J. Whitley, F. Pearson end Frank Warburton were re-elected overseers and Mr. J. Whitley was elected representative on the School Management Committee in place of Mr. Bouchier who resigned.</p>
        <p>Cough 1 cough ! with eyelids heavy and worn, Cough I cough ! cough ! from dewy eve till morn, A hollow cough and an aching head, Got a belief that they'll soon be dead ; • And so they will if they don't look out, Influenza is knocking about; Rush to the chemist and there procure Relief with Woods' Great Peppermint Cure,—Advt.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs sind Colds never fails 1/14,2/9.—Advt,</p>
