      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>rUE3LIC v. E3REWER.</head>
        <p>rUE3LIC v. E3REWER.</p>
        <p>PROGRESS OF THE FIGHT FOR THE LICENSING BILL.</p>
        <p>Trade Attack Weakening.</p>
        <p>habits of the country were effectual causes of slackness of trade. At the last election they fought about tariffs, and he did not know whether the chairman and he quite agreed on that subject. (Laughter). But for his (Mr. Parker's) part, if we had a Protected country hemmed all round with tariffs and were also a sober nation he believed we would be more prosperous than under a Free Trade system with a drunken nation. (Hear, hear). With regard to the tactics resorted to by the trade, a man was telling him the other day in regard to certain petitions in public- houses that be had signed fourteen times and he was quite proud of it. (Laughter). He had used a different name every time and he said he had exhausted all the names he could remember and he got so mixed in regard to thorn that he scarcely knew what his own name was. (Laughter). There were other tricks which tbe audience knew about. Tnere was the great ticket trick. (Laughter). He rather admired the cuteness of I a dodge of that kind if it was done well, but it was done so clumsily. (Laughter). Fancy trying to imitate tickets and printing them on a different shade of colour ! (Laughter.) If it had gone off, although they objected to it, they might have admirod the idea, but it was done so clumsily that, whoever did it must have had his brain muddled with the thing that he was drinking. (Laughter.) But it was a low down dodge nevertheless—an unworthy dodge—and he thought it showed the weakness of their side when they had to stoop to such contemptible practices. He wondered where it ori.;inated. (A Voice : "Not far away.") He hoped for the credit of the town that the responsible leaders of the Conservative party and the trade had no part in it, but it was rather a curious thing that at one of the local breweries they closed half an hour before the usual time and these dark green ' tickets were distributed at that brewery. It seemed to him that the credit of the officials at the brewery was ' somewhat involved and for their own credit they ought to clear themselves of any complicity in that low down and silly trick. (Hear, hear.) Alluding to the danger of the dominance of the trade in the municipal and social life of the country, Mr. Parker quoted Lord Rosebery's warning that when such dominance held sway they were getting perilously near to political corruption. He (Mr. Parker) was not quite sure that they were not approaching that condition in regard to the public life of Warrington. What other trade was I represented noon the Town Council as the drink trade was? He did not want to call in question the 1 personal character or the ability of the representatives of the trade on the Town Council. He was quite willing to admit that they might be in matters of general concern to the town perfectly honest and able men, but in as much as the government of the town was concerned in the control of the liquor traffic he said that the presence of such an increasing number of repre- sentatives of the trade was a danger to- the public interests. (Applause.) It was not only a question of those who were directly interested in the trade on the Town Council but of those who for one reason or another were dependent upon the trade for their posi- tion there in regard to the payment of their election expenses or anything of that kind. Not only had they the direct representatives of the trade but they had the , others who were under their control because they had parted with their independence and liberty by having , their expenses paid. (Applause.) Supposing they had a representative of any district and his expenses had been paid by the trade—it did not matter whether they called it the Conservative Association in this case or the Trade Defence Association or the brewers. He did not see much difference. The Conservative Association seemed to be a sort of tied house of the brewers. (Laughter.) Supposing a man came into the Council dependent for his position upon the trade and a question like the election of the Watch Committee came up. One connected with the trade was proposed as a member of the committee, orie of the main duties of which was to watch the trade of which that man was a representa- tive. If one of those men who was dependent upon the trade considered that was a bad thing and dared to vote against the representative of the trade, did they think he would go back to the Council again? Not he. He would be dropped out at the next election and another dummy put in his place. It was a danger to the public life of England that the trade was becoming so dominant and so powerful. (Applause.) One of the presidents of a Trade lossociation said : "I do not think the Government will go against the trade, for we put them in power and if they treat us properly we will keen them there, hut if they don't we will chuck them out." He would like to ask those boasters of the trade who appointed them as the national chuckers-out ? (Laughter.) What right had any trade to have the power to say such a thing ? The people of England had the right to be masters of theimown destinies, and it was a perilous danger when any trade attained such a domination over votes that it had the impudence and the power to say th4t it would act as the keeper-in of the Government or the chucker-out. (Applause.) It was a very serious question, and they wanted all right- minded men to stand on that platform and support the Bill. There was room for all. (Applause.) The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK seconded. He said they were engaged in a close struggle with a great foe. The time had arrived in the history of this nation when the property which N fr. Balfour gave away in 1901 must revert to the public. It had been delightful to witness the intense earnestness and seriousness with which the subject had been approached and spoken of in the town. One had felt glad and proud to be a Warringtonian. (Applause.) It was especially pleasing to him that Mr. F. W. Monks was in the chair. They wanted to see him back again in the public life of the town. —(hear, hear)—takieg the place which he once filled and which he was so capable of filling with such good citzenship and ability and usefulness to the borough. (Applause.) With regard to the issue before them,they wanted to pass such legislation that never again would the brewery interest be able to get behind the power of the people and put on the Statue book anything which approached the dominance of one trade upon the people of this land. (Applause.) The brewers had over- looked the fact that Englishmen never gave away their rights with their eyes open. They gave them away in 1904 with their eyes closed, and they had got to win them back. (Applause.) Referring to the letter by Canon Knox Little which the trade was circulating so widely, Mr. Cook said that Canon Knox Little was a man for whose intellectual ability he had very great admiration. The parsons, in this matter, however,seemed I to be taking it in the academic way and quite outside the practical world atmosphere. He confessed that many of the things that had been said seemed to be reasonable, but these gentlemen did not seem to take into account the evils and the wrongs and the tragedies which the trade produced. They said they were anxious for temperance reform and that they did not want the drunkard. He believed them. But they seemed to forget that the multiplication of facilities for drinking meant the multiplication of human tragedies. They who believed in human rights believed that the strong might to protect the weak, and anything which went to damage the weakest amongst them was the thing which first of all they must put right. 'Applause.) Mr. Parker had referred to the beautiful little dodge of last Friday which did not come off. (Laughter.) He (rr. Cook) did not wish to impute to the Conservative party leaders—to such men as Mr. Rylands and Mr. John White and others—and he dissociated himself from anyone who did wish to impute that they had anything to do with that dirty deal. He thought they would scorn it, as any man would. But those who did it did not understand—the difference between a comma and a full stop. (Laughter.) One thing that had pained him was that over and over again supporters of the Bill were told that they were breaking the Eighth Commannment. Did he steal when he took back what belonged to him but what somebody else had taken away ?When their friend like Canon KOOK Little told them they were breaking the Eighth Com- mandment they asked Canon Knox Little if he remem- bered there was a Ninth Commandment "Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour." (Applause.) Would he dare to say that the men who had drafted the Bill were robbers ? They bad to deal straightly and plainly in this fight, and to bit hard as they expected to be hit, but they wanted the tight to be clean. They wanted no poisoned stilletos and they would use none. " This trade" concluded Mr. Cook " which is howling and shrieking and making day and night hideous is fighting for the right to go on selling unlimited and ' unrestricted the thing that we English people have made a monopoly. As they have a monopoly, we have to restrict them, and we intend to restrict them more and t more." (Applause.) Mr. J. ELLW ELL, in supporting, said that if malt and hops were used in the manufacture of beer instead of so ! many chemicals, a great many more men would be employed in the trade. How were the brewers follow- ing the Eighth Commandment in Warrington? He was informed that many houses in Warrington were let at is low rental so that they could he assessed at a low rate, and the result was that the brewers were robbing the ratepayers. He as a ratepayer objected to having to , bear the brewers' burden. (Applause.) Mr. JAMES DODDS, who arrived towards the close of the meeting, was invited to support the resolution. He I pointed out that Canon Knox Little had made a charge ' against a member of the Government with reference to rhe sale of the "Coach and Horses" at Portsmonth. On Thursday night he (Mr. Dodds) was at Liverpool and heard Mr. Cameron Corbett, who had been for twenty three years member for Glasgow, deal with that matter, and Canon Knox Little's letter should be at once con- tradicted. Mr. Corbett stated that immediately he saw the statement about that particular house he telegraped to the purchasers to say he would give twice the price Ithat they had paid for the licence—he offered £4,400. He received no reply so he telegraphed again to ask</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>MEETING AT SANKEY BRIDGES. THE TRADE'S INFLUENCE IN</head>
        <p>MEETING AT SANKEY BRIDGES. THE TRADE'S INFLUENCE IN</p>
        <p>PUBLIC LIFE. --- The Forged Ticket Trick.</p>
        <p>Another successful meeting in support of the Licensing Bill was held in the Wesleyan Schools, Sankey Bridges, on Manday evening. There was a good attendance, Mr. F. W. Monks presiding. The principal speakers were Mrs. Mahood, of Liscard, Mr. C. E. Parker, arid the Rev. C. Harvey-Cook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>CHAIRMAN AND TRADE TACTICS.</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN AND TRADE TACTICS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he understood the meeting was promoted by the workingmen employed by the firm with which he Nrii.3 connected. He had a strong feeling that anyone who was connected with the noncern in such a responsible position as he was, ought Co do some- thing more than merely try to find orders to keep the place going and pay the wages on Saturday. (Hear, hear.) He therefore felt it was his duty when asked in such a manner as he was to come forward and help them in the promotion of that meeting, and that feeling applied not only to such purposes as that meeting had been called for but generally. If the men wanted his help in any shape or form outside business hours it was his duty to look upon that as the first call and whenever he possibly could to come forward and help them. (Applause.) He was glad to be present at that meeting because there were such vital issues at stake. Ile confessed that at first be was not very much in love with the Bill. It did not strike him as being particularly favourable in some respects, or as going as far as it ought to go. But seeing the attitude which the other side was taking on the ques- tion, and the methods which they seemed to be adopt- ing to defeat the objects of the Bill, it had rather roused his temper, and he was getting his back against the wall because it seemed to him that if they were going to he dominated and ruled by the interests of the drink traffic, if there was any probability or likelihood of it, the sooner they realized it and set themselves to put an end teeny such feeling, the better it would be for everybody living in the country. (Applause.) It WAS the attitude of the trade which had made him feel that whatever one might think as to the proposals of the Bill and the details involved, they must do something to try and get such a Bill passed through the House of Commons and through the House of Lords. (Applause.) It was for that reason that he was glad to be present to take part in passing a resolu- tion which might possibly have the effect of strengthen- ing the hands of the Government in their efforts to pass the measure and so improve the possibility of everybody living a better, healthier, and sober life. (Applause.) He was very much struck with a letter he saw the other day written by Mr Arthur Chamberlain, of Birmingham. (Applause.) If there was one man in the country who had had experience of that question more than another, it was Mr. Arthur Chamberlain as chairman of the Licensing Committee of Birmingham. In that letter he said that at the time he was chairman of the Licensing Committee, the , Dirminahara magistrates proved beyond doubt that a , 'carefully planned but large reduction of licences in any area did reduce drunkenness and improve the order and morality of the area affected. That was striking evidence to disprove what the brewers and publieana and the Conservatives generally bad been saying—that the bill was not likely to reduce drunkenness. The Bill was put forward by the Government with that as its main object and it seemed to him that if Mr. Arthur Chamberlain took such a view they might safely follow his advice and do what they could to help to make the Bill into an Act of Parliament. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>WHAT THE BILL WILL DO.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE BILL WILL DO.</p>
        <p>Mrs. MAHoon, of Liscard,then addressed the meeting. Dealing with the present conditions she pointed out that in his annual report for 1006,Dr. Hope, the Medical Officer of Health for Liverpool, dealing with the abuses of alcohol, gave particulars of the flornby-street demolition area from which it appeared that in three public houses which were to be acquired by the City Council, there had been an average annual turnover of £5,000 and within a radius of 200 yards there were 48 other houses. That small district was remarkable chiefly for its intense squalor and poverty. That was an instance of the condition of things that the Licensing Bill was going to deal with. It could not be too firmly realized that the Bill was not a teetotal measure. It was essentially the moderate man's Bill. But it went such a long way towards giving what they had advocated for so many years that the teetotal party were joining hands with the moderate party and seeking to have it passed into law. (Applause.) It was a very good thing that the moderate man was awaking to the fact that he as a person who demanded that a dangerous trade should be run in his interest, must do his best to hedge that trade about and make it as safe as possible. When she first read the pill, the reduction proposals seemed to be very sweeping, but they had to remember that the reduction was to be spread over fourteen years. They wereasked why they could not allow the 1904 Act to go on. It had been acting very well, but that amount of reduction could not go on indefinitely. The reductions under the 1001 Act were strictly regulated by the amount of com- pensation money available and so far the magistrates had been dealing with the cheaper and poorer houses which were more easily suppressed. They had acted wisely in doing that but when the poor and cheap houses had gone, there would be a great number of valuable houses left, and it would be difficult to deal with them under the limited operations of the 1901 Act. The new Bill was entirely fair to the publican and if he understood it and dare do it, he would be on the side of the measure because he was most shabbily treated by the 1904 Act. The crucial point of the Pill was the time limit. The trade were saying that they -would have no time limit short of the Day of Judgment. (Laughter.) When they considered what it was that the trade had and what the time limit was for, it was impossible that there should not be a time limit to their monopoly rights, because it was the logical out- come of the 190-1 Act. As to the cry of robbery and confiscation there bad been robbery, but some of them who knew more about it than the man in the street were inclined to think the robbery took place when the 1904 Act was passed, and the public was robbed of its monopoly rights. (Applause.) She hoped the time limit would not be lengthened. Were they going to treat the publican better than the inventor who only held his patent for fourteen years, at the expiration of which time any one could copy his invention? The trac'e said they could not recoup themselves for their outlay in fourteen years but Sir Thomas Whittaker, whose name carried weight wherever tigures were mentioned, said that if they would only put aside 5 per cent, of their annual turnover to provide for sinking fund and reserve fund, all the contingencies of their trade would be met. (Applause.) She did not know how many widows and orphans bad money invested in brewery shares. If there were many, they were peculiarly unfortunate in their advisers because everyone had known for years that there was not a more insecure investment on the market than brewery shares. (Applause.) No injustice was being done—in fact the Hill erred if anything upon the side of moderation and of tenderness to the trade. The struggle had now passed far beyond the bounds of the Bill itself. They were not only fighting for the Licensing Bill but for their rights vs citizens. The brewers wanted to put themselves beyond the law. They said that whoever was legislated for and against they were not to be legislated against. They claimed to run and to influence elections and they were stooping to methods which were doing them no good. (Applause.) Evidently the doles they had given to churches and chapels and various organizations in the past had simply been as a kind of sop and a silencer to ens thing ' that might be said. She hoped the Church of *Christ would be able to go on without the wealth of the brewers. (Applause.) If it could not it was time to stop the pretence. In conclusion Mrs. Mahood asked her hearers if they were going to hand over the political power they now enjoyed and for which their fathers fought and died in some cases to a gang of vulgar and unscrupulous traders—she did not think she was unjust in calling them that when she remembered what happened at Peckham. She was afraid they were in for something more of the same kind but she hoped that in the days that elapsed between Peckharu election and the coming contests the men of England would have learnt something and would not vote at the bidding of the brewers. (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE TRADE'S INFLUENCE IN PUBLIC LTFE. THE FORGED TICKET TRICK.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER moved :—" That this public meeting of the inhabitants of Sankey Bridges and dis- trict expresses its hearty approval of the Licensing Bill now before Parliament as a substantial measure of temperance reform, reducing excessive and unnecessary drinking facilities and securing to the people the ulti- mate control of the liquor traffic and calls upon all citizens to unite in this national effort to diminish the gigantic evils produced by the present licensing system." He said he was glad to be associated with Mr. Monks in that meeting. Like him, he (Mr. Parker) as a manu- facturer recognized that the excessive drinking</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>Why the Publican Himself Should Support the Licensing Bill.</head>
        <p>Why the Publican Himself Should Support the Licensing Bill.</p>
        <p>Sir :—ln all great agitations there are usually some individnals who are made the scape goats. Temperance' people forgetting that behind the publican there is the brewer and the distiller, often look upon him and his occupation as the objects of the attack. But it ought in justice to the publican to be remembered thael in very many cases he is conducting his business honourably and well. The present Bill takes much more account of him and his position than ever before and certainly much more than under the Act of 1901. Under that Act he only received such an amount as the justices might see fit to award him on quasi-eleemo- synary grounds at the expense of the share of the com- pensation due to other interests. In practice this has meant that while the brewer or distiller draw .£9O of compensation the publican drew only £lO. Under Mr. Asquith's Bill the publican will receive "separate and special compensation for loss of business." His position therefore will be very much better than before and if only for the lowest of motives he ought to support the measure. In this respect his trade is more his politics than his politics his employers. Yours &amp;.c., J. M. HOGGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>York, April 13th, 190. _ _ The Forged Ticket Trick.</head>
        <p>York, April 13th, 190. _ _ The Forged Ticket Trick.</p>
        <p>[ro THE EDITOIL.]</p>
        <p>Sir,—The action of the participants in the" Forged Ticket Trick" of last week, met with its well-deserved rebuff. That men should condescend to such a vulgar game does not speak well for the morals of the party to which they belong. The sad feature of the whole nasty business was the number of innocent men and women who were thus victimised. The prompt, courteous and energetic action of the Chief Constable and his men merits the thanks of the citizens of • the town. If this kind of low game is to be played no class of public meeting will be safe. Certainly no class of political meeting will be safe from infuriated ignorance. Freedom of speech which Englishmen have given so much to secure, and to which they hold so tenaciously must not be lightly parted with. If all sorts of vested interests and ugly monopolies, which menace the purity and dignity of national life are going to pack meetings with their own servants,not to elicit information,but to prevent free, fair, and open discussion, thou freedom of speech will be a thing of the past. It is to the interest of all citizens alike, without distinction of party or creed to maintain inviolate and inviolable the liberty and integrity of the platform. The seltish,unclea n, and clumsy methods of the trade have exposed its hatred of the national welfare and its utter immunity from sound reason and persuasion. As it stands self-exposed so it stands self-oondemued.—Yours Scc.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-04-18_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEWS.</head>
        <p>JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEWS.</p>
        <p>Bishop Thanks Mr. Hartley. Saddened By Recent Letters. A " Truly Christian Act."</p>
        <p>At the end of last week Mr. W. P. Hartley, of Aintree, who is a prominent Primitive Methodist, sent E:300 to the Bishop of Liverpool to be used by his lordship for such diocesan funds as might suffer by the threatened withdrawal of subscriptions in consequence of the Bishop's action in supporting the Licensing Bill. In reply to Mr. Hartley's letter, the Bishop of Liverpool writes:—</p>
        <p>I.__ . "My Dear Mr. Hartley,—Sir Edward Russell handed to me this evening your most generous letter and munificent cheque. "I cannot sufficiently thank you for such a gift at such a time, and even more for the way in which it has , been given. "I have had much to sadden me lately. Letters of surprise, reproach, anger, and expostulation are pouring in daily, many of them from those for whom I have a very high regard. " Such kind words as yours are especially encourag- ing and helpful after reading the letters of those old friends who are temporarily alienated by the line I have taken, and with all my heart I thank you. 1 “ Twill place the cheque to the account of the Bishop 1 of Liverpool's Fund, which helps to meet the spiritual I destitution of the poor parts of this city and diocese. An official receipt will be sent you to-morrow. " May God bless and reward you for such a truly 'Christian act.—Ever yours gratefully, 1 "F. J. LivErtroor.,." ' Mr. W. P. Rylands and Monopoly 1 I Value. [To THE: EDITOR.] Sir --Nlr.Rylands' reply to my last(published in one of I your contemporaries) appears to indicate an agreement, at least between Mr. Rylands and myself, on the question which at present looms so large in the public ' thought. Mr. Rylands admits, although with some ' apparent hesitation, that a licence has a monopoly ' value apart from the structure of the house and the capital involved, and that this " monopoly value is , ' created purely from the licence granted by the State." ' Most people see this very clearly, but the advocates of the trade both by direct statement and inference deny it, and the monotonous cry of "robbery and spoliation" ,is based upon this denial. The only point of differ- ence between Mr„ Rylands and myself (and I am not sure if we really do differ) is on the question of the , actual capital involved in the retail business and the ' value of the goodwill apart from the State-created } monopoly. I It is well to bear in mind that ninety per cent, of all ' on-licences are the property of the large brewing com- panies. Of the 205 houses in Warrington, I I understand, only three are free honses, practically , all the rest are the property of' two brewing ; firms who either rent the houses or appoint managers. These houses called by custom and courtesy 1 public-houses licensed for public convenience, have under the tied-house system become private houses for pushing the sale of the production of one firm whether the public prefers this make of beer or no. 1 1 Mr. Rylands knows that goodwill is one of the most , intangible assets in modern industrial life. It generally springs from two sources, first from the excellence and reliability of the article sold, and secondly from the I personal association of a tradesman with his customers. I In regard to the first the closing of a certain proportion , of public houses does not necessarily involve a reduced consumption, although that is its tendency. Walker's beer tastes as good in one house as another. If one public-house is closed it is reasonable to expect that 1 say ninety per cent, of the customers will patronize 1 associated with the article sold is secured I , throughadjoininggoodwill houses where the same beer is sold. The another house. By this the brewers gain because 1 1 it reduces expense of rent, management, &amp;c., hence the reason why in several cases brewing companies have , suggested to the magistrates the desirability of 11 scheduling certain houses which have only been kept 1 , open that as much as possible could be got out of them 1 'by means of the Compensation Fund. We have 1 I then left the second element of goodwill, the 1 I , association of the tenant or managers with his ' customers. This is recognized in the Bill and provided for, also the loss through the depreciation in fixtures. But it is the condition at the end of the I time limit which concerns Mr. Rylands. May I put a direct question, how do brewing companies treat this i value in the case of death, or change from any cause in</p>
      </div>
