        <head>'''.. '''':''''"'-':.7'.4..'''''':.:''':ii.-.;'...-,:'i,.;.•,,:-..:::::::i..:i."..',.,....';.,'.,."'.1.,...'"''''•,'...-.:?.i..-:.'..,''.',.!;::.'</head>
        <p>'''.. '''':''''"'-':.7'.4..'''''':.:''':ii.-.;'...-,:'i,.;.•,,:-..:::::::i..:i."..',.,....';.,'.,."'.1.,...'"''''•,'...-.:?.i..-:.'..,''.',.!;::.'</p>
        <p>about the price they would take. Again there was nol response and he found at last that they would not sell at the price they said they were going to lose— £lo,ooo. (Applause.) If the money spent in drink unnecessarily were put into other channels, such as the purchase of furniture,clothing, better food and more of it for the children, there would be such a time for labour that the employers would be complaining that they could not get suacient labour. If the publicans thoroughly understood the Bill he believed they would support it, because under clause 10 they were to have through the Inland Revenue officials compensation equal to three years profits. With regard to rates and assessments, under the new Act the brewers would receive compensation according to the asssessments, so that instead of wanting assessments to be low they would want them to be high. (Applause.) He appealed to the audience to support the measure which if it went through would be the finest passed by Parliament in his (Mr. Dodds's) time. (Applause). . .</p>
        <p>, The CHAIRMAN said that if the other side were really honest, and sincere in what they said about being anxious to promote temperance, instead of criticising the Bill as they had done and talking about spoliation and confiscation, they would make some practical counter proposals which would do something to bring about the cause they had so much at heart. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried unanimously. On the proposition of Mr. JOHN WALTEFtS, seconded by Mr. DAVID Elrr,r„ the Chairman and speakers were cordially thanked.</p>
        <p>Mrs. MATIOOD, in reply, criticised Some proposals put forward by Mr. Watson Rutherford, M.P.—the only constructive proposals she had seen from the other side —pointing out that the Licensing Bill was infinitely better, as it would deal with the evils of over-licensing, whereas Mr. Rutherford confined himself to dealing with the drunkard, the quality of the beer, and the suit- ability and conduct of the premises.</p>
      </div>
