        <p>they had at Grappenhall. On behalf of all the visitors BABy,s TERRIBLE he wished the concern every success. . Mr. ALSOPP (Stockport) seconded, and said he was particularly impressed with the orderly system • which predominated throughout the works. Everything was in apple pie order--(hear, hear)—and the good irupres- ITcHING sions which the visitors had formed would live long in 1: their memories. (Applause.) Mr. GARLICK (Salford). supported and paid a tribute to the energy and indomitable perseverance of the late Mr. Richmond. It was evident that the spirit of its founder permeated the whole concern from the directors downwards,and had produced the great success resulting in the -magnificent works which the firm possessed. (Applause.) The vote was enthusiastically accorded. Mr. J. W. SLACK responded and gave some reminis- cences of his own early experiences as a member of a Gas Students' Association. They in the Manchester district were to be congratulated upon forzning a junior association and carrying it on so successfully. The j papers which had been contributed by the members were highly creditable and the Association was worthy of every encouragement. Mr. Slack, expressed regret that one of the directors, Dr. Atkins, was tin-able to be with them owing to bereave.nent. Ile also conveyed the hearty good wishes of the Mayor to the Association.. (Applause.) Mr. W. H. SILERIICTIN (designer and head of the pattern department), else responded. The members then left for home, all being thoroughly delighted with their visit. RUGBY FOOTBALL. THE NORTHERN UNION CUR SEMI-FINALS. Hunslet Hull RED ROSE FAILS.. In each of the semi-finals a Lancashire club was engaged, but in each instance Red Rose hopes were clashed to the ground. Saturday's games leave Hunslet and' Hull in for the final, which is to-be played off at Huddersfield on April 25. BROUGHTON It ATEN. The leaders of the Yorkshire League showed their. usual prowess as cup-fighters on Saturday at Wigan, when they defeated Broughtoo Rangers by 16 points to 2. Such a margin was totally unexpected, but on the play the Rangers were thoroughly thrashed. After an interval score of 3-2 for the Ts kes, the Hunslet contin- gent ran their opponents off their feet. The Yorkshire- men were in tip-top form, and their combination,was a treat to twitch. Albert Goldthorpe especially sheeed hirusele a trilliant tactician, and in the second period kicked his 800th goal for Hunslet. The Rangers, on the other hand, gave one of their weakest displays, and the rout was entire. There. was little or no combination, a complete misunderstanding of one another's movements end a great deal of bad kicking and tackling. Barlow kicked a penalty goal for Broughton, the winners scor- ing through Smith, Farrar, Batten and Goldthorpe. Sixteen thousand people watched the match,the receipts amounting to £4BO. HULL THE OTHER FINALIST.. • At Halifax, Hull obtained a 7-0 victory over Leigh,. who lost one of their threequarters early on. Hull, however. just deserved their victory, for their men were the smarter lot, and made 'greater use of their chances than did Leigh. Play was not good, being of the hustl- ing type, and rather tog vigorous. JNo points- were obtained in the first period, brit after the interval Hull scored through Cottrell, Wallace and Rogers. The receipts were £267, representing an attendance of 8,000. j NORTHERN LEAGUE RESULTS: • G. T. P. Runcorn -r Oldham 2 3 13 Widnes 1 0 2 Swinton 4 4 20 Dewsbury 0 1 3 Wakefield Trinity 1 3. 11 Salford 0 1 3 Leeds 3 521 York 1 311 Batley 2 3 13 Huddersfield ... I 3 11 Barrow Bradford Hull K. It. ...... 6 6 30 Ebbw Vale 2 0 4- Wigan 2 3 13 Merthyr Tydvil 0 1 3 LANCASHIRE SHIELD COMPETITION. SEMI- FINAL. WA ItRINGTCN "A" v. RADCLIFFE RANGERS.—This match attracted a big gate to Wilderspool on Saturday. The home side included Beveled first team players, Fish, Taylor, and Watkin being included in the back division. The superiority of the Warringtonians was- early demonstrated, and after good forward work P. Board- man brushed aside the opposition and scored. Radcliffe were again in difficulties a few minutes later, for sharp passing initiated by Lloyd led up to Fish scoring at the corner. Maintaining the attack Vat-kin handed on to Lender, and Fish tide time improved ou the point. Radcliffe showed a little better form after this and the Outcome of some smart footwork caused Jordan trouble.. The heals worn penalized and Cross placed a goal: Warrington immediately attacked and Fish, securing at centre, raced through the whole Of the Ratcliffe side. and added his second try, closely followed with a third a few minutes later. Only one of these was converted and near the interval another hot raid led up to %Catkin scoring, Warrington leading at half-time by two goa.is six tries (22 poirits) to Radcliffe Rangers, One goal (2 points). The visitors had to again act on the defensive for the greater portion of the second halLand .fino cornhination bet eeeu the backs led up to Taylor smiting, Fish landing the goal. Watkiu next crossed and Fish notched hie fourth try. The Rangers made a smart effort near the close, and Holland scored on the call- of time, Cross placing a goal. Final. Warrington four goals nine tries (35 points); Radcliffe Rangers, two goals one try (seven points). ASSOGIAION FOOBALL • • WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST Devestors. Goals. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Fts. Buttonwood A.F.O l7 16 1 0 62 14 32 Ought rington Park l7 15 1 1 77 16 31 Lyrum Fairfield Palatine Rylands lees. Latchford Musks Hall Warrington St. Paula— 17 313 1 21 61 7 *Buttonwood Social l2 I 7 4 14 43 4 *Two points deducted. SECOND DIVISION. Orford A.F.O Warrington St. Elphin'e 14 53 6 22 17 16 Warrington St. John's : 13 6 4 3 27 21 15 Walkers Onghtrington Park lies lt 4 5 2 32 21 10 Warrington St. Mirry'ase l3. 8 8 0 14 38 10 Arpley A.F.O ........ Fairfield Reserves 8 0 4 4 9 13 4 April 18th. Latchford v. Lymm. Oughtrington Park v. Monks Hal